      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A Mex never knows what he can do tuitil he tries, and then he is often sorry that he found out.</p>
        <p>MANY men strive for fame -who wouldn't have the slightest idea what to do with it if they had it. Chumpley : "I say, Chollie, doncherknow, I just received an anonymous letter, doncherknow." Lapley : "Good gwapious ! Who from?" "How can you be certain the lady will read the letter, when you have directed it to her husband?" "I have put the word Private in the corner." YORICK : "What are you crying like that for, my little lad?" The Little Lad: "Because— that's—the only—way—l know how—to cry." Ititelor experiments have shown that the thistle may be used as an article of food. No doubt there are some fine points about the thistle. Titosir who would enjoy good eating should keep good-natured ' • an nngry man can't tell whether he is eating boiled cabbage or stewed umbrella. STRANGER (TO GRA7EDIG4ER) : "Who's dead?/ Gravedigger: "Old Squire Grumbler." "What complaint?" "No complaint ; everybody is satis- fied.</p>
        <p>Sustz, at her music lesson—i• I'd like to catch an old air I heard in the music-room last night." Professor—" What air was that ? " Susie, de- murely—" Oh, it was a millionaire." Mrs. Lotos : "Poor Mrs. Lovey is ill, and I must go and see her. Mr. Lotus: "An errand of mercy, oh?" Mrs. Lotus: "Partly; and then, I want to see how she looks with her hair down."</p>
        <p>LABOUR AGITATOR---" My friend, are you not in favour of the eight-flour movement?" Abe Lazy (the tramp)—" Movement ! who ever heard of me baba' in favour of any kind of movement?" Lc VIZ DARE ROOM.—Edwin (amateur photo- grapher) : "That's it! *Another plate spoiled." .Angelina: "What spoiled it? Edwin: "The light of your eyes." P.S.—Engaged. WHEN- Freddy was asked why his engagement with Miss H. was broken off, he replied, "Oh, she turned out a dee. iver." (But he did not mention that he was the deceiver she turned out).</p>
        <p>AN American traveller lately, describing a tropical shower, wrote to a friend in the following words: "The raindrops were extremely large, varying in size from a shilling to eighteenpenoe. Man. IlLossox (to her husband, who has come home with a black eye) : " That's wnat ye get for riding a bicycle." Mr. llloisom (mournfully): "No, my dear; it's what I get for not being able to ride one."</p>
        <p>"AND you rejected him?" "I did." "He has the reputation of beiag a largo-hearted man." " That's the trouble with him ; he is too large- hearted. He eau love half a dozen women at the same time."</p>
        <p>"Do you see any digerence in neighbour Pearce since he joined the church ?" " Yes; formerly when he went to work in his garden on Sunday he carried his tools on his shoulder, now he carries them under his overcoat."</p>
        <p>AN Irishman' beholding Niagara Falls, said— " W hat is there here to make such a bother about ? " " Why," said a companion, "see that mighty river pouring over into the deep abyss." "And sure, what's to hinder it ? " said Pat.</p>
        <p>STAGE MANAGER--" Mr. Heavy, you will take the part of Alonzo." Mr. Heavy—" I have never seen this play. Do you think I can please the audience in that part?" Stage Manager—"lm- mensely. You die in the first act." PCBLISHER—" Li this novel of yours quite com- plete ?" Anthoresa —"All but the name. In fact, I don't know what to do for a title." Publisher (with an admiring glance)—." Do what other pretty women have done. Maroy one." "I HAD to be away from school yesterday," said Tommy. "You must bring an excuse," said the teacher. " Who from?" "Your father" He ain't no good at makin' excuses. Ma catches him every time." JUDGE—" Prisoner, I sentence you to imprison- ment for life." Prisoner—" Very well, m lord. At least it will enable me to live up to my motto." Judge—" Your motto! What may that be, pray!" "Always in it." As a pleasaat-faced woman passed the corner Jones touched his hat to her, and remarked feel- ingly, to his companion—" Ah, my boy, I owe a great deal to that woman!" "Your mother?" was the query. "No, my landlady." Polite Visitor: "Why, the baby looks just like its father 1" Young Father (aside to servant) : "Mary, is there any laudanum in the house? Ido not wish to live any longer." BREAKING Fr GENTLY.—Foreman (quarry gang): "It's tlad nevvs Oi hov' fur yez, Mrs. M`Gahar- raghty. Y'r husband's new watch is broken. It was a foine watch, and it's smashed all to paces' Mrs. MG. : "Deane me! How did that hap- pen ?" Foreman : "A ten-ton rock fell on AT a trial a barrister, whose turn it would be- come shortly to speak, opened his bag to bring out his brief, and drew from it instead a roast fowl. There was a peal of laughter in the court. "It ' is that fool of a clerk of mine," said he, "who does not know the difference."</p>
        <p>A WORD OF CAUTION.—SpiatB (to Miss Munn) : "Mabel, love, I dote upon you Wildly." Miss Mann: "That's all right, but don't let papa know It." " Why ? " "He's violently opposed to young men's wild dotes."</p>
        <p>_ A Discov gay.—" I've noticed one thing in regard to children," said Mr. S callow. "In all the tamiliee with which I have acquaintance the first son is invariably a boy."</p>
        <p>A Citum. REMARK.-Mr. Mashbore (to young lady whom he has casually met) : May I have the pleasure of accompanying you part of the way? Miss Snappy: Oh, yes. Our family doctor says we must always walk with some objet, and I sup- pose you will answer the purpose. AN old gentleman's property adjoined a cemetery, and at one time a person who was looking at it with a view to purchase objected to it on this ground, and asked if its proximity did not cause him some annoyance. "No, indeed, my dear sir," said he. "The folks in the graveyard are the moat peaceable neighbours I've got ! " Quiz: "I say, Sophomore, you are posted in the early Germanic history ; what colour was the hair of the old Germans ?" Soph (pompously) : "Blonde or red, ef course." Quiz: " You are Mistaken ; the old Germans had grey hair." MAUD : "I don't see you at Dr. Flipsley's church any more." Madge: "No. The colours the sun throws through those new stained-glass windows were too trying to my complexion, so I was obliged to sever my connection with the church."</p>
        <p>HE: "Have you heard the news? Ye iterday morning Mary Dawson jumped into her f ~ther's carriage and eloped with the coachman. ' She : "What's her father done about it?' He: "He has advertised, 'Scud back the horses and at will be forgiven.'" NOT ALTOGETHER RECKLESS.—Wife : "John, dear, what would you da if I were to die? " Hus- band : "Don't speak of such a thing. I would be desperate." "Do you think you would marry again?" " Well, no; I don't think I would be as desperate as that.' A MISNOMKR.-Mr. M'Darmo : "I font you said this was a bird dog?" Dog Fancier: " Well, of course I did, and of course he is. What's the matter with him? Ain't he all right ? " Mr. Mg): "No, sir, he isn't all right, sir. When I pay for a bird dog, I want a bird dog. That zniaerwable cweature don't catch birds at all, but be caught a howwid rat thiimorning, and you must• take him back."</p>
        <p>A FRENCHMAN, just arrived in England, who had been taking English lessons on the voyage from a fellow•passenger, complained much of the difficulty of our grammar, especially of the irregular verbs. "For instance," says he, "so verb, to go. Did you ever see one such verb?" And with the utmost gravity he read from a sheet of paper—" I go, thou departest, he c!ears out, we cut stick, you make tracts, they absquatulate. Mon Dieu! moo Dieu! what disreplar veihs you have in your English language A CRUSTY old bachelor sat opposite a pa it of middle-aged lovers in a restaurant. They had evide3tly been wedded about a week, and marriage was not yet a failure. They were quite indifferent to their neighbour, who rustled his newspaper and rattled his knife and fork in ,a vain attempt to drown their tendir utterances, and his look of di,. pat was something to see in order to appreciate. There was a momentary silence—then, in honeyed t 03.-4 from the bride—• Oh, baby, please pass the es.l Here the bachelor "drew the , and se.z,uo his hat and coat nvlea hasty exit.</p>
