      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>'LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>'LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>Mr DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I find that pale lemon is a particularly popular. colour for washing dresses this summer, and cotton crepon in this shade makes up charmingly, and what is of greater importance it is very ine.cpenaive, as it is frequently to be had for EP. a yard. A nice way or making.a dress of this material is to trim the skirt with is knee-deep gathered flounce, headed by a narrow-pleated frill, and to make the bodice round-waisted with a small yoke edged with a double-pleated shoulder frill of the crepon. The sleeves have a big, loose pull at the shoulder, but St closely from the elbow to the wrist, and the bodice is finished by a waistband of black satin ribbon, tying just, a trifle to one side of the heat with a bow and short ends, sashes of black satin or moire being introduced on many washing gowns. A hat with a black "nutmeg" crovrn u.nd a wide Tuscan brim, trimmed with rosettes of black satin ribbon and yellow roses, would go nicely with this dress.</p>
        <p>Large hat shapes, as well as close-fitting toque; are worn by young ladies. The former have wide brims, and extremely low crowns, and are some- times trimmed wito a profusion of flowers, whilst others have large bows of coloured crepe and white lace mixed. The smaller hate are trimmed sym- metrically with ' wings, bunches of flowers or rosettes of crepe placed on either side of the crown towards the front.</p>
        <p>A beautiful evening gown io of black Lyons velvet, with front and side panels of pearl white satin; the entire dreis tieing embividered with a Florentine design in gold, picked out with out topazes, facetted like real gems. The corsage has a point of satin let in back and front, and worked to match. The full sleeves are of gold embroidered satin, while a deep Louis XIII. collar of spangled end embroicleree satin, with Vandyke edge, en- frames the decolletage and spans the shoulders with great effect. Yet another gown is of rosy mauve China crape, with lines of broad enru guipure in- sertion running from the waist to below the knees, where a deep flounce of lace to match falls to the feet headed by a toreade of prettily draped China crape. The corsage, under a smart cape-like arrangement of lace, very full over the shoulders, Is drawn down beneath a belt of crape, finished by a bow coquettishly tied on one side.</p>
        <p>One of the prettiest as well as one of the limp- est of the fashionable crossover bodices is made in plain cotton crepon, in becoming shades of mauve and white, and is wonderfully :heap at 4s. lid. Bodices of the same description can be also obtained in r ink and white crepou at 4s. lid. or in ordinary print at 2s. 111 d. Dark blue cotton sprigged with white is much in favour, and a pretty gown of this material which I was shown the other day was made in the following way :—The skirt had a very deep single flounce edged by a band of inch wide guip.ure lace inser- tion, ecru tinted, laid on the material, the flounce being sewn on in festoons with a small bow of dark blue satin ribbon at the to of each curve. The short-waisted bodice had a ribbon belt and white revers edged with lace to match the skirt, and showing a plain pointed vest of the material banded across at rather close intervals with strips of inser- tion. Such a dress as this will retain its freshness almost as long as a stuff, and is suitable for almost any occasion. Flounces are seen on the skirts of the majority of the thinner cotton gowns, and many have three deep ones reaching to the waist; . but this mode is not very pretty, and is most unbe- coming to short figures.</p>
        <p>A fae walking dress in foulard, striped with pale green fading into heliotrope, and flecked with black, was very pretty. It had a deep flounce and a narrow one surrounding the skirt. Bodice with cross over flchu in chiffon and lace fastening at the side beneath a large paste buckle. Full sleeves with double puff; lace epaulette and deep gauntlets. Daring the past few weeks tt.ie weather has been so hot that the thinnest materials have been in demand for dresses for ladies of all ages, and muslins of various kinds are especially fashionable for children's and young girls' frocks. A pretty and inexpensive costume tor a girl of ten is of white spotted muslin, made in the pinafore style, over an under-dress of very pale lettuce-green fabric. The skirt is edged with two narrow frills, and the square-cut top, which shows a chemisette of the green and the braces over the shoulders, are also edged with frills, while the wide sleeves are of cambric. White muslins, -figured faintly with delicate coloured flowers, make up very prettily in the same way, the tint of the under-skirt, yoke, and sleeves corresponding with that of the flowers. White alpaca is also a fashionable Material for the dresses of girls from ten to fifteen years of age, and as it lasts some considerahlo time, and will, indeed, wash well, it is very serviceable. Yours sincerely, Jawsiz.</p>
        <p>Rose Leaves.</p>
        <p>Gather the rose leaves that are about to fall, and dry them in the shade, or three feet from the fire or stove. To 1 lb. of rose leaves add 1 oz. of each, cloves, caraway seeds, and allspice ; pound it in a mortar. Or grind in a mill. Mix all together with a lb. of dried salt ; put the ccmpound into little Japar we silk bags, and place among the linen. This will not only take away the un- pleasant smell of the laundry, but keep away the moths.</p>
        <p>Strawberry Tipsy Cake. Take a high sponge cake, which must he very stale, and with a sharp knife cut a large scriare out of the centre. Meanwhile soak ,sone strawberries in sherry,</p>
