      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>1:14E EXAMINER. SATURDAY, JULY 29, 1893. DARKWOOD.</head>
        <p>1:14E EXAMINER. SATURDAY, JULY 29, 1893. DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>with some sugar, and mash them well with sherry and whipped cream, and the slighe,t flavouring of cinnamon Pour this into the hole, powderiug it with sugar, and fill up to the top ; then pour over the cake sufficient sherry to thoroughly moisten it. Just before sending to table, cover it all over with whipped cream, and sprinkle it with blanched almonds cut into tiny strips.</p>
        <p>Sandringham Sandwiches. Cut thinly a plate of brown bread and butter, cover half tld, slices with potted fowl or game; screen plenti- fully with freshly skinned and minced shrimps, and sea- son uith pepper, salt, and finely chopped parsley. Place on each a Plain slice of bread and butter, and dust.. Parmesan cheese lightly over the sandwiches grated. Put them in is square block under a small press. Remove, and cut them into diamond-shaped pieces. Arrange them on a dish which has been previously covered with an ornamental paper, and garnish with tufts of freshly-picked parsley.</p>
        <p>Setting Doubtful Colours. I It is not always possible, to tell what will set all colours, but a trial of different things with samples of the various gob&amp; will usually settle the matter. Black ' and white cotton goods are generally successfully fixed by adding 'about a cup of salt to every two gallons of water uged in the last rinsing. - A delicate lavender or pale blue had better be set by soaking it for two hours in a pail of strong salt and water before it is washed. Wash it as usual after this, being careful to use no soap that has soda or any strong alkali in its composition. As a rule it is best to avoid any brown soap in washing flannels or coloured goods, but use a good white soap. Wring all calico goods as dry as possible, and hang them in the house to dry. This is better than to dry them in the strong light outdoors, where delicate colours are liable to fade. Another important matter is to sprinkle calicos and coloured cottons just before they are ironed.</p>
        <p>Digestion. this season of the year, according to " Science Siftings," many people have violent attacks of indiges- tion, bemuse they will persist in eating hearty meals when in an exhausted condition. They never seem able or willing to realise that there are times when the system is in no fit state to grapple with a full meal. They come in tired and hungry—almost ravenous—not thinking that may be a good deal of what they consider hunger is gastric irritation, then sit down to a table covered with the substantials of life, and deliberately go to work to overtax the already strained vital powers. No person should eat- heartily when very tired. The wisest thing it, do iq to drink a cup of but water with three tablespoonfuls of milk in it, sit down, for five minutes, and then begin slowly to eat, Masticating thoroughly. In a little while the vigour of the stomach will come back, and all will be well. If this course were followed,there would not be one case of dyspepsia where now there are a dozen.</p>
