        <p>A TALE OF LONE-11100R. - BY Mits. HAARIST Author of " Her Double Life," "Old Lifs's • Shadows," " Lidy Kildare," etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 1.1. AGA'S DISCOVER'S'.</p>
        <p>Squire Markham was back in his old home at Lonemoor ; but the spiriVof unrest possessed him still. Ife looked over his accounts with lawyer, bailiff, butler, and housekeeper; but had made no comment on their administration of affairs.</p>
        <p>To the great surprise of the. entire Orkney family he had not discharged the Qui!lets for their folly in regard to that Miss Winter, He had even declined to listen to. the honest but narrow-minded bailiff, when the-latter had desired to expatiate_upon the dishonesty, the greed, and the general enormity of, the conduct of Mr. and Mrs. Quillet in having established a pauper girl as virtu- ally mistress. of. Lonemoor. But all this had been poured into his ears upon • the ,firat day of his return home, and, though he would not hear another word thereafter concerning Gwen, he had yet heard enough to convince him that the girl was wicked,Aleceitl al, a wretched little monster of .duplicity, and he would as boon have ;made a pet of a deadly cobra as have admitted her again into his honk. After a week' or two the old squire, aged by trouble i rasher than years, shut himself up in his library and saw no one: His old neighbours, who had been his watm friends years before, called upon him to welcome ,him home agaiii, but lie refused to see them. , He was more misanthropic than ever,, a. grim, rarsh old man, with a dictatorial manner, without human interests, esteeming DO man above another, caring for nothing, living only .hecause he must live, but looking forward- to death as the 'laying down of an insuiferabte' burden. Whether beneath that stony see Ming Iffts a capacity, for hopes and fears, for suffering and for happiness, was a ques- tion none could solve.,</p>
        <p>. He announced immediately after his return that be shohld .go away again. in !...Itine to spend the remainder of of his life Omni.. No arguments that -his lawyer cieuld use 'could swerve him from this purpose. His stay at,Lonemoor,- short. as he inteuded to make it, became very soon monotonous in . the extreme. He arose at a certain hour, breakfasted, rode to Penistonc, closeted himself with his book:4 or papers, lunched, dined, and went to bed. lie saw no one but his servants, his la.wYer, and iti</p>
        <p>• . His return to the old house, with its haunting memories, had a bad effect upon his health. Tiara and grim as he was, his heart was very . sore. He had loved his daughter as the apple a his eye • be had been very proud of her, and she had wrecked his love and pride in one fatal horn, and with them had wrecked his life. He bated her meinOrY.: She' was the first of her name who had over brought sorrow to her kindred, and she had been fairest of all her race.. And 3/et, often as he sat in the old library, he would start at the creeking of the stairs, fancying that he beard her. step again, and then he grew angry with himself for his folly, and scoffed at himself for it. . _ _</p>
        <p>In the house she had made bright with her sweet presence he thought more often of her in one week than he had thought of her before in a month. He never went intd the drawing-room; she had loved to sit there. He never went into her chamber; it was there that Gwen had been born.</p>
        <p>But one day, when the thought of his lost daughter haunted him even more than usual, he rode over to Penistone, and, as if by accident, he strolled into the churchyard. He sought and found that dishonoured grave with the name," Mag- dalen " upon its headstone. Then he returned home and shut himself in his library, whence he did not emerge until the next day. During this retire- ment he fasted, and no one saw his Lee or heard ,his voice. _</p>
        <p>It wash day after this visit to Penistone that he received Lord Chilton's letter, imploring Win for Gwen's address. , . .. .</p>
        <p>We have elsewhere given the substance of the young viscount's letter. It recounted all that Lord Chilton knew of Gwen ; it told of her beauty, her innocence, her iweetness, the purity of her nature, the grandeur of her character. No one had spoken to the squire like this of his grandchild. He was amazed. " The young serpent has completely • charmed Lord Chilton,' he thought. "But even were she what he thinks, she is no fitting wife for a viscount, and I would wrong him in permitting him to marry her. If I knew her address I would not give it to him."</p>
        <p>He was about to toss the letter aside, when his eyes caught sight of . the address mentioned in the postscript. " He is visiting at Beechmont, the property of Miss Norreys," he said. "Miss Norreys? I won- der if she is any relation to my late wife? My In other-in-law, General Norreys, who lived in India nearly all his life, and died there a year or two ago, left a daughter. Her name, I believe, was Sicily. If this new owner of Beechmont is Miss Sicily Norreys, she is my niece by marriage— my wife's niece, in fact. I should like to see her before I leave England for ever. If she be the niece of my wife, as I believe, I will make her my heiress. She has as good a claim upon me as any other. I will go to Beechmont before I return to the Continent."</p>
        <p>He locked the letter in his desk, and paid no more heed to it. He forgot its as‹sertion about Gwen, but thought often of its allusion to the owner of Beechmont and the desiie strengthened within him to see this Miss Norreys, and it she should prove tO be the niece of his late wife, to make a will, devising and bequeathing to her his entire property. A few days after the receipt of this letter, Miss Norreys' Hindoo servant appeared at amentoor. At first thought it would seem that Aga was especially disqualified for the mission entrusted to him. Although he dressed after the English fashion, his countenance was unmistakably Eastern. He could not hide his nationality under any dis- guise whatever,, although he spoke the English language with astonishing fluency, and his manners and habits were English. But, notwithstanding this qualification, Aga was more than ordinarily shrewd and quickwitted. Once upon a scent, he was like a bloodhound. Miss Norreys could not have entrusted her errand to safer or better hands. , He walked from Penistone in the early morning, and presented himself at the kitchen door of Squire Markham's house, offering to mend clocks, watches, and glass ware, and asking humbly for 'his break- fast.</p>
        <p>Meg hurried to Mrs. Quillet with his demand, and the housekeeper herself came out to see him. Aga had Is small leathern 'bag of tools in his hand. He was respectably dressed, quiet, respect- ful, and altogether the impression he made upon Mrs. Quillet was favourable. "You can come in," she said ; " we have two clock.s to repair, besides a pile of crockery to mend. If you can do it well, we've work enough to last you a day or two." • The Hindoe followed her .into the kitchen, and thence to the servant's hall. Breakfast was sup. plied to him. After the meal he repaired the kitchen clock with such skill and celerity as to delight Mrs. Quillet. She conducted him to her own room, and he pcsformied a like service in a like manner for her own clock, and was then sent back to the servants' ball to deal with the broken crockery. Meg the dairymaid, was deputed to attend upon him—in other words, to watch him lest be Nhoubl depart with the spoons or other valuables. He produced from his bag a roll of hard white cement, and called for a lighted lamp. Then while he worked, he talked to the girl, who was loqua- cious, and who did not hesitate to impart nearly all the knowledv she possessed to , this. polite heathen. ,</p>
        <p>, By the time dinner was placed before Aga, he had learned all that popular rtanour was wont to' declare concerning Gwen. Ile was toid of the mother who had come to Lonemoor, and who had subsequently perished in a storm upon the moor ; of Gwen's education, of her lovers, of her disap- pcaratice, and of the anxious inquiry that bad been made after her. . . . .</p>
        <p>It must be confessed, in justice to Meg, that all this had been elicited from her by judicions inquiry and hints, all so cleverly veiled that the girl herself supposed she was telling the known history of her life. In the afternoon Mrs. Quillett came in to examine the work. Finding it well done, she sat down and ;watched the process of doing it. 'Then she questioned the Hindoo as to his home—how long he had been in England, and so forth—and he was finally induced to tell his story, which he did in this wise : di have been in England not quite a year, itoara. seaFQl4pg U11..2,1411 Yorksldre I,4l:ita</p>
        <p>old country house where a Miss Granger onc.e lived as geverncss." ' Mrs. Quillet started. "What do you want of Miss Granger ?" she de- manded. .</p>
        <p>"Miss Granger married an English curate," replied Aga, "and the pair were said to have gone out to China as missionaries. If they are ln England they have it in their power to help me greatly. If I -could find Miss Granger and her husband', I'' would' .give ten years of my 'life, lady." "You want them to recommend you to people, or introduce you as a lecturer, or. as a collector of funds for your own people, I suppose ?" said Mrs. Quillet. "But how did you ever hear or know of Miss Granger?" • "A lady from Paris told me of her," said the Hitidoo, working deftly as he talked. "The lady said that Miss Granger had been a governess many years, and had married a curate, and that they oould assist me. The lady was a relative of Miss Granger," he added; "but had lost her address for some years." Aga spoke with the utmost possible seeming innocence. It is possible that he soothed his con- science with the assurance that all the descendants of Adam are relations, and that this conviction of his truthfulness gave him his tranquil mile and serene expression. Mrs. Quillet was completely de- ceived by him. ' She questioned him further.. He talked religion •in a manner that convinced lieethat he was a missionary among his own people, and that he sought Miss Granger's husband as a man seeks another of his own profession.</p>
        <p>Airs. Quillet was an unusually practical and shrewd woman. A vast amount of credit must therefore be given the clever Ago for sc completely hoodwinking her, for in spite of her acuteness, she fell into the snare he had laid for her.</p>
        <p>"Miss Granger used to be a governess in this very house ?" she remarked. . . .</p>
        <p>‘rEli ?" ejaculated Aga. "In this house? Have I then found the place? " yes," said the housekeeper, "she married a curate named Myner—Thomas Myner." "And they are in China ?"</p>
        <p>" No • he was never a missionary, although be talked Of becoming one. He obtained a euracy in London, but lost it after his Marriage. He is now teaching a family school for boys in London, 'and his address is No. SO, Queen-street, Northumber- land-terrace, Nottiji ill. '. She did not notice how the Hindoo's eyes gleamed under their" black brows. "Will yo tt please write the addresi ?" he asked meekly. Mrs. Quillet complied, writing the address on a scrap of paper and giving it to him.</p>
        <p>His object was gained. He placed the paper in his pocket, carefully hiding his jul dance. When he entered the house he had not kor.wii in what manner he should gain success, but he had felt con- vinced that he would be successful. His plan of Procedure had been produced upon the spur of o,e moment, and his delight in its success was all the greater in consequence. He finished his task that day, and at night de. parted on foot, as he had come • for Penistone. And it was not until he had gone that Mrs. Quillet felt any misgiving in regard to him. She at down at once and wrote to Mrs. Myner shout he Hindoo, and disclaimed all knowledge of him, save that he worked one day for her. This letter was dispatched by the next day's post, and went up to town in the same train that carried Aga. But had he known it ho would not have cared. All that he wanted was the name and address of the former governess of Gwen, and that he had safe in his pocket-book.</p>
        <p>CICAP7ER LIT. RE-UNITED.</p>
        <p>There was no chance for flight, if Gwen had had strength to flee. Her lover had found her out in the shadow of the fir tree, and was looking eagerly down upon her face, half in incredulity, half in joyous recognition. Before she could command her thoughts, while her brain was in a wild whirl, and her whole being divided between pain and joy, in that supreme moment Lord Chilton caught her hands and drew her forth from the shadow into a patch of moon- light close at hand.</p>
        <p>—The pale gleams fell upon her massive waves of bronze gold hair, upon her lovely Greek face, white now with great emotion, and into the upturned eyes of purple pansy hue, so full of entreaty, yearning, and deathless love. "It is Gwen I" cried Lord Chilton, rapturously. "I have searched everywhere for you, my darling. Where have you hidden yourself all these months? Gwendoline, speak to me, darling." He clasped her in a passionately tender embrace, raining kisses upon her beautiful face, and Gwen submitted, conscious only of a vague wish that she might clie in his arms in this moment of perfect joy. The Maltese valet, with black, gleaming eyes and bated breath, drew stealthily near the young pair, watching and listening. The sudden suspicion, amounting almost to con- viction, that he had found the outcast child of Constance Markham in "Miss Myner " still held him in a sort of stupor. ' Lord Chilton put the girl from him gently, gazed upon her features with passionate eyes, and drew her back to him in another transport of delight. "It is really you, Gwendoline !" he ejaculated. "It is no dream—fantasy—no mistake I Thank heaven I have found you at last I"</p>
        <p>And still 'Gwen vrae silent. "What have I not suffered in losing you, Gwen r exclaimed her lover. "I was thinking of you at the very moment when your movements among the shadows attracted my attention. To find you here and thus is marvellous; yet, when I recognised you, when I found you under the tree, I was scarcely surprised. Gwen, speak to me. Why are you so silent r She drew away from him now, pale and shiver- ing. "I forgot, in the excitement of seeing you, all that I should remember," she said, tremulously, "No, do not touch me, Ronald. Do not touch me again l" "What do you mean, Gwendoline r demanded the viscount, in amazement.</p>
        <p>"Have you forgotten ' how we parted ?" asked Gwen.. "Oh, Ronald—"</p>
        <p>Lord Chilton drew her arm in his in spite of the resistance she would have made, and led her away from the close vicinity of the ruins, and from the possible neighbourhood of curious servants. Pietro crept after them with the stealthiness of a cat.</p>
        <p>The young viscount paused in a little moonlit glade enclosed by trees, and then addressed his ,betrothed with a tenderness that was almost infinite and with a love that shone from every feature. "Gwen," he exclaimed, "I forgot explanations are due to you. What must you think of me ?" "Nothing but what is good," interposed Gwen. "I never blamed you, Ronald. I told you to so away and think over .the' proposal, and that, if upon sober second thoughts you decided not to see me again, I should not blame you. Remember, I told you that; I did not expect you back, Ronald. I knew that I was no fitting mate for a viscount's son. How much better I know it now. I told you that iT you did not come in the Morning I should know that you had thought better of your proposal to meet me. And—you did not come l" _ .</p>
        <p>The last sentence was fraught with an intense anguish, and Gwen again strove to release herself from his grasp. Ho let her go this time, and regarded her with mingled joy and bitterness. And you believed that of me !" he exclaimed. "You thought that I went back to the shooting lodge and calculated the length of your pedigree, contrasting it with mine? You thought me so cool of heart and head, so base—" "Stop, Ronald. I thought you all that is noble and good. I never blamed you. I knew that I, a nameless girl, the child of an unknown vagrant woman, who lies to-day in Penistone churchyard in a dishonoured grave, was and am no fitting wife for Lord Chilton's son." How Pietro's heart leaped at these words. Why, there could be no possible doubt upon the subject. The girl was the missing Gwendoline Winter—and here under his very hands. " Gwendoline," cried her lover, "hear me, I do not deserve what you thought of me. lam not the base coward you have believed me. Hush! darling, do not protest. Listen to me. When I left Lonemoor that night after our betrothal, I intended to seek you again early in the morn-, "I know it. And I never blamed you, Ronald, for obeying the instinct of your race—" " My poor little Gwen, my own darling, you can- not yet see your mistake. I never for one moment faltered in my allegiahce to you. ,I never for one moment'aesirecl to break our engagement. I have always loved and honoured you above all beings on earth—" - _ _</p>
        <p>"But you did not come back," said Gwen, sor- rowfully. • " cx_94c. I returna4 tile slloothlz</p>
        <p>lodge, and found there a telegram from our family physician, stating that my father had been stricken with apoplexy, and bidding me lose no time in coming to him. That bidding was unnecessary. I never slept that night, Given. I walked the floor of my room. I wrote you a letter explaining my hurried departure, and the night dragged away. At daybreak I rode over to Penistone and took the trst train southwards. I left the letter in the hands of my servant at the „lodge, telling him to deliver it to you and to follow inc by a later train." "I never received that letter, nor sly letter from you, Ronald."</p>
        <p>"I knew that, afterwards. My servant after my departure set out for Lonemoor to deliver the letter. He encountered young Orkney on his way to Lonemoor also. Orkney recognised him as my servant, questioned him, heard of my departure, and of the letter to be delivered. He offered to take the letter to you himself, saying that he was about to call upon you. My servant gave the letter into Orkney's keeping, and turned about, proceeding to Penistone, thence southward by train. It was weeks afterwards when you had disappeared, and no trace could. be found of you, that my servant confessed to me the truth. I knew then that you had never received the letter." "Orkney must have suppressed it. I never even knew that your father was ill, Ronald.'</p>
        <p>_ . . • "I reached home m time to see him•alive," said Lord Chilton. "My father lay for two or three days in a stupor. He revived at the last sufficiently to know me and smile feebly, but he never spoke after his seizure. He died ; a week after came his funeral. In my grief for him I remembered you, my darling, but I was so occupied that. I had no time to write to you, and I had left a letter for you explaining my absence, so that I believed you knew all I would have said." - • "If I had but received the letter." "As soon as I could, after my father's death, I hurried to Yorkshire, but You were gone." "I saw you at the station on my arrival in Lon- don. You drove in as I drove out in a cab. And you were then on your way to sec me. Ronald ?" "Yes. The Quillets refused to give me your address. I searched for you in London, every. whore. I set men to search for you :Llso. I found out young Orkney. watched him, and finally called uPon him to lcarn if he had your address. Re had fallen into very evil ways; but he told 160 that he had once met you in the street, montb.s ago, in Notting Hill, but that you had escaped from him. and that he had not been able to see you here. Had he not been intoxicated he would not have tOld me this. I haunted Notting Hill thereafter, but I never found a trace of you. I wrote to SOuire Markham lately begging him to obtain your address for me from the Quillets and forward it to ate here, but he has not answered my. letter. Oh; Gwen, I was very hopeless, to-night, when I stood at the window. I began to fear that I should never find you!" He put his arm around her yet more closely, and Gwen did not resist him.</p>
        <p>"You are Lord Chilton now, Ronald, are you not ?" she asked, presently. He replied in the affirmative. "Then, more than eve; 1 am not fit to be your -.vife, Ronald !" she exclaimed. "You Must marry in your own station in life. I have 'learned many lessons since we were at Lonemoor, Ronald—and I shall never marry:" s.</p>
        <p>. _ "That resolve I shall soon overcome," said Lord Chilton, smiling. "I have found you, Gwen, and I shall not let. you go again. But tell me, darling, how is it that you are at Dunholm Castle" • "I am governess companion to the Lady Geoe- gina Charteris, Lord Darkwood's daughter," • an- swered Gwen.. "I am Gwendoline Winter no longer, Ronald. You know I had no right to that nanie—that is, that it was not .mine by birth.' When .I left Lonembor I went to London and to the hokse of nly dear old governess, who in married to a very good luau, formerly a curate, now a pro- prietor of a boys' school. They were very good to me, Ronald, they founa *me . pupils in music ; but utter I encountered Claxton Orkney one day in the street 1 became frightened, and dared not go oat into the streets again alone. I knew he would watch for me and I feared his insults. It was at that time that Lord Darkwood advertised for a governess for his daughter. I answered the adver- tisement. Mr. Sutton, his lawyer; came: to see me, and the Myners recommended me for the situation. They desired me to be called by their name urging me to start •anew as their charge, übl I ealue to Dunhohn- Castle as Marian Myner, which is the' name of my dear old friend and governefis." " Then you are the Miss Myner of whom Miss „ Norroys told me?" •</p>
        <p>‘, sae." "She .wa/ery earnest in her admiration of you, 'darling. She has fallen in love with you." "And I love her, Ronald. She is the most beautiful woman I ever saw !" cried OWen, enthusi- astically. "And the loveliest also ! Oh, Ronald, I should be glad to have her for my friend !". "She is your friend now, Gwendoline. And when you become Lady Chilton you shall exchange visits7witih her to your heart's content. Do hot tell me you will not marry me. You belong to me, dearest. You must and shall be my wife. Look in my face. See, have I not suffered? Would you make me suffer more? Would you make my life one terrible blank, as it would be without you? What are considerations of birth and fortune to um, who love you for yourself? And in yourself you have riches unspeakable. Your beauty, your nobleness, your sweetness, your spirit, your. character—all these are wealth. I will not give you up—unlesi you have ceased to love me, Gwen." The girl„looked up into his face, as he had bidden her. In the pale glow of the moonlight he looked years older than he had looked when she last saw him.. His features were sharpened, 'there were deep lines about his mouth, and care and sorrow had left their impress upon his face. She saw that he had suffered more than Words could tell—even as she . had suffered. How he loved her! After all, what were the distinctions of birth and fortune 'to those who loved as they did? Why should the error of the dead be visited upon her innocent young head? Why should he not yield to his per- suasions and become his wife?</p>
        <p>"Oh, Ronald," she cried, "you tempt me cruelly. But are you sure that your friends will never make you repent your love for me? Are you sure that you will never be sorry if I consent to marry you? I have bad blood me, and you may grow ashamed of it."</p>
        <p>"I deny that you have bad blood in you," inter- rupted Ronald. No one can look at you, 0-yen, and believe that story. Your looks, your carriage, your very features are all noble and aristocratic. I know that you have sprung from honourable people, Gwen; because your mother came to Lone- moor alone and crazed they have all thought her wicked. No one knew her name or staticin, except that she was a gentlewoman. Might she not have been an honoured wife who fled her home in a fit of insanity? Might she not have been an honoured widow, crazed by her husband's death ? " • "I never thought her any other than the name on her tombstone declared her."</p>
        <p>"I will have that stone, with its lying inscrip- tion, removed," said the young viscount. "I will make every effort to discover her real name and identity. But if I fail, Gwen, as I probably shall, for after all these years it will not be easy to dis- cover her history, I shall still believe that your mother was an honourable gentlewoman, made insane by some misfortune. I shall still believe you of good family. We would be insane to believe otherwise.' Do figs grow from thistles? Do grapes spring from thorn trees? Do noble children come from ignoble parentage? No—a thousand times no !" His" earnestness infected Gwen. Could it be possible that the load of shame that, had lain so heavy upon her was to beremoved ? Could she honour and love the memory of that young mother whose life had guae out in such horror? " Oh ! Ronald, It it only might be as you think I" she exclaimed.</p>
        <p>"It is so, darling, believe me. And I will prove it so, if skill and patience can unravel her history. Whatever your origin. you are to me the one woman in all the world. lou are mine, Gwen, and 1 shall not let you go !" His gentle air of authority, his infinite tender- ness and passionate love, overcame all Gwen's scruples. Her head dropped to his shoulder. She had given over the contest. "I intend to claim you very soon, Gwen," said her lover. cannot leave you here. You must marry me at once. I will tell Miss Norreys in the morning and we will be married froni her house." ",No, no," cried Gwen, entrektingly. "When we marry, Ronald, we must be married from the house of the • Myners. And I must go to them first. And, Ronald, Miss Norreys and your friends must not know me as- Gwendoline Winter. Let them know me as Miss Myner. I cannot come to them with that old Lonemoor story hatigiog about me in a horrible shadow." , "But, Gwen, I have told Miss Norreys that I loved a certain Miss Winter." "Then say nothing to her about having found me here--,not at present, Ronald. Let me go •to London 1.4o:le 2,0 u tell her,"</p>
        <p>"She will love you all the more for your sorrows, - darling. ' Trust in me, Gwen. If you do not want Miss Norreys tc; kr,..w just yet, I will not tell her, but I know that she will be more than ever your - friend and mine when she -heara of the truth. I I.iant her protection for you. Let me tell her to- norrow, dearest. I have fold her' too much to , withhold the rest." 1 "As, yon think best," said Gwen, with a half .sigh. I would like your friends ta think well of re • for your sake, and you kno7iv the prejudice that exists against unequal alliances." "This will not be unequal. Oh, Gwen, I thank' heaven again and again that I have found yclz I . do not like Lord Darkwood, and I shall not leave you in this house another day. I will call 'upon him to-morrow and tell him that you are to becorao my wife' and that he must look for another govet" ness for his daughter." __</p>
        <p>The lovers wailed up and down the moonlit with its encircling shadows, and Pietro flittered to and fro from tree to tree in safe obscurity so doge at hand, and listened to their words with 'a sinister joy that showed itself in his distorted Vis' age. Gwen was the first to think of the parting that was inevitable. "They will miss you Ronald," she said at last. "You must go back to the ball-room, and I must return to my rooms." glade,. Lord Chilton held her captive a little longer, and then released her with tender words, He declared that he BM:mid call upon het' the next day and bring Miss Norreys with him. He desired a speedy mar' riage, which might be as quiet as she pleased, b w...s yet in mourning for his father, but it must be immediate.</p>
        <p>....... And so they parted in the little glade, and tli young viscount watched with woi m shippines the slight and graceful figure of his young betrothed, as ,the flitted across the quadrangle and disappeared' within the inhabited portion of the castle. Then Lord Chilton slowly retraced his steps towards the ball-room. , When he, too, had disappeared, Pietro exulted,, within himself, and muttered— have found my game at last ! Miss Gwendo- line Winter has been six months within my reach and I did not know it. Lord Darkwood is ignorance of the truth. Before he can know it, she must be.beyond his reach, and in my power, o; My Lord Chilton, I shall be obliged to spoil your little love affair. This girl must belong to me. She is my lawful game. Per BaCCO I How am I take possession of her? That's the question !"</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
        <p>FOUR HOURS TOO LATE.</p>
        <p>" Oh, God ! if I could recall the past three or lot' hours. ' See what trouble a man may briny upon himself cE in a moment."</p>
        <p>Mr. James Curtis and Mr. C. S. Bennett, both of , Fiancisco, had been intimate friends for years. Lastlf g summer they quarrelled ,for the first time, each accuE'i ing the other of wrong. Retter thoughts -prevailed and they were reconciled. But you can't undo what is onc-6 done. Memory kept the record on her slate. Violj avoided each other as much as possible. Still, living 86_ neighbours, men must meet some time. These two ine°, in the street. 'I he quarrel was renewed. Bitter wordg flew fast and thick, and Curtis beside himself with 111 i drew his pistol and sht Bennett dead on the sl:Ot'i Later in the day he used the above language—so laden ,1 with self-reproach and sorrow. Yet how useless, ilov"' hopeless, how vain.</p>
        <p>But was Curtis right in saying that trouble may CO all in a moment? True, it often seems so, but is there not a deeper fact which we don't see ? It mattere nothing what the nature of the trouble is. Therefore let us consider a different case on the same principle.</p>
        <p>1 An intelligent woman says :—" In October, 18900 J. had an attack of illness from which I never expect&amp; recover. I had aching pains all over me and a cJuga that nearly shook me to pieces. I obtained no good : sleep night or day, and had to take to my bad. I fed with liquid food fiom an invalid's cup, for I cow" not raise myself in bed. My heart fluttered so Y°) could hear it beat on the pillow, and often pains strue" through it as though somebody had stabbed me. I 135'4 perfeetly helpless and could scarcely breathe. A dcnt°Ar attended me over a month, but I grew weaker a" 1 1 weaker. Sometimes at night I was so bad he feared I ' would not live till morning. He called in a consulting ' physician and both agreed that my condition wag critical. I was fed with brandy to.keep me alive. SY' husband and daughter stayed with me- almost non' stantly. None ot the medicines administered had ail -I effect. I was almost at death's door.</p>
        <p>"At this time Mrs. Keeling, of Mutley, near Fir mouth, a friend etA mine, urged me to try a medicine r' called Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. I procured JO, bottle, and after a few days I was able to take neu, digest sufficient food to give me some strength, 'and worst symptoms were greatly abated. After haViuei.„ used six bottles of the Syrup my health was comPlete3/1 restored, and I have since felt better than for thepretl.o%, thirty year.t. My two daughters have also been cured e` indigestion by it. I will gladly answer any enquiries. (Signed) Mrs. Louise Jackson, Builders' Arms Hotel, Bridge-road, Hammersmith, London, Jan, 14th, 1892.</p>
        <p>• In Me letter from which the above is an extract, gig' si Jackson further says that for over twenty years bef°:re the attack of October, 1890, she had suffered from a ordered stomach and liver. She had a bad taste mouth, a poor appetite, and what little she ate gala her pain. She felt dull, languid, and tired, and ba(l.o miserable sinking feeling in the stomach, great pain the chest and sides, palpitation, giddiness, and I'l quently fell it the street and had to be airisted hoi</p>
        <p>So we perceive that in her case a cause, long in opery tion, at last produced the crisis which came near cud; , 1 ing her life. It is always thus, whether we reccghls the cause or not. The crime committed by Curtis the sudden passionate act of a man who allowed thougl"it' , of hate and vengeance to take possession of his 111113ie and breed the condition which made murder Pcssittr; , In the very different ease of this lady's illness it was enemy of her body, indigestion and dyspepsia, which ° length broke out into violence.</p>
        <p>The lesson is the same. Watch the beginning 01 e' and check it while yet it may be easily controlled.</p>
