      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>ARTEAIUS WARD ON WOMAN'S</head>
        <p>ARTEAIUS WARD ON WOMAN'S</p>
        <p>RIGHTS.</p>
        <p>I pitcht my tent in a small town in lnjlanny one day last season, &amp; while I was standing at the dore takin money, a depytashum of ladies came up &amp; sed they wos members of the Bumkumeille Fe- male Moral Reformin &amp; Wimin's Rite's Associashun, and they axed me if they coed go in without payin. "Not exactly," sea I, "but you can pay without gain in." " Dew you know who we air 2" tied one of the wimin—a tall and feroshus looking critter, with a blew kotton umbreller 'under her arm—" do you know who we air, Sir ?" "My impreshun is," said I, "from a kersery view, that you are feuales." "We air, Sur," said the feroshus woman—" we belong to a society whitch beleeves what' has rites—whitch beleeves in razin her to her proper speer—whitch beleeves she is intiowed with as much intelieck as man is—whitch he- leeves she is trampled on and aboozed—&amp; who will resist hense4th &amp; forever the incroachments of proud 8c domi- neering men." Dunn her discourse,the exsentrid female grabbed me by the coat-kollor and was swinging her umbreller wildly over my hed. " I hope, marts)," sea I, starting back, • That your intensions is honourable ? I'm a lone roan hear in a strange place. Besides, I've a wife to hum." " Yes," cried the female, •• k she's a slave ! Doth she ever dream of freedom -cloth she ever think of throwin of the yoke of tyrriny &amp; thinkin &amp; yotin forself P—Doth she never think of these here things ?" "Not bein a natral born fool," sed I, by this time a little riled, " I kin safely say she cothunt." " 0 whet—whot !" screamed the female, swinging her um- breller in the air. "0, what is the price that woman pays for her expeeriunce !" • "I don't know," sea I; " the price to my show is 15 cents per individooal." "&amp; can't our Society go in free ?" asked the female. "Not if I know it," sed I. " Crooil, crooil man !" she cried, &amp; burst into tears. " Won't you let my darter in ?" sed anottiv of the exsentrle svimin, taken me afeckshunately by The hand. "0, please let my darter in—shee's a aweet gushin child of natur." " Let her gush !" roared I, as mad as I cood stick at their tarnal nonsense; "let her gush !" Whereupon they all sprung back with the simultanious observashun that I was a Beest. "My female friends," sed I, " be 4 you leeve, I've a few remarks to remark ;, wa them well. The female woman is one of the greatest institnoshuns of which this land can boste. It's onpe.ssible to get along without her. Had there bin no female wimin in the world, I sheuld scarcely be here with my unparalleled show on this very occashun. She is good in sickness—good in wellness—good all the time. 0, woman, woman !" I cried, my fehlins, worked up to a hi poetick pitch, "you air a angle when you behave yourself; but when you take off your proper appairel &amp; (mettyforically speaken) —get into pant) hams—when you desert your firesides, k with your beds full of wimin's rites n,shuns en round like roarin lyons, seekin whom you may devour some- buddy—in short, when you undertake to play the man, You play the devil and air an ernfatic noosance. Mv 1 female friends," I continnered, as they were indignantly departin, " we well what A. Ward has sod !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>• A IRADES UNION LIBEL CASE.</head>
        <p>• A IRADES UNION LIBEL CASE.</p>
        <p>The trial of the action for libel brought by Mr. Have- lock-Wilson, M.P., against a London evening news- paper, was begun on Monday, at the Guildford Assizes. Mr. Wilson claims £7,000 damages against the news- paper company for a statement published during the recent Hull strike, to the effect that the funds of the Seamen's Union, of which he is the secretary, had been used in a manner contrary to that provided for in the rules Of the Union and the Act of Parliament. The plain- tiff. under examinat on, stated that out of a sum of £12,000 nearly £7,000 went to the maintenance of offi- cials. He defended the non-publication of certain docu- inents showing the position of the Union because of the reason that they did not want the shipowners to know the state of their finances.</p>
        <p>The action was concluded on Tuesday. The jury held that the comments were fair and reasonable, and not malicious, and that there was no libel. On certain points, if the High Court held there had bren libel, the damages would be one farthing. Mr. Justice Grantham entered a verdict for the defendants.</p>
      </div>
