        <p>nticePr'tninent tir'mye in all Irish and Catholic re- l'ettrtie: ,LOndonha' s just passed away. Mr. G. N. o 'wn has just died at Bermondsey at the Ot ti'eed age of seventy, was for nearly a quarter ,stillsrd,e,entnry Major-General of the "Cardinal a disciplinary corps, established for the „,411 fli-h'e of attending the late Cardinal Manning at `jtese-"„elians connected with the League of the temperance organisation, which he founded Petit eara ago. It is expected that the inter- alajor Pearce, which will take place at 0,..--natnne probably on Saturday, will be made- ITlttp;,.'asion of a demonstration by the various ',esieVinne organisations of the metropolis. Mr. POlitaT 110 Was formerly a member of th4i Metro- Ise ilp,ice force, wherein he obtained the rank iln.aottiVrisPector, received the pledge from the 1 043 temperance advocate, Father Mathew, in ' and kept it fdithfully until his death. 41114 l' • --- r. tea h, Iva in deeds' not years ;in thoughts, not thivitg--:,aud measured by this line Alderman James 141t11;' 'no dead Mayor of llalifax, had lived a umlong ,1• heredonourable life, though his actual years ni d„. three short of the half century. As Alr.G Rint,!ilsed to say of the late Mr. Ayrton, he was a ' teat "()TI for work," he has really died of want of kin!. e' men have rendered, within their ap- Rene—r.BPhere, so much service to their day and ttle vzition and not only the town of Halifax. but tzerialv eSt Riding generally, has reason to keep his att,i°l'3, green. Himself a scientist of no mean Dre,„tnnents, he was unceasing in his efforts for the lierc",°tion of popular education, especially as RP- technical industries, and it was to his ini- hatieZ:lnd Subsequent labor that Halifax owes its after ne puoile library and art galleries, while_ te4aanY difficulties he carried his pet project.o - nical college irit successful working. hi T. l f I.' 411 th' 1 11,i Is he was actively interested in th Y Cite orksre loßic;,n,of Mechanics Institutes, the Yorkshire Geo- l. 'al and Polytechnic Society, the Yorkshire Col- t</p>
        <p>loge, the Yorkshire Fine Art Society, and half a dozen others; was a prominent Freemason,an ar&amp;nt and advanced Liberal, serving the local association as hon. sec. for some years ; a scientific litterateur of note, his treatise on the geology of %Vest York- shire, in two large volumes, being regarded as a standard work ; and he was a permanent Member of the General Committee of the British Associa- tion. Ile was serving the office of mayor for the third time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilfred Blunt, who held on Saturday at his place Crabbet Park, one of his biennial sales of Arabian thoroughbreds, is not only interesting as having married the daughter of Bron's Ada," sole , daughter of my house and home," but from the , open and fearless sympathy which he and Lady Anne Blunt displayed for Arabi Pasha at the moment when the fate of that rebellious Egyptian was hanging in the balance. Mr. Blunt and his wife during their long wanderings amide, the tents of , Syria had learned to love the Arab race, and they frankly expressed their feelings as.regards Arabi, 'whom they regard as the honest. leader of an oppressed people striving for freedom. How Mr. Blunt stirred public opinion in England into obtain- ing for Arabi not only a fair trial, but English. council, Mr. Broadley, the counsel in question, has, ably related in his well-known work, " How we defended Arabi," a volume he dedicated to Mr. Blunt and Lady Anne," Arabi's best friends—his friends in need."</p>
