      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>SCIENCE A 2, D LABOR.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE A 2, D LABOR.</p>
        <p>BY Mn. H. WHITIIAM.</p>
        <p>In an article I recently read on the Hull strike, I was not at all surprised to find the writer attsibut- ing to the ignorance of the workmen the difficul- ties placed in the way of an early and amicable settlement of the trouble. That we are ignorant, I do not attempt to deny. We have had so far no real cause to be ashamed of the fact. If it con- tinues much longer to be a true ell arge,to be brought up against us as a claes,eaeh time a difference arises betwixt us and our employers, it will not only be dis- graceful, but it will be damaging to us as important factors in the social problems hurrying on to solution at this closing of the 19th century. lam not just now thinking of any money loss that fella upon the workmen and community alike on account of this want of know- ledge, but rather to that other, and, so far as I can see, more important feature, the opportunities and possibili- ties that science is steadily, yet surely, bringing to the front. So far as I have been able to discern, I can see no difference between the workmen in the States and those in the old country. I think lam right also in stating that the larger portion of the workmen in the heavy trades here are of European birth. And lam quite sure that the disturbing element in the labor world, and of which we have heard so much lately, is not native born. This explains.largely, the great similarity exist- ing between the labour ruptures of Europe and those of the States. And yet there is a more intense feeling in- troduce.' into the labor disputes here than is common in England, or has been since the days of Broadhead,which some of us well remember. The continental unrest, or what I may call the exotic Socialism ot Europe, which is well read,and not at all to be looked upon as ignorant, comes over here expecting to find some kind of Utopian liberty. What it does find, is the labor market over- stocked with the cheap labor of Finland, Armenia, Hun- gary, and Poland. This, with grasping capital acting as fulcrum, causes the up and down see-saw troubles that end in nothing but trouble for all concerned. Writers here on labor questions seem to be pretty well agreed that European .immigration is the great clog Uncle Sam has at his heels, and that the best thing for us all would be its decisive stoppage. This would give the States a chance to reap some benefit from her splendid edueationalsystem,and it would most certainly force Europe to get a move on her, and try if possible to snap the Conservative chains that bind her fast. That these chains are being weakened link by link is evident by the concessions recently made in Belgium to the revolutionist and Socialistic party. The recent elections in Germany where the Secialist party has grown from 124,000 votes twenty years ago to 2,000,000 votes just cast, is an object that ought not to be lost sight of. This globe of ours is growing less. The astronomer, and the geologist may not be aware of the fact, but the social economist knows all about it. The capitalist and the manufacturer know it too. What we want now is the working man to waken up to the fact that a great shrinkage in distances is bringing the whole civilised world into neighbourly contact. That the Western States of America, the greatest grain growing district in the world is nearer, in the sense of cost, to the Liverpool market than the agricultural counties of England. He will then understand why a farm in Essex of 300 acres can be sold for 365. an acre as I read has been done recently. I am a trade unionist in principle, I may say, of the new sort. I have a fair stock of theoretical socialism, not practical, I allow, sort of latent, all the same. I want to press upon my fellow drudges the necessity of study- ing Socialism from the Fabian standpoint, and then the world may be made worth living in, because we should then know how to take opportunity by the hand, and make her our hand maiden. In the meantime what is happening ? In every manufacturing town of note, I care not where it is, labor is crying but for its share of the good things of life. It riots, and plays the fool to the top of its bent, while wealth and luxury stand by laughing at the impotent monster, or possibly trembling, and hoping it will last its time. While referring above to vanishing distances, my memory was also travelling back to a time when I along with others was being made to feel its full effects. I hope to be forgiven by some of my readers for referring to it. Nothing but a deep and intense interest in their welfare induces me to do so now. I refer to the time when one of the largest wire manufacturing firms in England was importing hundreds of tens of small wire for weaving purposes. Now everyone knows that when a manufacturer turns merchant and buys the article he should produce that there is something radically wrong somewhere—buying it too, from a foreign country. It means that his own workmen are idly walking about. I also remember the late Mr. P. Rylands telling me that he could make more money, sitting quietly in his office, buying, instead of manufacturing the wire used by his firm. And I also remember that this was all scouted, and treated as bosh by those more vitally interested in the question. Time has proved what truth there was in the statement. I have no desire to drag any particular trade into the columns of the Examiner. What I have said, I am going to say of the wire trade could be said almost to the letter of the textile trade—as for instances of the extra number of looms under the care of each weaver here compared with what is done in Lancashire. Trade Unionism here troubles not its head with methods of working so long as it gets a wage that is satisfy- ing. I find from my study of the labor question that the individual output of the wage earned for such out- put, is about the same the orld over, India included. Now, as to the wire trade, what I think about it now differs not one tittle from what I thought ten years ago. I find in a published letter I then said, "To have to compete with the German or the Americans in the markets of the world imposes a duty upon employer and workman to join hands in securing a triumph over that competition; but don't let one starve while the other rolls in luxury--don't leave it to the workman to display all the patriotism and to sustain all its costs." Fate, I suppose, has since made me an American, so we must cross that word out of the above. Anyway, we must ask why should English wire manufacturers buy Ger- man wire, as I sin assured they are doing largely, and keep their own work -nen walking about the streets year after year? There are some awfully sharp stinging points about-this nettle, but I think it best to grasp it. Have employers done all they could to carry their men along with them? Have they tried tsi make them realize the fact that a good trade is being killed ? I fear not. If the employers are receiving a fair percentage of profit on their capital, no matter how it is invested they will no doubt eat their bread and butter in peabe, rather than rush into trouble with their workmen. But about the workman, is he content to eat the bread, without the butter, and live in a chronic state of grumble? Apparently this is so. I have said workmen here. raise no objections to methods. A roller here is asked for a piece of g wire 300 lbs. weight, and he at once rolls it. If he is asked for a piece of Bof 200 lbs. weight he rolls that. These come to the drawer, and he draws them. Ile takes the rod at 8, runs four, five, or six blocks, and gives it three holes or more. In numberless instances that have come under my observation, men who never saw a piece of wire three months before are earning 10s. and 12s. a day. I could be doing the same had not rheumatism, and my eyesight unfitted me for the work. The work as done here is relentless, and knows no forbearance with the old, or the weak. Like your own Deptford dockyard,it has no use for men over 50 There are more one armed and one legged men at the place I now work at than I think I have seen in my life before all put tog,ether. No fear of this state of things coming about with you. Retain your apprenticeship system. Let boys learn when young how to handle wire, how to make their own tools, and learn to under- stand the nature of wire. The speed at which mac,hinery runs in England is much quicker than here. This, and the weight of the pieces accounts for the number of blocks a man flan run. It can easily be seen that this all only needs modifying to be applicable in England. There are of course a few other features of detail, important in themselves, but which I cannot go into here. Outspoken honesty of purpose may of course turn out to be mistaken zeal, but when year after year pass over showing a gradual declination of trade, it must be evident to the least thoughtful that the industrial system like farming interest is breaking down, and may call for heroic remedial measures. What is the fencing trade of England compared with what it was twelve or , fourteen years ago? Simply nothing. So it will be with the telegraph trade. Science is just revelutionising the face of the globe, and it will drive the industries along with it, or it will leave the trade that will not move, to fall through. It matters not what one's private opinions are about all this. The inexorable law of competition has no favourites.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 29, 1893.</head>
        <p>E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 29, 1893.</p>
        <p>A BEIiKSIIIRE MIRACLE.</p>
        <p>MARVELLOUS ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Hearing a good deal of village gossip about a won- derful restoration to health, we had almost said resus- citation to life, by the use of some new medical dis- covery, we sent a representative of the " Berks arid Oxon Advertiser" to Elm Cottage, Harwell, a few days ago to interview the person said to be brimming over with narratives of the extraordinary results allege a to be attained by the use of it. We expected to find ' either that the tale was a hoax, or the patient either a fanatic or a person of weak intellect. We were wrong in all conjectures. The gsssip even understated the case, and the patient is a man of extraordinary shrewdness and intelligence. Our representative found him in his shirt sleeves gardening, and on being told Iris errand, viz., to have a chat with him about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, he at once in- vited the reporter into his house, and without further ado told his history, and gave a description of the numberless ailments he had suffered from, and thel many narrow escapes from death he had experienced. I He had\been in hospitals and infirmaries in many parts of England. He had been discharged first from one and then from another as incurable. He had taken nearly every medicine he could hear of, consulted dozens of doctors, had undergone operations, taken sea voyages, contracted nearly every disease known, even including Yellow Fever, and though given up by his friends again and again, he never lost hope himself, though he once lost all he possessed during an unusually protracted illness of threa years' duration in Liverpool, and then he did for a time despair, for in addition to the loss in his savings and loss of health, he could obtain but little rest, and had scarcely any appetite. His case was so complicated ,that as a doctor told him, it was impossible for any man living to understand it. In short, he was so thoroughly ! broken down, that to use his own words, "lie at last came to Harwell to die." He was indeed a wreck. The former stalwart frame had wasted to a skeleton, the use of one leg was rendered powerless by rheumatic affec- tion, whilst the unskilfulness of one of the medical men who had attended him had deprived him Of an eye, land he was as much like a mummy in appearance as it is possible to imagine. He often longed to be out of his misery: he told us. Quite by chance,however, he heard of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Palo People. He deter- mined to try them, and fancying that they afforded him relief he continued taking them. He Was soon enabled to take refreshing sleep, his appetite gradually returned he began to take an interest in what was going on around him, and hope at last returned. In fact, he realised that his whole system was being built up or renovated, and he gradually threw off his ailments one by one, and became a new man. When we called upon him, not- withstanding that it was the hottest day of the year, Mr. Woodley, as we stated at the outset, was in his shirt-sleeves, and was busily at work in his garden. And a well-kept garden it is, with vegetables, fruit, and flowers in profusion, everything in perfect order,show- ing that a good deal of energy and hard physical work had beer, expended on it. Mr. Woodley explained that he ultimately recovered the use of his leg through the cut log of the tendon,and as we looked upon the active, hale old man, full of life and hope, and contrasted his posi- tion to day with the terrible sufferings he had under- gone, we certainly thought the results attained were very wonderful. It was with difficulty we were able to get away from him, and as at last we were making our way down the garden path from the pretty cottage on the hill-side, the old gentleman called out exultingly, "They told me before I came here that I should never be able to do any more work. Look at me now. See what wonders Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have done for me." We may add that Mr. Woodley is a man of wide experi- ence. In his younger days he crossed the Atlantic no leSs that sixteen times, and was for some time a steward on a Mississipi steamer. He afterwards kept a restaur- ant in Liverpool. He is therefore a man of the world, and not one at all likely to be gulled. We give his testimony just as he gave it to us, and as he will only be too glad to repeat it to anyone else. If any should doubt the reality of these facts, it is easy to call on Mr. Wood- ley, as we have dene, and verify them. Harwell is about two miles from Didcot Junction, and Elm Cottage, where Mr. Woodley lives, is in the Reading-road, on the south side cf the village. The postal address is Elm Cottage-, llarwell, near Steventon, Berks. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are shown on unimpeachable evidence to be a positive and unfailing cure for all diseases arising from an impoverished state of the blood, such as Antenna, Pale and Sallow Complex- ion, Green Sickness, General Muscular Weakness, Depression of Spirits, Loss of Appetite, Palpitation of the Heart, Shortness of Breath, Pain in the Back, Nervous Headache, Dizziness, Loss of Memory, Early Decay, all forms of Female Weakness, Hysteria, Paralysis, Locomotor Ataxy, Rheumatism, and Sciatica ; also of all diseases arising from vitiated Humours of the Blood, which cause Scorfula, Rickets, Hip-joint Diseases, Chronic Erysipelas, Consumption of the Bowels and Lungs, and that they invigorate the Blood and system when broken down by overwork, worry diseases, exces- ses and indiscretion of living. These pills are not a Purgative Medicine. They contain nothing that could injure the most delicate system. They act directly on, the blood, supplying to the latter its live-giving quali- ties, by assisting it to absorb oxygen, that great sup- porter of all organic life. In this way the blood becoming" built up" and being supplied with its lack- ing constitutents, becoming rich and red, nourishes the various organs, stimulating them to activity in the per. formance of their functions, and thus eliminating diseases from the system. These Pills are manufactured bv Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, of 48, Holborn Viaduct, London, England (and of Brockville, Ontario, Canada, and Schenectady, New York), and are sold only in boxes bearing the firm's trade mark and wrapper at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d. Pamphlet sent free by pest on application. Bear in mind that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are never sold in bulk, or by the dozen or hundred, far any dealer who offers substi- tutee in this form is trying to defraud you, and should be avoided. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills may be had of all chemists, or direct by post fro= Dr. Williams' Medicine Company from the above address. The price at which these pills are sold makes a course of treatment comparatively inexpensive as compared with other remedies or medi- cal treatment.—Berkshire Advertiser, July 21.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WIDNES BAKERS AND THEIR HOURS</head>
        <p>WIDNES BAKERS AND THEIR HOURS</p>
        <p>OF WORK.</p>
        <p>PROBABLE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Tt e Widnes branch of the Amalgamated Union of Operative Bakers and Confectioners of Great Britain and Ireland has issued a circular to the master bakers in the town, stating that on and after Saturday next the following conditions will be demanded by the men :—That there shall be five o'clock starts and one twelve o'clock start on Friday nights ; that the minimum rate of wages be—for before- hands 345., second hands als., and third hands 245. per week, wages above the minimum not to be interfered with ; that ten packs per man per week constitute a week's work, and 2s. 9d. per pack be paid as overtime, eighty loaves public being reckoned as a pack ; bun and seed loaf to be paid for at the rate of 2s. Sd. per 100lbs.' and smalls 3.-3/4 per tin ; that public baking be abolished altogether in the . towns on Saturdays ; that the minimum weight of loaves be not less than 21bs., any- thing under that weight to be reckoned aa small and to be paid for as such. A deputation from the executive council of the above union attended a special Meeting of the Widnes and District Trades Council this week, and explained the position of the bakers In the town at present. The following resolution was eventually adopted unanimously :—" That this council endorse the action of the Bakers' Society, and cordially approve of their reasonable demands, including the abolition of night work, and it trusts that they will be entirely successful. It further asks to be supplied with a list of those masters who refuse to comply with the same," The circular has been issued by the Bakers' Union in consequence of the body being unable to arrange a meet- ing with the masters. It is very probable that the bakers will come out on strike next week if the above concessions are not granted.</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say Critehley's " STARCH GLOSS s unequalled tor impartinv a beantifrfl finish to starched l'nen, either cold or twilling water starch. Mr. —, Cors. cr.tll, says :—'' My wife will not finish our linen wit hout your 'Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs.—. Bradtord f— "Dear sit . I am pleased to inform you that 1 have tried many so-called arch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It :ould be used In every house. Bold at Id., 33., and 63.. by food starch selhrs, if not in stock will procure it. T. egucHLEY. BlAckburn. Proprietor.-4d.2t.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>This Board held their fortnightly meeting on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse, when there were present Mr. J. Mclntyre (chairman), Revs. J. W. N. Tanner, Dr.Gastaldi and J. Hanby, Messrs. T.Lca, A. R. Norman, T. Handley, D. H. Jones, E. Aaron, J. M. Williams, H. E. Heywood, T. Baxter C.C., C. W. Davenport., J. Parr, S. Woodward, T. Lewis, E. Bratherton, J.Purkinson, S.Taylor.S. Varwood,and J. Brandreth, J.Ashton (clerk), and G. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION QUESTION. The Clerk writing to the Local Government Board on June 10th stated that at the last meeting of the Board a resolution was passed of which the following was a copy. " Resolved cn the motion of Mr. Brandreth, seconded by Dr. Gastaldi, that the Clerk forward to the Education Department a list of the schools to which children go for whom the Guardians pay school fees, setting forth the names of the children and the amount of fees paid, and stating that the Guardians require free school accom- modation for the said children. The followed the names of 48 children paid for during the quarter for which the amount was £2 Bs. 7d.—A letter of reply was read from the Education Department inquiring whether the Clerk's letter represented that there was in the School District of Runeorn or any part thereof an insufficient amount of public school accommodation without payment of fees for children over three and under 15 years of age for whom such accommodation was desired. If such was their intention, their Lordships would at once proceed to make inquiry under Section 5 of the Elementary Education Act, 1891.—Mr. Taylor said that the question was as to whether the Board had power to ask or decide where the children should go to. The parents declined to send their children to the Board schools.—Mr. Bran- dreth said that the Local Government Board asked the Guardians to give them particulars. They ought to go right through with the matter.—Mr. Woodward said it seemed to him that there was not sufficient free school accommodation in Runcorn.—Dr. Gastaldi said that he had got room for twelve more free.—Mr. Hanby: It must be stated what is the religious character of the schools.—Dr. Gastaldi : We do not interfere with them. —Mr. Brandreth thought that the Clerk should answer "Yea" to the letter, as there was not sufficient accom- modation.—Rev. J. Hanby said that they had room for a dozen in Dr. Gastaldi's schools. They ought to know how many other places there were in other schools.— The Chairman : I think trpre are no other free schools except the Board Schools.—Mr. Hanby: Then there is additienal room for 37 required.—Mr. A. R. Norman said he was on the committee before which the parents appeared and not one of them were willing to send their children to the free schools. They were ques- tioned very pointedly on the matter, but they were anxious that their children should attend the denomina- tional schools. The Local Government Board would therefore enforce the Guardians to pay the fees. It seemed to him that they were wasting a lot of time in righting against an Act of Parliament that was imper- fect and did not give free education.—Dr. Gastaldi pro- posed that the letter lie on the table for twelve months. If they went on with it they would have to build new schools in Runcorn when there was no necessity for it as many of the Ship Canal people whose children went t ) the schools would then be nearly all gone. It would pay the Guardians better that this question should re- main untouched until such time that they really did see there was a necessity for doing so.—Mr. Jones seconded Dr. Gastaldi's proposition.—Mr. Taylor said it was not a quektion as to whether they had sufficient school accommodation. It was whether the parents would send their children to free schools or not. They had Dr. Gastaldi's :chools and there were the Board schools, but the parents who came, before the cernmittee distinctly said that they desired to send their children to the schools which they had been going to. They were most particular in every case in putting the question to the parents. Every case was thoroughly ince stigated, and every member of the committee felt quite satisfied that they were genuine cases. It was a surprise to all of them that two-thirds of the people were not having pay from the parish. He maintained that these people had a right to demand their own choice of schools.—The Chairman (interrupting) : That question is not before us Mr. Taylor ; it is what reply the clerk is to give to this letter.—Mr. Woodward proposed to reply to the Local Government Board requiring in the district 49 free places in denominational schools for which the Board was at present paying.—Mr. Norman thought that would do no good. The Local Government Board would wish to know whether the parents were willing to send them to free schools, and if not were these denominational schools free ? The Guar- dians would have to reply that there were no free places. The Local Government Board would sineply write back saying that if the parents insisted upon sending their children to fee paying schools when there were no free places, the Guardians wmuld be compelled to pay the fees.—Mr. Hanby moved as an amendment that the correspondence with the Local Government Board cease, and that the fees asked for be paid by the Guardians.—Mr. Jones seconded.—Dr. Gastaldi with- drew his motion in favour ef Mr. Hanby's motion.— Mr. Brandreth objected. He wanted free education (Mr. Norman : You have not got it). Never mind that. Here were 49 cases for which every week the Guardians had to pay 2d. or id. each. The schools got the grants besides for the children passing the various standards. If the schools could not do with that, they had no right to come to the Guardians to help them out in their ex- penses. He opposed the payment in every way. If the Guardians declined to write he would write himself to the Local Government Board.—Mr. Norman supported Mr. Hanby's proposition. Mr. Brandreth might write to the Local Government Board 50 times, he would never get any further in the matter. Some members of the Board did not seem to have confidence in the Board. He rather resented it.—Mr. Brandreth again maintained that they wanted free education. The schools had no right to charge and the Guardians ought not to pay the fees —Mr. Hanby thought that that was both illegal and illogical. Nothing would satisfy Mr. Brandreth but 'free education, and that was not the place to get it.— Mr. Woodward said that his proposition had not been seconded. It was the only answer to the letter as far as he was concerned.—Mr. Heywood said he would second R.—After a long discussion Mr. Hanby's motion was defeated, and the the motion to write to the education department requiring 49 free places in denominational schools was carried by 12 to s.—Mr. Hanby : The answer would be pay for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF NURSE.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF NURSE.</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Williams of Liverpool was appointed nurse ab the Workhouse at £25 per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>DISTRICT COUNCILS BILL.</head>
        <p>THE DISTRICT COUNCILS BILL.</p>
        <p>A petition was adopted with reference to the Local Government Act, 1893, in which the Board expressed their concurrence in the proposal of the Bill to establish District Councils, and to amalgamate the duties of sani- tary and highway authorities within the district, and they urged he desirability of the insertion of a clause empowering district councils to assume the manage- ment of main roads in their district in the same manner as urban authorities, which were in existence at the passing of the Local Government Act, 1888 ; and it was undesirable to have the authorities charged with duties of maintaining roads in one district, but that the whole of the roads in the district should be under the control of the District Councils.—The petition was adopted, and it was decided to be sent to Mr. J. T. Brunner, M.P.,and other members whose constituencies lay in the Board's jurisdiction. stansiENANce OF DESTITUTE PERS,NS.</p>
        <p>A communication was received from the secretary al the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners, enclosing an account of the maintenance of destitute persons in their hospital. The officers of the Authority had looked care- fully into the circumstances of the persons named. There had been treated in the hospital 114 persons dur- ing the late epidemic of smallpox. The account for 23 persons amounted to £B3.—On the motion of Mr. Bran- dreth, the Clerk was instructed to inquire on each pay- ment intn the condition of the persons for whom they were paying.</p>
        <p>COST OF PAUPER LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>A letter from Mr. Alexander Hornby, Clerk to the Committee of the City Asylum, Chester, was read, stat- ing that the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in the institution for the quarter ending Michaelmas next, was fixed at 7s. per head per week.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth, in presenting the Finance Committee's report said that the amount in the treasurer's hands was £642J.5. lid.; expenses amounting to £251 185.4 d. The chief accounts had been for vaccination.—rhe minutes of the committee were passed, which concluded the business.</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>PARLIAMENTARY FOURSOME (FINAL TIE.) This competition concluded on Saturday, when Browne and Nicholson, clerks, receiving two strokes, beat Hon. T. W. Legb, M.P., and H. Seton Karr,M.P., three up and two to play. The final consisted of thirty- six holes. Nicholson and Browne were two up on the first round. L.gh and Karr started well for the second round, winning first hole. Browns and Nicholson were five up at twenty-seventh. Legh and Karr improved. and at thirty-third were only two down, but Browne and Nicholson won next hole and the match.</p>
        <p>WI .GUARANTEE SATISFACTION I . _</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaints. Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder, Urinary Organs, Stone, fleet, Stric ture, Sciatica, Rheumatism, and Gout, will find a positive cure in lloldroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box, and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Price Is 14. Of all chemists, or post free for 12 stamps from Hun- BOYD'S Medical Hall, Oleckheaton, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor, and a box will be sent Rext post.—Advt. --</p>
        <p>Mr. William Aronsberg, the Jew philanthropist and justice of the peace for Manchester, and well-known public man, against whom £5OO damages were given in the breach of promise of marriage case at Manchester Assizes, on Tuesday filed a petition in bankruptcy. He declared in the box that he would be unable to pay if damages weie awarded. Before filing this petition an offer was made to settle with the lady for £lOO. "CADBURY'S COCOA has, in a remarkable degree, those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endur- ance and hardihood, with a steady action that renders it a most acceptable and reliable beverage."—Health.</p>
        <p>SAD FATALITY TO A WARRINGTON SCHOOL BOY,</p>
        <p>THE ADJOURNED INQUEST.</p>
        <p>In the Parade Boom on AI ondqy afternoon, the adjourned inquest on the body of Thomas Smith, aged six years and nine months, was conducted by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner.—Mr. A. Browne again watched the inquiry on behalf of Mr. Toole, marine store dealer. James Tinsley was the first witness called. He said he was driving a coal cart along Bewsey-st., when the accident occurred. He was about 20 yards behind the coal cart of Mr. Griffiths. He saw Mr. Plinston driving a spring cart with two other persons in it, but it had passed Griffiths' cart before the lad left the footpath to cross the road. About twenty yards behind Mr. Puns- ton's cart was one driven by the boy Toole, who was driving at the rate of about eight miles an hour' and it was Toole's cart that knocked the boy down. He was knocked down by the shoulder of the horse, and one of the wheels went over him. He afterwards saw the boy get up, and the policeman carry him to the house of Dr. Adams. He considered the boy in Toole's cart was driving furiously.—Mr. Browne: Who called you to appear here ?—lnspector Bowen.—Mr. Browne : But did You not walk down here with Plinston P—l was with him about ten minutes.—Mr. Browne: Was Plinston drunk at the time of the accident P—No.—Mr. Browne: Did not he drive as furiously as Toole P—No, not as fast. James Smith, carter for Mr. Edward Lunt, said lie was going down Bewsey-st. from Tanners-lane. He saw the boy standing on the footpath near the school-yard gate. He afterwards saw him run between two coal carts which wsuld be about 15 yards apart. Two spring carts were going the opposite way, the first one belosging to Mr. Plinston and the other was Mr. Toole's, and the shafts of Mr. Plinston's cart knocked tile lad down, and the wheels el the cart then passed over him. Toole's cart passed while the lad was on the floor, but didn't run over him. He was quite certain that it was Plins- ton's cart that knocked the lad down, and it would be travelling about eight miles an hour. The boy got up and walked a step or two and then Sergeant Lawrence went to him. He had no ill-feeling, against Plinston or Toole, he only knew them by sight. Both carts came out of Tanners-lane, and the accident occurred near St. Alban's Catholic Church.</p>
        <p>William Plinston said he was in charge of a spring cart, and was accompanied by his son, and a man named William Hewitt. On the afternoon in question he came from the horse fair in Winwlck-street, up Tanner's-lane, and along Bewsey-street, but it was half an hour after passing along Bewsey-street that he heard an accident had occurred: The Coroner : Did you not see Griffiths' or Tinsley with coal carts or James Smith. No.—The Coroner : But James Smith has said he saw the shaft of your cart knock the boy down.—lt is false, the children had not commenced to come out of school when I passed.—Mr. Browne : How many times have you been fined for being drunk in charge of a horse and cart? That has nothing to do with this case and I refuse to answer.—Mr. Browne: How many times have you been charged for furious driving.—l refuse to answer those questions.—Mr. Browne: When you went down Bew- sey-street, what did you turn down Brown-street for ? I went to my brother'e stables to " swap " horses with another man. The witness now went on to give par- ticulars of the bargain when he was stopped by the ,Coroner remarking that he was not now in the horse fair. Ile " swopped "horses by the Wesleyan Chapel, when he was told that young Toole had run over a boy. His , son afterwards told him that he saw the accident when lin the cart and put his hand up to Toole to stop. The Coroner: And did your son David see the accident in Bewsey-street, and not draw your attention to it ? Neither ef them opened their mouths to ane.—The Coroner : This seems very funny. David Plinston said he was in his father's spring cart sitting down at the back of the cart with Hewitt, looking behind. When coming along Bewsey-street, he saw Toole's lad following with his cart about 100 yards behind. He saw Toole's cart knock the boy down. He shouted to him and waved his hand for him to stop, but he still came on and caught them up as they turned round Brown-street. When he shouted to young Toole that he had run over a lad, he replied " Say nothing about it."—Mr. Browne : When you shouted was it loud enough for your father to hear ? Both lay father and the lad would be able to hear.—Mr. Browne: Was not the horse your father was driving in the habit of rearing up.—No, it was a kicking horse. Mr. Browne : Didn't the horse rear up at Mrs. Duck- worth's corner, in Allen-st. ?—No, sir, that was the horse we weregoing to " swop." William Hewitt, of Widnes, said he was in the cart with the Pliostons. He never knew that a boy had been knocked down until Saturday, when Mr. Plinston came for him.</p>
        <p>Charles John Jones, 1, Froghall-lane, said he was sit- ting, at one of the windows of their house when he saw Mr. Plinston driving in a spring cart, and Mr. Toole's cart. He was about 20 or 30 yards in front of Toole's cart. He saw the lad all right when Plinston's cart had gone past the two coal carts which were standing still when the accident occurred. Plinston's cart would then be a little past St. Alban's gates. Dr. Adams said he had examined the coat and the clothes, but could not find many hairs on them. Some -of the hairs were of a brown or bay colour, and some were of a grey colour. Mr. Browne said Toole's horse was a jet black one. Frank Jones, a scholar at Wycliffe School, said he knew the deceased, and saw him kicked in the mouth. The horse was gAng towards the railway bridge. It was a black horse that knocked him down, and the cart , had a boy in. ' Percy Wood, aged six, another scholar, said he saw the boy knocked down. It was a brown horse, and a man was driving it. Annie Turton, twelve years of age, a scholar at Wy- cliffe Schools, was again called, an,cl corroborated her former evidence that it was Plinston's cart that knocked the boy down. Her evidence differed completely from that given by the other witnesses, in so much that she said Plinston's cart came from the Liberal Club way. When they hae knocked the boy down they went to the corner of Bewetiv-road, and there they examined the horse's feet. They afterwards proceeded to Bewsey briclge, and when they came back they brought a brown horse back. She then went to the infant mistress, Miss Porritt, and told her What had happened. On being asked by the Coroner if she could point out the driver of the horse, she immediately went to Mr. Plinston. The Coroner commended her upon the way she had given her evidence.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Marratt, 17, Foundry-st., who lives near Mr. Toole, saw Mr. Toole's son crying, and on being asked what was the matter, he said he had run over a child.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mary Ann Morris, 16, Camden-st., said she saw Toole' s lad after the accident, and he said, " I ran over a boy, but I could not help it, could I ? There were two lurries passing at the time." Edward Toole was re-called, and said he told Mrs. Marratt and the other woman that a child had been run over, but he didn't know whether it was him or not. Mr. Brighouse afterwards severely commented on the inaccurracy of the police report which was sent to him, in the first instancc, in which it was stated that "two carts were being driven respectively by William and David Plinston down Bewsey-street, when the deceased who was just leaving school was standing in the middle of the road and was knocked down by the first horse which never stopped. Sergeant Lawrence picked the boy up and took him to the surgery of Dr. Adams where he died." From that report it seemed as if the: Sergeant saw the lot. The Sergeant in his evidence said he did not see the accident, but hi thought the deceased was not knocked down by Plins- ton's cart. The duty of the jury was now to unravel thia mystery and say how the boy came by his death. If they could their finger on the person they must say whether he was negligent or not. The jury returned an "open verdict," with the pre- sentment by the Coroner to the Warrington Corporation that the horse fair should not be held in unprotected public streets. The Coroner also commented on holding horse fairs in public streets. Be said in his own town they held horse fairs in unprotected streets. He con- sidered it a remnant of the barbaric age to see horses being driven up and down public streets where people were continually passing and repassing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>GOOD STIIP RADICAL.</head>
        <p>THE GOOD STIIP RADICAL.</p>
        <p>MR. LABOUCTIERE ON TEIE POLITICAL.OUTLOOk.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lab-a-U.-thee-re, M.P., writes in " Truth " :—When Admiral Tryon ordered the Victoria and the Camper- down to turn within six cables all thought that there must be a collision. The admiral's subordinates, how- ever, obeyed orders, deeming that the admiral possibly knew better than they did, and that there was some- thing within the resources of his mind which would render it certain that the collision would be avoided. This is very much my mental condition in regard to the course of procedure in the House of Commons on the Home Rule Bill. As a member of Parliament I obey the signals of Admiral Gladstone, as a journalist I take the liberty to suggest that the good ship Radical will encounter a serious danger of being swamped at the general election. What, then, do I suggest? That the Home Rule Bill be carried over to next year, and that the report stage and the third reading be taken next session. I would begin the session by passing a few British bills, then, say in June next year, take up the Home Rule Bill at the point that it has reached this year, and send it up to the Lords.</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CURED.,--S. J. SHERMAN, Hernia Specialist, 64, Chancery-lane. London. sends his hook on kupture post free. Branch Chambers, 26, Eling-st., Manchester. —Advt.</p>
        <p>ThR Recounts of the Salvation Army Self-denial Fund for 1892 show that the sum raised by this effort for the work of the organisation was £50,002.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL.</p>
        <p>A VAAL.</p>
        <p>-F EVEREND JOSEPH. HOLMES' improvPd JL Mexican Prescription, as discovered In the het table- lands of Old Mexico, is a guaranteed cure to all those wh3 tuffer from the errors and indiscretions of youth, netvous weakness, physical exhaustion, and early decay. To Intro- duce it into England, the Mexican Prescription will be sc.nt free of charge. upon receipt of a self addressed stamped envelope. Only address: JOSEPH HOLMF S. Remedy Co.. Lloornsbury Mansions, Bloomsbury-square, London, W.C. Ziention this paper.</p>
        <p>CRISIS IN THE COAL TRADE,</p>
        <p>STATISTICS OF THE' MINERS' FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>TV--officials-of the National Miner's Ft deration have completed their returns wi•li regard to the number of men who will cease work to-day (Saturday), in accor- dance with the decision come to at the Birmingham Conference. The total number of notices served by the coalowners in the Federation districts affect 218,320 men employed underground and 36,224 employed on the surface, giving a total of 277,000 men who will cease work at the end of the week. The notices which have not been served by the coalowners in the Miners' Federa- tion districts but which the miners themselves have been instructed to send in affect some 62,000, including 2,000 in Lancashire, 7,800 in Yorkshire, 640 in Derbyshire, 20,000 in the Midland Federatios (which includes Staf- fordshire), 300 in Nottingham, 584 in North Wales. together with about 20,000 in Monmouth and about 6,000 in Cumberland. All these have been directed by the Miners Federation to serve notices on the coalowners to terminate contracts. In Durham, Northumberland, Monmouth, and South Wales there are in addition some 125,000 men whose action with regard to the dispute is doubtful, but the Miners' Federation hope that a consid- erable portion of these men will also come out. The Executive of the Miners' Federation has insisted that in all cases proper legal notices shall be given by the men —where they are under weekly contracts a seven days' notice, and where they are under fortnightly contracts a fourteen days' notice,—se that in all probability a con- siderable portion of the 62,000 men who havegiven their employers notice will continue at work for fully a week after the 277.000 men have ceased work.</p>
        <p>THE RAILWAY COMPANIES AND THE PROSPECT.</p>
        <p>On inquiry of tt;e-LOW;;;;;;;;IN-o-rth--W-e-st-ern Railway Company at Crewe on Tuesday, as to the effect which the strike of colliers may have upon railways, a corres- pondent was informed that the London and North-Wes- tern renewed their coal contracts three weeks ago. Most of these contracts contain the strike clause. The company do not anticipate running short, as they have large stocks. Some trains, however, may have to be discontinued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>GLOOMY OUTLOOK.</head>
        <p>A GLOOMY OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>One of the largest coalowners in the Midlands, in the course of an interview on Tuesday, said a stoppage,and probably a prolonged one, is inevitable, as the masters Ao not intend to offer any other terms than those now before the men. The miners in the Midlands will come out on Monday, and that is the district which will be most acutely affected, as the stocks of coal there are low, one of the largest railway companies being within a fortnight of exhausting its supplies. The same author- ity says that fresh contracts will cf.rtainly be at consid- erably advanced prices ST. HELENS AND WIDNES ALKALI WORKS. _</p>
        <p>It is probable the stocks of coal in the works of the United Alkali Company at St. Helens and Widnes will last about a fortnight after the colliers have ceased work</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>DECISION OF THE SCOTCH MINERS.</head>
        <p>DECISION OF THE SCOTCH MINERS.</p>
        <p>At the Scottish miners' conference, in Glasgow, on Thursday, repreientatives of over 21.000 men decided to restrict the output by working only four days per week pending the perfecting of their organisation. Re- presentatives of over 17,000 favoured a strike in a fortnight failing an advance of wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>STOPPAGE AT sr. HELENS.</head>
        <p>STOPPAGE AT sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night the whole of the casting halls at the Sutton and Ravenhead Works, St. Helens, of the London and Manchester Plate Glass Company, were stopped for an indefinite period. Notices were issued last week that the casting halls might be stopped on account of the shortness of fuel, and these expired on Wednesday night. A large number of men are affected, and the stoppage will cause exceptional distress, especi- ally in the Sutton district. CONFERENCE OF SCOTCH MINERS</p>
        <p>The Scottish miners' delegates met in conference on Thursday in Glasgow to decide on their future action with regard to the colliery dispute. Mr. S.Woods,M. P., who attended the conterence,urged that united action on the partof the whole of the miners of Great Britain was necessary in order to maintain wages advances. Reso- lutions in favour of a strike following a demand for an increase of wages, and, on the other hand, pledging the miners to work four days weekly pending the Improve- ment of their organisation, were submitted. The votes of the delegates on these were so evenly divided that the meeting was adjourned. In the districts of the National Federation where notice of the 25 per cent, reduction has been given by the employers work at the mines ceased on Thursday. - - - -</p>
        <p>The Cumberland miners have decided to throw in their lot with the Federation. They will demand an advance of wages of 20 per cent. Their notices for the advance will be sent in on Friday, and, if not agreed to, work will cease on August 11. In Lancashire and York- shire it is stated that thorn is an equally determined feeling for and against the 25 per cent. reduction on the part of both the celliery owners and the miners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>'WORMWOOD SCRUBBS MURDER EXECUTION OF COOKE. A PATHETIC STORY.</head>
        <p>THE 'WORMWOOD SCRUBBS MURDER EXECUTION OF COOKE. A PATHETIC STORY.</p>
        <p>Constable George Cooke, of the Metropolitan Police, was hanged at Newgate at nine o'clock on Tuesday morning for the murder of Maud Smith, with various aliases, at Wormwood Scrubbs. The particulars of the crime are painfully notorious, and while great provoca- tion was undoubtedly given, the murder could not be regarded as other than of the most brutal character. A pathetic phase of the sad story is the devotion shown by Cooke's fiancee, whose persistent efforts to obtain a reprieve have evoked general sympathy. The execution took place punctually at nine o'clock, and by order of the high Sheriff the representatives of the Press:were excluded from the precincts of the prison. Since his arrest Cooke had manifested apparent indifference to his ate. He expressed no sorrow for the crime, but on the contrary seemed to experience a feeling of intense relief at being rid of a woman who persecuted him, and threatened his future happiness. During his incarcera- tion his chief concern has been for his aged parents and his sweetheart. He had paid most respectful attention to the chaplain's ministrations. Billington was the Pxecutioner • and he arrived at the prison on Monday and tested,the apparatus to be employed in his grue- some task. Cooke retired to bed at ablait ten on Mon- day night, but was very restless, slept little, and rose by seven on Tuesday morning. Breakfast was taken to his cell, but he ate sparingly. Ile spoke cheerfully to We warders, and thanked them for the kindness and consideration shown to him. The chaplain remained with the unhappy man till a few minutes before the hour fixed for the execution, the approach of which was signalled by the tolling of the passing bell at St. Sepul- chre's Church. Billingtomentered the condemned man's cell about three minutes to nine, and pinioned the cul- prit in the presenco of Governor Millman and the other officials, after which the procession was formed to the scaffold, the chaplain reading the burial service. Though pale and haggard Cooke walked firmly to the scaffold, an shook hands with the chief warder prior to the final scene. A drop of over 51t. was allowed, and death was stated to have been instantaneous. • From eight o'clock on Tuesday morning a huge crowd had waited in the Old Bailey to witness the hoisting of the black flag on the flagstaff above the debtor's door, the majority of those present hay ng, evidently expected the execution to take place at eight as usual. Immediately after Cooke had paid the penalty of his crime, the Under-Sheriff saw a Press representative, and stated that the convict died without a struggle, and made upon the scaffold no statement or remark beyond an expression of thanks to tho officials already referred to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE BARTON SECTION. On the Barton Becton of the Ship Canal the work has now reached its final stage. The various dams which carried the Irwell at several points across the course of the canal are being rapidly cut through. Just above Barton aqueduct is a large dredger which has ploughed its way down from Salford, and this will shortly supple- latent in a most effective fashion the operation of many spades. The aqueduct itself is all but complete. There was a mishap some time ago in connection with the deviation of the waters of the Bridgewater Canal from the old channel to the new. Since then everything has been rostored and strengthened, and on an early day next month we may expect to see the swing aqueduct in use. The swing bridge which runs side by side with the aqueduct Is already part. of the general high- way. The two structures are noble pieces of worktnan- ship. They swing on a common platform placed midway in the channel of the canal, with a clear opening on either side of it of 90 feet—an ample allowance for the largest vessel. Between the aqueduct and the bridge is the signal tower. Here the look-out man, by the simple pulling of a lever, sets in motion the powerful machin- ery by which the swinging process is sccomplished. All the machinery, it should be said, is now in position in the red-brick building adjoining the aqueduct. In the boiler-house are :,wo splendid boilers made by Messrs. Galloway and one. On an upper floor there are engines in duplicate which control the most powerful and at the same time the most delicate mechanism. Not only does the hydraulic installation command the turning of the aqueduct and bridge, but the power is further communicated by means of pipes to the locks , half a mile or so below, . where it is regulated with the ; utmost simplicity. So far complete are the operations I on the Barton section that many of the mechanical aids ! to excavation have been sent to the yard at Salford, ! where in due course they will be disposed of by sale. The water which runs down the channel is far from pure, but in this respect it very considerably improves on that in the Salford docks. A nuisance is here caused which has led to the calling of a public meeting, which was held at Ordeal on Tuesday night. The subject is one in which Old Trafford also is very deeply concerned.</p>
