      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENL</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENL</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evening the Home Secretary brought in a bill to regulate the sale and use of pistols, and it was read the first time amid cheers Mr. ACLAND stated, in answer to a question, that the Education Department was taking measures to have structural defects in all schools, Board and Voluntary, remedied within a reasonable time. The House again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill, and discus- sion was resumed on Mr. Gladstone's new clause for regulating the financial relations between the United Kingdom and Ireland. The second reading of the clause was carried by a majority of 35.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords Earl CADOGAN raised a discussion on the attitude adopted by the Lord Lieutenant of Ireland in regard to the reception of addresses from the 'loyal minorities" of Ireland. Earl Spencer defended , Lord Houghton, upon whom the Marquis of Salisbury ' made a bitter attack. TUESDAY. In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening Sir E. GREY, in answer to questions, said her Majesty's Gov- ernment had received from the French Government verbal notice of their intention to establish a blockade of the coast of Siam. Her Majesty's Government pro- posed to c mtinue their present protection to British life and property in Bangkok, for which they had sufficient naval force.</p>
        <p>The House again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill, taking into consideration Mr. Gladstone's new clause on the financial relations between the United Kingdom and Ireland. Mr. J. REDMOND moved an amendment which had finr its object to give the Irish , Legislature immediate control of Irish taxes. Mr. Glad- stone, could not accept the amendment, and Mr. Sexton advised Mr. Redmond not to press it. On a division the amendment was rejected by 249 to 53. Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN moved an amendment reserving control of taxation to the Imperial Parliament until Parliament should otherwise determine. Mr. GLADSTONE, in a spirited speech, replied to Mr. Chamberlain's remarks of the previous night, and rebuked the right hon. gentleman for his exaggerated misrepresentations of the Home Rule scheme. Debate on the amendment was adjourned at midnight.</p>
        <p>The House of Lords devoted a lengthy sitting to the discussion of the London Improvements Bill, and before reading the bill a second time adopted by 55 to 36 a resolution expressing the opinion, that proposals for the assessment of capital values instead of annual values and for creating new areas of taxation ought not to be embodied in a private bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WhDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WhDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill on Wednesday, resuming considera- tion of Mr. Chamberlain's amendment retaining to the Imperial Parliament control of taxation until Parliament shall otherwise determine. Mr.CUAMBERLAIN replied to what he called the "ferocious speech" made by the Prime Minister on Tuesday evening, and after Mr. Bal- four, Mr. H. Fowler, and Mr. Goschen had spoken a division was taken, the result being that Mr. Chamber- lain's amendment was rejected by a majority of sixty. Sir J. LUBBOCK moved to omit sub-sections 2,3, 4, and sof the new financial clause. Mr. GLADSTONE said this amendment was really in opposition to the clause, and on a division it was defeated by a majority of 47.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TIIURSDAY</head>
        <p>TIIURSDAY</p>
        <p>In ths House of Lords, on Thursday, the Royal assent was given by Commission, to 73 public and private Acts. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Lord Roseberry in answer to Lord Lamington, said that from the very first Her Majesty's Government had refused to interfere in anyway in the dispute between France and Siam. They had, therefore, limited them- selves to providing for the safety of the British lives and interests at Bangkok, and it was to be regretted that some should appear to suspect in that provision—none too large for the security of life and property in an Oriental population of 350,000 souls—an encourage- ment. to the Siamese to persevere in a hopeless resist- ance. Lord Roseberry next referred to the preponderat- ing commercial and shipping interests of England in Siam, adding that for this reason they regretted that a blockade should be deemed to be necessary by the French authorities, and it might raise some difficult questions nf international law. Moreover, the terri- torial dispute involved a matter of British concern. The baw of Commons Amendment Bill was read a second time by a majority of 9. The Trustees (Consolida- tion) Bill, the Copyhold (Consolidation) and the Con- gested Districts Board (Ireland) (No. 3) Bill were read a third time and passed. The Housing of the Working Classes Act (1890) Amendment Bill was read a see3nd time.</p>
        <p>lathe House of Commons, Mr. J. Montiiir,replying to questions, agreed that so-called outrages lin:hided in the returns of Irish crime were often ordinary police offences and conveyed a wrong impression to many who read the returns. Mr. ACLAND informed Lord Cranborne that no com- pulsory charge for books was permissible iii any public elementary school. Mr. Herbert Gardner obtained leave to bring in a bill ,to confer further powers under the Contagious Diseases (Animals) Acts, 1878 to 1832, with respect to swine I fever, and it was read a first time. Mr. Accaign,repl}ing to Mr. Snapp, said tlie primary -object of the departmental committee appointed to re- present the Education Department, the Science and Art Department, and the Charity Commissioners, was to bring about more co-operation between tne departments concerned in matters in which they were generally inte-</p>
        <p>rested. Mr. FOWLIIER informed Mr. Holland that the Local Government Board were dealing as promptly as possible with the case which came before them with regard to works required for the purification of the river Irwell. Mr. AENOLD MORLEY informed Mr. Macartney that it . had been decided to abandon as too costly the experi- ment of sending American mails from Queenstown by special train and packet services. .The Ebuse went into committee on the Home Rule Bill. An unparalled scene occurred at a later hour in I connection with a division, twenty or thirty members engaging in a struggle on the floor of the House,in the course of which blows were freely exchanged by Colonel Saunderson and mr.crean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>VICTORIA COURT-MARTIAL</head>
        <p>THE VICTORIA COURT-MARTIAL</p>
        <p>,DEbIVERY OF JUDGMENT. MALTA, THURSDAY, 3.35 P.M.—On the conclusion of Captain Burke's defence, at alf-past ten, the court de- liberated in private until half-past three. The public proceedings were then resumeu, aud toe court delivered its verdict. The court found that the accident seas en- tirely due to Admiral Tryon's order, RIO acquitted Cap- tain Bourke and the other survivors of the Victoria of all blame. The court regretted that Itedr-Aumtral Markham did not carry out his first intention to ask for an explanation of the signal, but found that it would be fatal to the best interests of the service to say he was to blame for carrying out the directions Of his Commander-in Chief present in person. The President afterwards returned Captain Bourke his sword, and the court dissolved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ANOTHER DEEMING CASE.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER DEEMING CASE.</p>
        <p>WIFE'S BODY BURIED IN AN OUTHOUSE.</p>
