      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. JULY 29. 1893.</p>
        <p>THE opponents of Home Rule, The Houso of - • to_ile_d and defeated in the House Landlords.</p>
        <p>of Commons, delight to dwell upon the subject of the "happy despatch" which is to be given by the House of Lords to what they describe, in their genial jocular way, as the "Betrayal Bill." They never tire of telling us of the summary and contemptuous treatment the Bill is to receive when it is sent to the " Upper " House. According to some of 'them the Lords will refuse even to consider the bill ; they will • treat it as an unclean thing and keep it at a respectable distance. Others go so far as to con- cede that their Lordship will, perhaps, condescend to dissect and mutilate the measure before kicking it out. There is always a full crop of these pleasant prognostications just after one of the mis-named amendments has been decisively nega- tived in the House of Commons, or an otherwise interminable debate has been ended by the closure. It would be well, however, if these political prophets would be a little less petulant and out- spoken. As Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT told Mr. CARSON the other evening, " they let the cat out of the bag." Besides it is not very discreet to put too much faith in hereditary legislators. They have on more than one occasion deserted their guns and left the field to the enemy,and they may do so again. The House of Lords, as everyone knows, does not I any means occupy an invulner- able position. As at present constituted it is an archaic member of the Constitution, and it can only hold its ground in these days by what may be described es irreproachable behaviour. Now its exaduct during the present week has not been ir- reproachable, and it is quite within the range of possibility that the House may find itself in con- flict with the popularly-elected Chambr even be- fore it has had a chance of expressing its pleasure, or giving vent to its passion, with regard to the Home Rule Bill. The House has again given proof that it is opposed to self-government not only in Ireland but in London, when self- government is at variance with the interests, or rather the unjust privileges, of landlords. The House of Commons quite recently endorsed by a large majority a provision in a Bill promoted by the London County Council giving effect to what is known as the principle of" betterment." The London Council is about to undertake some large improvements, one of which will entail an ex- , penditure of four orfivehuudredthousandpouuds.</p>
        <p>When this improvement is carried out it Will bring " to the front and vastly enhance the value of cer- tain property which is at present not so well situated. As all this additional value will be coy ferred by the spending of public money, the Lou- don County Council very properly proposed that - small and fixed proportion of it should be diverted</p>
        <p>-- towards paying for the improvement. The justjCe and propriety of the proposal was so manifest that,</p>
        <p>as we have said, it was endorsed by a large majority in the House of Commons, there being'" fact scarcely any direct opposition. But not som the House of Lords. It has always been usual for landlords to • pocket the whole of ths enhanced values given to their property by, public improvements, and the Lords, who are al— ways mare mindful of the interest of the landlord!' than of the public, could not witness even this slight encroachment upon unjust privilege with equanimity. They, therefore, passed a resolutwo which, in effect, amounts to an instruction to th° Committee before whom the Bill will come untler.'r to strike out the "betterment " clause. This, I' done, is almost certain to bring the two House into antagonism, for it ienot to be expected tha the House of Commons will quietly submit 'to 3' provision which is so obviously for the advantage' not only of London but of all populous centre'. where expensive improvements have to be undertaken,being wiped out bytheir lordships. Tli° House of Lords, which is, we are told, to take uP so militant an attitude with respect to the Bole Rule Bill, will then at once be placed on the fensive. The ultimate result is not at all douL ful. The peers will, as they have so often d'2ne before,make a judicious retreat from an untenabl' position. Bat the lesson may be useful.</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN declared on Thl- Riotous Legislator. day evening that the Government ill reduced' the proceedings of "Mother of Parliaments" to a discreditable fare' Before the evening was, out his bitterly abusics tongue brought about a scene the like of %chic' for disorderliness had never before been seen le British legislative chamber. This was noth less sensational than a " House of Comulet3 Riot" as the "Daily News" appropriately' dB-. scribes it. Hitherto, hon. members, no illet_tel' how excited, have been content to throw at es`'-' other nothing mom hurtful than carefully,or lessly, chosen adjectives, but on Thursday e' ing actual physical violence was resul to. The commencement of this alale outbreak was, as we have said, acrimonious speech by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. Tl7"e,i the member for West Birmingham went te length of comparing Mr. GLADSTONE to 11-• Herod there were cries of "Judas" from the 111, benches. The more rabid members of the party were spoiling for a scene, and this was 601. opportunity. They made loud and boistoreue.e demands that Mr. T. P. O'Coxxon, who was esibe to have uttered the objectionable word, should se' asked to apologise, and they persisted in the 0. demands in spite of the fact that the Chairel*, declared that lie had never heard the cry "Judas." The scene rapidly developed, 91 according to one descriptive reporter, ',Hats' knocked off off in all directions. The House wee' with uproar, and in the gangway was seen a tura tuous mass of men clutching at each throats." Colonel SAIINDERSON has made bellie-ose speeches, and now he had an 41n— d displaying his valour. He took advantage it, for he was seen "striking out right and These disgraceful proceedings were only bre` to an end by the advent of the Speaker 10' authority was immediately recognised. The cident will, however, stamp a dark blot on records of Parliament.</p>
        <p>THE verdict of the Court 318%,10 The Victoria which has been inquiring into Disaster. causes of the Victoria disaster 'ily returned just such a verdict as was geverter anticipated. CAPTAIN Boman: and all the otP</p>
        <p>surviving officers of the ill-fated vessel aro - pletely exonerated from blame. The evil:len/It% ',- given all tended to confirm the previOUslY lished accounts to the effect that the calamity ",70 directly due to the orders of the commander a t. woo. fleet. As to the motives which impelled'a so thoroughly experienced as was Anmnut to initiate a manceuvre which was regard every one else in the fleet as practicsll37:7; possible of execution, even the searching laclulnrie of the Court Martial have given no further mation. As far as can be judged from the medico- testimony, the ADMIRAL was not suffering °to any ailment, either physical or mental,which account for a temporary aberration of intelleot vi,. the same time it was to be observed that 807 te to deuce which was injurious to the memoryof this 141.0 • commander was given-by the surviving officers yaw the greatest reluctance. The nearest approach .ta censure which the verdict of the Court Marti* . contains is in its allusions to ADMIRAL MAItICI° it: the second in command of the fleet, and ville'wo will he remembered,was on board the Caroperacce-• which" rammed" the Victoria. The Cotni ot" grots that ADMIRAL MARKEIAM did not o8?"11, °the. his first intention to ask for an explanation fatal signal, but at the same time it considers t" it would be fatal to the best interests of the SLD vice to say that ho was to blame for carryillg t the directions of the Commander-in-Chief, Preild in person. With such a conclusion Ivo , think that most people will agree. To his'-e- -subordinate for not sitting in judgment tlPOlrje orders of his superior would be strangely at ,v; g- ame with the "iron discipline," which lies 114`1- to prevailed in the Navy.-</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>1 ~0' ,„: . . , : , ~,</p>
