        <p>At RUNCORN CASES AT CHESTER ASSIZES. Rro, the Chester Assizes on Monday, George Bond 09). Frodsham, was bound over in his own recognis- s f ZlO to come up for judgment when called upon. %Int. Burke Wood prosecuted.—John Hilyer (39), boat- the ,'llleaded guilty to an indictment charging him with le ft el two pairs of boats at Runcorn, on July 15. ktvas sent to gaol for four months.—Horse stealing tqwoll.—John Hulse (38), boatman, pleaded guilty to Ing a horse at Weston, on June 18, and was sent Irlson for nine months. 4e3.•,`'" Vaart your WATCH REPAIRED by skilled workmen 1" to estrcumus's.—Atirt</p>
        <p>JUVENILE THIEVES AT WIDNES. Ceo boys, named Thomas Conroy (9) and John i'lar:l (ID), Caroline-street, and James M*Cann (11), Or, ~IRret-street, pleaded guilty at Widnes Police Court i°ndaY, to stealing three pigeons, value 23., from a .1,c,1n Caroline-street belonging to James Broadhurst. 5i0:44 and John Conroy had been twice previously inter:,cted for theft, and Thomas Conroy once. • Super- t eighley said both Conroys received six strokes ;Flom" "ia`ch rod for stealing coal. The chairman said Conroy would have to receive three strokes and °itch'rodx. noy and M`Cann six strokes respectively of the SUNDAY SCHOOL TREATS AT WIDNES. We I: anltUal treat in connection with the Lacey-street h ,` ,4_14 Sunday School was held on Saturday afternoon .4i fi 'fie 4 scholars' headed by tha Widnes Subscription 16: raarched to s. field in Lowerhouse-lane,kindly lent occasion, by Mr, Kelsall. Aftea tea, games, kn. hipiilndulged in.—The scholars attending Deacon-road St Sunday School held their annual tread on Satur. afternoon in a field placed at their disposal by Mr. '•aPpleton. A good tea and the customary sports %AD'S have now stood the test for fifty years, and fit-class workmanship are still guaranteed,—</p>
        <p>hitext,</p>
        <p>WASTING WATER AT RUNCORN. incorn Petty Sessions on Monday, David Watkin, Church-street, Runcorn, was summoned at the of the Runcorn, Western, and Halton Water- -ottipany, for wastinz their water. It was shown n tap was kept running on Saturday, Sunday, %rt of Monday. Defendant had previously been as to wasting water. A large quantity had )wed to run away.—Defendant's manager tho offence, and said it was due to the care- of one of the nien.—A. fine of 103. and costs 4Pneed. g</p>
        <p>areaway's, 51., High-street, Runcorn, f•r the 'best (its WATCH a workingman ca* have.—Advt. /MIELE FALL FROM A CHIMNEY AT WESTON. )nday morning, shortly after 11 o'clock, a young 'Med Frederick Allman, of Weston, was engaged chimney belonging to the Weston Chemical ,testing it, and was seated in a sort of chair, led by a rope at a height of about 10 feet. From ',explained cause the rope broke, and he fell to Ind, alighting on his shoulder. .He was subse- Carefully examined by Dr. Brunton, assistant to who found him suffering from bruises shock. He was not, however, considered to 31y injured. In want Of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WEDDING In. 4 PitHscsr go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-st.—Advt. LAUNCH OF A FLAT AT RUNCORN. . on Saturday, a flat intended for the carrying ,cween Liverpool and Manchester, and Liverpool Helens, was launched at the yard of Mr.R.Abel, 1. Its dimensions are 70ft. long, 14ft. Sin. and 61t.e1n. deep ; and its carrying capacity 4. The vessel is constructed of English oak ""Dine, and has been built and fitted up by Mr. .i.tubbs, to the satisfactton of the owner. Mr. he launch was accomplished in an expeditious N 4,1 messlike manner. The christening ceremony ')°llt4cefully performed by Mrs W H S. Taylor. of C (40. Pt) who named the vessel the Albatross, of Run.</p>
