        <p>PROMOTION FOR A WIDNES SERDEANT.</p>
        <p>P.S. Ross. of Ditton (Widnes), has been promoted to the rank of Inspector and transferred to Bury.</p>
        <p>SLEEPING OUT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>For this offence Mr. J. Brundrit and Mr. P. Speakman at Runcorn Police Court on Thursday committed Elm,- beth Barker to gaol for 14 days. PROFANE LANGUAGE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Rtinc-orn Police Court on Thursday Ellen Coffey, a noted character, was fined L 1 and costs 14s. Bd. for using obscene and profane language in the street.</p>
        <p>WIDNES FREE L.IERARY. During the past muuth 11,834 persona have visited the reading room, being a daily average of 473. The num- ber of bcoke borrowed, during the same period was 4,298. ALEXANDRA THEATRE, WIDNES. _ _</p>
        <p>The Widnes magistrates on Thursday granted Mr.,Tas. Kiddie, the proprietor of this theatre, a renewal of license, on his undertaking to place a protecting rail round the gallery. MILL BROW SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The scholars had their annual treat on Thursday, when 300 were conveyed by special train to Frodsh am, and spent an enjoyable afternoon at Mersey Vievr,Over- ton Hills.</p>
        <p>A TRESPASSER ON THE RAILWAY. , James McCormick,Barnes-st., Widnes, was summoned by the Lt and N.-W. Ry. Co. for trespassing on the line at Widnes, but as he had expresse I regret for so doinv , and undertaken to pay the costs, Mr. Bland, the Coin. pany's solicitor, withdrew the charge.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND TILE COAL STRIKE. The coal strike is likely-to have a serious effect upon the chemical trade of Widnes and Runcorn. To-day the manufacture of caustic soda will be suspended. Other processes may be continued for a fornight or three weeks shod the coal and salt supplies be reasonably main- tained.</p>
