      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ASSAULT. ON'. • WIDNES POLICEMAN.</head>
        <p>ASSAULT. ON'. • WIDNES POLICEMAN.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Petty Sessions, on Thursday, Wm.Kins,illa, of George-st.,was convicted of Assadlting P.C.Messenger on the Ifithinst.,and was fined 10s. and costs. The con- stable was taking the defendant's sistQr to the Police Station, when the defendant came behind him and kicked him severely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>THEFT FROM BOLD BALL.</head>
        <p>THEFT FROM BOLD BALL.</p>
        <p>James Lord, a labourer, was brought up at Widnes Court on Thursday, charged with stealing ilb.of tobacco and seven postage stamps from Bold Hall, the property of Henry Young. The prisoner, who had been eygaged in removing a billiard table from the Hall, said he had yielded to a sudden temptation. He was fined 7.05., or seven days. THE OPERATIFE BAKERS OF WIDNES AND THEIR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MASTERS.</head>
        <p>MASTERS.</p>
        <p>Nearly three months ago the operative bakers of Wid- nes demanded from their masters fewer hours, and better scale of pay. At a meeting on Thursday night, the masters agreed to the men's terms with slight varie- tions, otherwise the men would have gone on strike: LICENSED VICTUALLERS PICNIC FROM WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The members of the Widnes Licensed Victuallers ASsoeiation with their wives, had a picnic to Winder- mere on Thursday. To the number of 150 they left Wid- nesat 7.15 by special train and arrived at the destination in good time. The weather was charming, and the glorious scenery of the district wits seen at its best in drives to Grasmere and Rydal Mount. During the day the party were well catered for at the Queen's Hotel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>DEATH FRODI SCALDS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DEATH FRODI SCALDS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>• On Wednesday evening, about six o'clock, Marc Col- man, agedl7 months, daughter ef Bridget Coltnan, Back Mersey-st., succumbed to injuries received from scalds on Saturday afternoon through the upsetting of a cup of tea, the contents falling upon the baby's arm. Lime- water and /lour were used, and evidently the child was doing well until 'Wednesday. Then it began to have fits, and Dr. Mangan was called in. Death was due tst shock to the system. The Coroner held an inquest yes- terday,and a verdict of accidental death was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RUNCORN FREEIdASONS' PICNIC.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREEIdASONS' PICNIC.</p>
        <p>The annual installation picnic in connection with the Ellesmere Lodge of Freemasons, Runcorn, took place on Thursday. About fifty brethren and their wives tra- velled to Ethyl in special coaches on the 9.14 a.m. train, and as the weather was most favourable ,for outdoor enjoyment, the party spent an exceedingli pleasant day at the fashionable watering place. St. Asaph Cathedral and the marble church were visited. The visitors were well catered for at the Westminster Hotel, where the proprietor served a high class dinner and tea. Bro. Burgess W.M., presided, and appropriate toasts were honoured. The return journey was accomplished soon after ten o'clock. WIDNES COUNTY COURT. .</p>
        <p>At Widnes County C6urt, yesterday, before his Honour Judge Shand, Robert Booth, tailor, Victoria- road (represented by Mr. F.J.Poole) sued Thos. Finegan, of Ditto!' .(formerly of the Boathouse Inn, Runcorn), re- presented by Mr. Lynskey,for £ll 55., being a quarter's rent for the premises 72, Victoria-road. Verdict for the defendant, with costs.—Eli Sterne, of Liverpool,claimed value of an umbrella, from Elizabeth Rowe, licensee. of the Child of Hale public-house Hale. Mr. Lewin defended. It was alleged that the umbrella was pur- loined from a trap while under the care of Mrs. Rowe's servants. His Honour held the defendant liable, and gave a verdict accordingly.—A married woman named Sarah Gallagher, of Liverpool, sued her brother Michael Jennings, Woolton, for £l3 14s. Bd., alleged to be wages due for services as housekeeper. Verdict for £3 103. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>" LT HONE" AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>" LT HONE" AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A very pleasant social function took place at Runcorn on Thursday. Mrs. and Miss Pierpoint of Halton-road, had invited ,a large number of ladies and gentlemen to afternoon tea at Bethesda schoolroom, and there was, a very numerous response. The room had been charm- ingly decorated, and the guests had an opportunity of viewing a number ofpaintings and pencil sketches from the easels of Miss Pierpoint's many pupils. '1 here were I not wanting evidences of talent on the part of the artists and of careful and intelligent training on the part of the teacher. There was also on view a fine collection ot old and rare china, lent by Mrs. Deaken, of Simm's Cross, Widnes. The pleasure of the company was enhanced hy Mr. BantoCk Pierpoint, (who has earned renown as a powerful and finished vocalist in London and the provinces) singing "The Queen of the Earth," "Simon the Cellarer;" " The Mill Wheel," and " The Bedonin's love song." Mr. I3irket Musgrove was the accompanist. Miss Etty Soulby performed on the piano a couple of Listz compositions. The proceedings throughout were of the most enjoyable description. MARRIAGE Or MISS F. WRIGHT AT RUNCORN. On Tuesday afternoon the marriage between Mr. William Epps, son of Mr. Thomas Epps, of Canterbury, and Miss Fanny Wright, third daughter of Mr. Robert Wright, of le, Oreenway-road, Runcorn, was solemnized at the Parish Church, the officiating clergyman being the Rev. J. Holden. The ceremony was witnessed by a large number of friends and well-wishers. The brides- maids were Miss Eliza Doming Wright and Miss Susie Wright, sisters of the bride, and the groomsmen were Mr. R. Weiss-Mfiller and Mr. Harry Wright. The bride was given away by her father. On leaving the church the happy pair were the recipients of numerous hearty congratulations, and they were Nell besprinkled with rice. The bridal party subsequently partook of break- fast at the residence of Mr. Wright, after which the newly married couple left for London en route for Kent. A num her crf handsome and costly presents were received The bridegroom for two years has been the headmaster , of the Weston Wesleyan Day School, and during his stay in the neighbourhood has gained many friends. He has ably fulfilled the duties of secretary to the local branch of the St. John Ambulance Association. The bride's family is well-known throughout the town and district, her father for many years having been the superintendent of the Runcorn Fire Brigade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RUNCORN'fi POOR CHILDREN IN THE COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'fi POOR CHILDREN IN THE COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>The Committee having charge of the aged person's treat gave the poor children of Runcorn a day in the country on Wednesday. This is the second occasion, and as the treat confers so much enilyment upon little ones whose ordinary lives are not of the brightest, it is to be .hoped this country otiting, will become an annual insti- tution. Over five hundred travelled by special train Irons Runcorn at one O'clock, and -an Frodsham being reached, they were formed in pitocessinnal order and marched, headed bv the Ragged School Fife and Drum Band to Mersey View, Overton Hills. Here they were at once -grouped and photographed by Mr. Brown, of Waterloo-road. The afternoon was spent in a large variety of games for which provision had been made and there were numerous ladies and gentlemen present, who lent their aid in amusing the young ones. The weather was beautifully fine. At four o'clock tea was provided by Mrs. Parker: and their appetites whetted by the pure air and romp,the young people had an excellent meal. At the conclusion they gave hearty cheers for the kindness extended to them.- Afterwards, at the invitation of Mr. S. Taylor, chairman of Runcorn Commissioners, the ladies arid gentlemen who had assisted in Om treat, par- took of a capital tea. Sir. W. M. Sowerhy, on behalf of the Committee, expressed the thanks due to Mr. Taylor for his hospitality. At the same time they congratulated him upon having attained the position of Chairman of the Commissioners for the second time, and they sincerely ti usted hi year of office would be prosperous and unclouded. Thanks were also due to those who had forwarded sub,eriptions.—The Rev. J. Holden seconded he motion, which was supported by the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi and the Rev. J. Hanby, all referr- ing to Mr Tim. lor in terms of eulogy.—The resolutions were unanininusly carried.—ln responding. Mr. Taylor said he had been one of the happiest those who had been able to participate in the treat that day, It was a pleasure to demonstrate to poor people, that they were not forgotton M their trials and sufferings, and those who had subscribed and worked for a treat were amply repaid by the smiling fartes of the boys and girls, fie trusted-that the event was now 'an established one and that annually the children would be taken for a day in the country. He felt, certain that any future chairman of the Commissioners who proposed to let it drop Would have a warm time of it. (Applause.)— Before leaving the children Were each presented with a bun. Runcorn was safely reached at 8 30, and the children marched in procession to the Market Hall, where after singing the National Anthem ani giv- ing three cheers for their benefactors Ml' diTerse3.</p>
        <p>NEW WE:tLEYAN MINIgTERS At VARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Accort-li)ig to the second draft of the Stationing Committee of the Wesleyan Conference held at Cardiff, the Rev. G. H. Carnson of Gloucester, who was expected to succeed the Rev. A Freeman as econd minister in the Warrington Wesleyan Cir- cuit, has been appointed to Redruth in Cornwall. The Rer. Owen Watkins has been transferred' from Reelriith to Warrington. The minister to succeed the Rev, 'P. Ross will be the Rev. G. E. -Mason, of Manitield. 21. TO Sa. IN THE B. OFF, on all cash orders at R. W. MARSH'S, Furniture Warehouse, Bridge-Foot, Warrington. -See tuner advt., page 4. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JULY 20. 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JULY 20. 1893.</p>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING TOWN COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Mk,'ETING.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held in the Town Hall, on Tuesday, when amongst other business, the following will be con- sidered.</p>
        <p>NEW BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee proposes that the plans of the following buildings, Am., be .approved : House in Folly-lane,for Mr. James Knowles ; 15 cottages in Margaret-st., for Mr. Lees ; 18 cottages in New-st.,off School-brow,for Mr. Stephen Dale.</p>
        <p>TIE URINALS AGAIN. The sanitary Committee again recommend that the public urinal in 'i3uttermarket-st. be removed.</p>
        <p>THE MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>-The Museum Committee recommends that applicat'on be made to the County Council for a grant of £Bl5 for the year 1893-4. It also reports the following gifts : Deatlishead M.th, taken at Sankey, from Mr. L. Green- ing; Eggs (2) of grey parrot. from Mr. J. Woodhouse. Deathshead Moth, taken in Warrington, from Mr.' Sharples ; " Geoeraphy f Ptolemy elucidated," by Mr. T. G. Rylands, F.S.A. ; from the Author; "Year Book of Learned Societies," from the Literary and Philosophi- cal Society ; "California," by Royce, from Mr. H. Whit- ham; "'lime Tests and Sight Tests," by W. S. Woods; from the Author ; Parliamentary Papers 18 vols., from Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P. ; " Lectures on Papers, Plumb- ing" by Mr. R. J. Angel, from the Author.—The follow- ing loan is reported :—Four bronze implements found at Winmarleigh, from the lion. Miss Wilson-Patten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL WARRINGTON .STUDENT.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL WARRINGTON .STUDENT.</p>
        <p>Among those who received prizes at the recent diStributiou by the Lord Mayor of London, at St. Francis Xaviers College, Liverpool, was Mr. Joseph Dolan, sou Of Mr: Joseph ,Dolan, Church-street, Warrington. This student is. in the fourth form. Ile was first ii good conduct. Accessits, Classical Course, and obtained a certificate of the highest. distinction in that course. lie gained a £4 " Rob Hoy "scholarship and was first for final examination certificate. lie also secured a certificate in Chemistry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FIRE AT AlooßE.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT AlooßE.</p>
        <p>About four o'clock on Saturday afternoon a fire WiiB discovered to be raging in a corrugated iron laulding containing hay and straw at the Crow's Farm, Moore. The Warrington Fire Brigade were called out, but their services were not required on arrival, as the Wilderspool Brigade had per- formed all the work required. The fire when dis- covered had secured a firm hold of the hay and straw, and all efforts to extinguish the flames were unitvailing. For, some time, however, tite farm- house was threatened by the conflagration, and the firemen directed their attentien towards prevent- ing the ignition of the house. The fire burnt itself out towards five o'clock. It is estimated that the damage is about.t2oo.</p>
        <p>DIRTICTS and I.dranittnas made and fitted free of charge dm Mg sale at J. and W. DUTTON'S, Sankey-street, Warring- _</p>
        <p>BURTON WOOD SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting or this Board was held in the class room of the Board Echool, Burton- wood, at 7.0 p.m. on Tuesday evening', when there were. pr- sent. Messrs. ,John Warburton (chairman), Thomas Perks. William Fairclough, John Porster. (attendance officer) 11. Oawthorp (school master), William Webster (clerk). The report of drawing examination, slowing that the bobs had passed a fair examination was read, also was Her Majesty's InsPector's report of the annual examination, from which it appeared that there had been an increase in the grant of £35 2s. sa. over the amount earned last year. The Board resolved to defer considera- tion of the report till next meeting. Miss Eliza Perrin was engaged as monitress. LAWN MOWERS.—GARLA•D, Ironmonger, Warrington and Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WARRINGTON SHIRE HORSE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SHIRE HORSE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>It has been decided to hold the forthcoming mare and foal show promoted by the Warrington Shire Horse Society in Walton Park, by the kind per- mission of Sir Gilbert Greenall, in September. One of the main objects of the Societ:v is to improve the breed of liOrses,in this locality. The officials of the society are : President, Mr. W. C. Jones ; vice7pre- sideni, Mr. Edward Greenall (Mayor) ; hen. secre- tary, Mr. Colin Mclver ; hon. treasurer, Mr. Joseph Knowles, while the committee consists of the fol- io% ing gentlemen : Messrs. Richard Ainscough, Harold Broadbent, William Bradford, A. Broome, T. Charnock, William Derbyshire, J. E. Davies, Gilbert Greenall, Cyril Greenall, H. Greenough, John Hal- sail, W. B. Longton, John Marsh, .Charles Mawson, Henry Mawdsley, Henry Milling, Syl. Reynolds, Joseph Shakeshaft. and Win. Whitlow. Prizes are offered in 23 classes, including a champion cup value £lO for the best shire mare, and many other prizes are given, including prizes for the best turn- outs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>EXCURSION TO OAKMERE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the botanical contingent of the Fii4d Club, journeyed to Oakmere, via Hartford, in search of rare plants which have long been known to grow there. Mr. Billups was the leader, and a very successful day's botanizing resulted in the following and many other interesting species being found. deyeuxia negleeta, a grass which grows nowhere else in England, and exists . only in small quantity at Oakmere ; carex rostrata, var. involuta, a sedge pecu- liar to Lancashire and Cheshire, and having its head- quarters at Oakmere. Carex filifornius, phynehospora alba, Drosera rot undundifolia d. intermedia, the round and long leayed sundews, the former of which used to be pleatiful on Money Cammon before the Ship Canal operations dried up the locality. The latter was new to most of the members, although it was once cernmon on all our peat mosses. Andromeda polifolia which occurs on Itixton Moss, but is new to the club as a Cheshire plant. Lastraea cristate (1 hare-crested shield farm), manyanthes trifoliata, littorella lacustris, and aretium intermedium were also found. The return to Hartford was by A botto Moss, where utrieularia minor, the small bladderwort was found in flower,, and through Kennel Wood to Sandiway. The pleasures ot this botanical ramble were enhanced by the extreme beauty of the forest scenery and a perfect day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>INSPECTION OF WIDNES CORPORATION PROPERTY.</head>
        <p>INSPECTION OF WIDNES CORPORATION PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>The first inspection by the members of Widnee Town Council took place on Tuesday. A start was made from the Town Hall at 1030 a.m., in two con- veyances supplied by Mr. J. H. Lee. The accident hospital, which is under the control of Miss War- burton, was found the picture of bri4htness and cleanliness. There were six patients under treat- ment. The surveyor (Mr. Sinclair) explained the improvements he was affecting at the Promenade. The river wall and the ferry slope are nearly completed. The visit to the gas works as usual was full of interest. For the benefit of the new members Mr. Carr, the engineer described the process of making gas, explaining the use and mode or working of the various plant. The retort house, was made the object of a careful inspection, and the general opinion was that it was a capital place for the class of work that had to be performed there. The annual consumption of gas is 150 millions cubic feet. There were about a thousand tons of coal in stock, suffi- cient said Mr. Carr to last a month, but should the chemical works stop through the strike much less gas would be needed and the coal would be sufficient for six or seven weeks. En route to Ditton, a visit was paid to the piece of land, five acres in extent, off Moor- lane, which the Corporation are endeavouring to pur- chase for the purpose of a nightsoil depot. The locality appeared a very suitable one. At Ditton Mr. John ' Taylor, contractor, is rapidly proceeding with the work of c7;nstructing the bank for the new gas holder, and it will soon be completed. The new holder is being con- structed by Messes. R. and J. Dempsey,of Newton Heath Manchester. It is Gadd and Mason's patent colurnnless holder, and it, is composed of three lifts. It has a diameter of 115 feet, and a height of 82 feet, and has a storing capacity of 70,000 cubic feet. The present holder has a capacity of 400.000 cubic feet. It is intended to dem( Ugh the two small holders at the gas wo. Ire 113 ause the space is needed ter the ex,ei sit.* arl development- of time works. At the ca'ural rant: i mucrease, the Corporation will have sufficient stoma ze capacity to last for the next ten or 12 years. From Ditton, the members were driven to the Infectious Diseases Hospital, in Warrington-road. Miss Millburn and her assistants had twelve patients suffering from typhoid fever under their care. The garden was blight with foliage and blow, and the Institution ap- peared to be carried on with the greatest efficiency. Pex Hill was next visited and lunalm partaken of. The re- servoirs inspected, the journey was continued to Stocks- well, wtiere, however, there was nothing new to report. The old engines Virginia and Mary, were standing idle ; and it was stated that having served their day, their ultimate destination will be the scrap hcap. A pleasant drive brought the party to Netherlev, where the new engine which had been previously described in these columns was a source of much iuquiying interest. It was working in splendid order, making over 13 strokes per minute. and sending up to Vex Hill at every stroke 142 gallons. The engine pumps n million gallons per day which is about half a million gallons more than the makers Messrs. llaythorn, Davy and Co:,contracted that. the engine should pump. The daily consumption of the town is 4. million gallons, so that this new engine, the Benjamin Brown.is capable of supplying more than half the daily needs. The engineer informed the members that the Corporation had practically an unlimited supply if water. At Belle Vale it was found, that notwithstand- ing the increased pumping at Netherley, the spring sent forth its customary copious streams of water.. By this time it was nearly half-past five, and a brief drive brought time party to Clmildwall Abbey Hotel, where tea was provided. Afterwards Councillor G. 1. Neil who presided, expressed Ins gratification at finding the town's property in such an efficient condition, and he remarked that they owed much to the far seeing judg- ment of their predecessors on the governing body of the town. He complimented the officials upon their able services, especially mentioning Mr. Carr, their talented engineer.—Mr. Carr said he had had a great deal of adverse criticism from his brother engineers with regard to the new engine at Netherley, and its success was the more gratifying. Netherlev was the only place where water was pumped direct from the bore holes, and it ' was to the credit of the Widnes Corporation that they were the pioneers in such a novel and successful and cheap 1 method.—Councillor Christie expressed his pleasure at having harl the opportunity of inspecting the valuable propsrty of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>TIIE CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>TIIE CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Registrar reports to the Cemetery Committee that the interments for the past four weeks were—Adults, 42 ; minors, 161 ; stillborn and premature, 12. Corres- ponding period last year—Adults, 28 ; minors, 35, still- born aad premature 12.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>BATES.</head>
        <p>THE BATES.</p>
        <p>The report of the bathkeeper to the Town Hall, &amp;c., Conumittee, states that the income from the bathe for the past four weeks was £BO Bs. 2d., and for the corres- ponding period last psir £2l 9s.</p>
        <p>. _ THE MARKETS. • The Markets Committee reports that the total income from all sources for the past four weeks was £342 17s. 11d. ; corresponding period last year,£392 195.11 d. ; decrease, £5O 2. It also recommends that a suitable receptable be provided in each in,irket for which stall- holders may depoeit any food which 'has become unfit for human consumption.</p>
        <p>THE GAS.</p>
        <p>The Gas Gras.Committee reports that the average illumina- ting power of the gas for the past three weeks was equal II) 18•80 candles at both works. , TUE CORPORATION, AND' THE CANAL. The 'Myers Committee reports that, the chairman IC- potted that in company with Mr. 'Councillor Boltori and ! the Borough Surveyor, he had warted upon the engineer to the Manchester Ship Canal Company with reference to the construction of the lock and bluices at Waken, and also the dredging of the river.—Mr. Hunter, the deputy engineer, stated that the contract for the lock and sluice was let to a contractor, who was proceeding with the work, that the dredging of the river was to be commenced On Mon,?.ay, the 24th inst., and that no time would be lost in completing the works. According to the amended plan .0 tracing of which has since been sent to : the Borough Surveyor, it is proposed to construct the lock at Walton 150 feet long and 30 feet wide,its position being on the north side of the rive4nearly opposite the cutting forming the entrance from the canal to the Warrington dock. It is also proppsed to construct the lock on land forming part of Arpley meadows, and to widen the river at the point 150 feet, to enable the work to be carried on without interfering with traffic on the river. This is considered an improvement upon the plan previously proposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>TENDERS.</head>
        <p>TENDERS.</p>
        <p>Sanitary Committee: The following tenders have been accepted by the committees named. Of Messrs. Heenan and Freud° to supply a " Heenan " Patent Force Draught Fan, at £53, delivered at Longford Depot. Cemetery I Committee : Of Mr. E. W. Gittins for 200 yards of 7 be 3 York stone delivered at the Cemetery at 24. per yard. Town flail, &amp;:., Committee : Of Messrs. Clare and Ridgway to supply 4,000 tiles for the ladies bath at ss. 11d- per superficial yard. Gas Committee : Of Messrs. Edward Heaton and Son, of Manchester, to repair No. 2 boiler in Mersey-street, at £37, and of the Abram Coal Company, Limited, for 2,000 tons rough Arley slack de livered at -Longford Works. also that the following ten- ders for coal and cannel be accepted :—Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Limited, 5,000 cons of coal ; -Cross, Tetley and Co. Limited, 10,000 tons ; Padeswood Coal and Cannel C 0.,. 2,000 tons of cannel. Water Committee : Of Mr. William Taylor to point the brickwork of the two hou.es at the VVinwick Pumping Station at £2B be accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>COST OF STREET-MAKING.</head>
        <p>THE COST OF STREET-MAKING.</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage Committee recommends that the following final apportionment of the cost of completing the private street works in the following streets, as ascertained and divided by the Surveyor be approved. Leonard-st. :-Messrs. Kay and Cowden,. £37 23. ad. • Wm. Bostock, per agents-Messrs. Percy Davies and 'Forshaw, ,£lll N. 7d. ; John Platt, £124 10s. 2d. ; Mrs. Tabitha Sharratt, £47 193. 64. ; John Appleton, £55 10s. Bd. ; Thomas Robinson Ford, £4l N. 4d. ; Mrs. Mary Anna Crompton, £37 103. Bd. ; Thos. Ashley, £37 15s. 6J.; Jabez Boardman, £l9 14s. ; John Newell. £l9 183.; George Boardman, £4O 63. 7d. ; total, £572 17s. 84. Porshaw•st. :-John Appleton,£3os 103. 8d ; John Miller.£s3 83.2 d. ; George Shakeshaft, £2B 108.10 d. ' • Samuel Blythe, £34 173. 5d.; Charles Edward Bromley, £35 Os. 26. : James Mllls, £2B 63. 4d. ; George Boardman. £4B 13s. 10d. ; Thomas Booth, £94 N. 44.; Charles Lawton,£9o 17s. 7d. ; Henry Whitehouse,£37 108.4 d.; t0ta1,:£756 153.8 d. New Woad : The Warrington Permanent Benefit Building Society per Secretary, Mr. Robert Proud, £l4l is. 4d. ; John Lunt, £97 19s. 64. ;. John Austin, 1E36 N. 6d.; John Platt, £2 123 24. ; Henry 'Fowler Watt, £Bl 163. 84. ; Peter. Worthington, £lB2 18s. 24. ; total, £542 Bs. 4d. -Robson-street: William Bolton, £5O 33. 9d.; Alfred Marsh, £5B Os. 4d. ; William Whittaker, £7l 123. 84. ; John Rigby, £3l ss. 6d. ; John Edward Wright, £95 133. 114. ; Sarah Jolley, £3O is. 11d. ; executors of the late Thos. Porter-per John E. Davies,£so 3s. 9d.; total, £385 Is. 104. THIS ROYAL WEDCIRG..•</p>
        <p>With regard to the addresses to Rvalty voted by the Corporation in connection with the recent marriage the Home Secretary, Mr. Asquith,writes " that Her Majesty was pleased to receive the same very graciously." Sir Francis Knollys on behalf of the Prince of Wales thanks the Corporation for their kind congratulations • and T. R. H. the Duke and Duchess of York, throng); Major Oeneral F. De Winton, convey their warmest thanks for the" kind address of congratulations and good wishes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>F RODSiLA M.</head>
        <p>F RODSiLA M.</p>
        <p>PRUi nr Fyn CHAPEL. —The annual sermons were Preached nn Sunday, in the Primitive Chapel, Prod- sham Bridge, by Mr. J. Whitley, of Stockton Beath. The services -vere well attended.</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH PARADE.—On Sunday morning' the officers and members of the 2nd (E lad of Chester's) V. 13. Cheshire itstriment, " I" Cpmpany. attended th:, Parish Church for service, when the Rev. Mr. Bennett preached. The company was under the command of Capti.in H. Harrison.</p>
        <p>TREAT TO SUNDAY SCHOOL SCHOLARS.— On Wednesday through the kindness and generosity. of the Misses Cros- field, ef Enfield House, Frodsham the Children teachers, and officers of the Sunday school, with which the ladies are so closely associated, were provided with an enjoy- able excursion to Rhyl. In the afternoon they partook of a sui-stantial meal in the 'town Hall, the caterer being Mr. W. Clarke. of Church-st., Frodsham. During their stay the children were provided with various amusements. Home was safely reached soon after nine o'clock. TUE PARAFFIN OUTRAGE. • AtKuutsford Assizes on Tuesday, George Jackson surrendered to a charge of throwing paraffin at his aife Eden Jackson, at Frodsham on the 20th May. For the prosecution, Mr. Colt " Williams stated that the parties resided together, and whilst under the influence of drink quarrelled in the house of a man and Mrs. Darling-ten. with whom they lodged at Frocishain. In the course of the row it was alleged that the prisoner snatched a lighted paraffin lamp from the mantlepiece and threw it at his wife. Her clothing caught fire, and also the wall paper of the latitise. The names of the woman were only extinguished ,by a neighbour .wrapping an Article of clothing round her. Mrs. Darlington gave eyidenca in support of this statement. The prisoner's wifestated that the lamp was knocked over accidentally in a scaffie whilst the daughter also gave evidence to the same effect. The jury returned a verdict of "...l.Vot and the prisoner, acting on the advice of,Mr. E. fl. Lloyd, who was for the defence, pleaded guilty to com- mon assault. His Lordship remarked that the jury had exercised a wise discretion ln returning a verdict of " Nor guilty "on the graver charge. Alluding to the increase of cases of assaults by throwing lighted paraffin lamps,he said it was an offenco Unknown seven or eight years ago. He could not possibly look lightly en such fi dangerous form of assault. The prisoner must be ini- prhained :Ind kept to hard labour for six months, and at the expiration of the term must enter into his 11W11 recognisance of £25 to keep the peace for twelve months.</p>
        <p>• THE PETTY SESSIONS. At these Sessions, on Wednesday, temporary autho- rity was granted to Peter HugheS„ to sell at che Railway Dunham ; and to Joseph Faulkner, at the Weaver Tavern, Prodsham Bridge, until the . annual 'licensing. sessi,m.—Hannah Atkinson,. Booth.st., Fredsliam, so as summoned for assaulting Mary Clarke, a neighbour, on the 21st inst. A cross summons was taken out by Mrs. • Atkinson against Mary Clarke,Martha Hopley,and Mary Ellen Clarke, for assaulting her at the same time and place. Mr. Arthur Linaker repreiented the Clarkes and Hopley. The Betich distni,veci the ease aeainst Hannah Atkinson, and imposed a tine of 103. and 12s. 6d. costs on Mary Clarke, and ss. and 9. 6d.costs each on Martha Hopley and Mary Filen Clarke. In default of payment they would have to go to gaol ter 14 days.—Henry of Helshy, was summoned at the instance of Win. Davies, farmer, Helsby, for doing damage to growing grass, to the amnunt of is, on the 18th inst. Mr. Lina-. ker, who represented defendanP, denied the offence. There being a doubt in the case it was dis missed.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A WARRINGTON '1 N ON OGENARIAN.</p>
        <p>CUTTING TEETH AT NINETY-THREE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Adam Campbell, Brick-street, Warrington, died late on Wednesday night at the advanced age of ninety-three. Deceased was a native of County' Down, Ireland, but has resided in this lolality for the past sixty years. Up. to within a few days of his death he was able to move 'about freely in course of his duties as a coal agent. He retained almost complete mastery of his lacultias until the end, it being a particular boast of his that -he could easily read the ordinary small type of a news- paper without the aid of spectacles. Even en Saturday last he shaved himself unaided. He was a staunch Presbyterian and Conservative, although his father took part in the rebellion of 179 S against the Government. A remarkable instance occurred a fortnight ago, which is well worthy of note. Mr. Campbell apparently had premonition t hal his end was near, and in proof of that confided his reasons for thinking so to an intimate friend, whom he I asked to feel his upper gum. This was done, and the phenomenon of two new teeth cut through the' gum in place of two which had been knocked out thirty years ago was findeniabiy plain. These,' Mr. Campbell said, Were for his death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WARRINGTON CLERGY DAL) GHTE RS' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON CLERGY DAL) GHTE RS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The second of the sales of work in connection with the Warrington Clergy Daughters' School will be held on Thursday in the school, to wipe off the debt which has been upon the school during the past few years. The school was, we may mention, built about fifty years ago, ,by *the Rev. Horace Po wys,the then Rector of Warrington. They were for the-daughters of clergymen in the three dioceses of Chester, Liverpool, and Manchester,which comprise the archdeaconries of Cliesi;er, Macclesfield, Man- chester, Blackburn, Li verpool.and Warrington. The charge for admission is very *moderate, so that clergymen with limited incomes could afford to give their daughters the advantages of a good education. There were also places for orphans, who were admitted at the charge of £2O a year. Those who were not orphans were charged £25, and for those out of their dioceses a charge of £45 was made,and some years past a debt has gradually risen and at present amounts to over £l,OOO. This is largely due to the numerous deaths of the benefactors of the place, such as Mr. Tinsley, Mr. Wm. Sharpe, and others. During the past three years quite a large number of subscribers have died, and Unfortunately, fresh ones have not been found in their places. Tha expenses, of course, have kept the same,so that the income has become less than the expenditure. The public also do not appear to appreciate the good work done by the institution, and their subscriptions have not been very numerous. The authorities at length decided to make a determined effort to remove the debt that has accumulated, and the ideas of holding sales of work at Chester, Warrington, Manchester and Liver- pool, was adopted. Accordingly the first sale of work was held on luesday at the Bishop's Palace in Chester, when Lady Lettice Grosvenor, performed the ceremony of opening the proceedings. The second one will be held at Warrington on Tharsday, Friday, and Saturday next, and it will be opened by Mr. IL D. Gilmour. The re- maining two sales of work at Manchester and Liverpool have not yet been arranged. Thu committee are wish- ful to raise £1,00) at these sales of work, and it is hoped that the public will sh Aw their appreciation of the good work done by this institution by doing their best during, the three sales when the sale will be held. In addition to the Sale a number of pleasant attractions are being provided, including an operetta by the school girls, tableux vivants, and a dramatic performance, and musi- cal performancea given by Mr. R. H. Pearson's string band, and the Volunteer band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NEW HOTELS. PRoIPOSW EXIENsION OF THE CROWN AND SCEPTRE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW HOTELS. PRoIPOSW EXIENsION OF THE CROWN AND SCEPTRE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Saturday. before Alderinan Holmes (in the chair), Alderman Chandley, Messrs. F. Monks, J. Burgess, and Captain Ridgway, Mr. Robert Davies, who represented Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Co.. said he appeared that day to announce that the two new houses for which provisional licenses were granted at the last licensing sessions, had now been completed to the satisfaction of the Borough Sur- veyor on behalf of the borough huthorities, and to the satisfaction of Mr. Hunt on behalf of the magistrates. Mr. Owen would tell them that these houses had been built according to the plans sanc- tioned by the magistrates, in every particular. He . would ask their worships to confirm these licenses, so that they could go before the excise authorities for per- niission to sell, and commence business on Monday, when the old houses would be closed. He would remind : Mr. Hunt that the old houses although licensed until the next licensing sessions, were not to be used for excise purposes, with the exception of the Currier's Arms, which would be added to the Crown and Scep- tre, in order to make a decent and commodious house. The houses to be given for the Imperial Hotel, in Catherine-street, off Bewsey-road, were the Bear's Paw, the Black Horse in Turner-street, and the Olive. Branch in Oliver-street. The houses to be given in return for the Albion Hotel in Battersby-lane were the Fox Inn, the eurriers Arms, and the White Bear in Buttermarket- street.</p>
        <p>Mr. Longland, solicitor, infsrmed the Bench that he appeared on behalf of the Temperance Federation Com- mittee who opposed these licenses last year. They thought that it would only be respectful to the Bench that they should be represented that day in consequenee of the opposition which they raised. Mr. Wm. Owen, architect, stated that the plans for the two hotels had been complied with and carried out exactly as had been sanctioned by the magistrates. Mr. Longland asked when the old houses Would h, closed, to which Mr. Davies replied that if the exise licenses were obtained the new hotels would be opened on Monday and the other houses would not be opened after 10 o'clock on Sunday night. In answer to another question from Mr. Lincland whether it was proposed to sell drink at the Curriers Arms, Mr. Daves said business would not be carried on there, until the alter- ations were made.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hunt asked if it would he illegal if anyone were found drinking in the Curriers Arms.—Mc. White (magistrates clerk), said it would not be strictly ingal, but would be a breach of the arrangements made with the magistrates and the owners of the houses.—Mr. Davies said he thought he could %lake this statement that no business would he done in that part of the pre- mises, known as the Currier s Arms, until notice had been given to the magistrates. _ _</p>
        <p>The Ecenses were then confirmed. Licenses were afterwards transferred to Mr. Thomas Partridge for the Imperial Hotel ; and that for the Albion, was granted to Mr, John Llewellyn. Alderman Holmes said he had been told that they were going to open these houses by giving free drink. Mr. Longland said he had heard the dame report. Mr. Clarke of the Brewery, said there was no truth in the statement, and Mr. Partridge said he never intended to give " free drink."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TECHNICAL TNsTitUCTION IN LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL TNsTitUCTION IN LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>DISCOURAGING ANNOUNCEMENT. '</p>
        <p>The report trilc submitted to the Lancashire Council by the Technical Instruction Committee at the meet- ing to be held on Thursday next states :—"The County Council. in February last, decided to offer twenty-four scholarships of L'6o per annum each, tenable for three years. Of these fourteen were to he awarded for pro- ficiency in science, lour for proficiency in art, and six for pr,ficiency in commercial S u bjects. The number of candidates who entered were—in science twenty-seven, art twenty-four, and emnmercial subjects fifteen, mak- ing a total of sixty-six. Although the nu.mber of entries was less than might have been expected, the standard obtained by the successful candidates was a high one, and the committee, on the advice of the examiners, is pleased to be able to recommend that all the scholarships should be awarded_ Ten of the sue- cnsful candidates vio.re holders of exhibitions awarded by the County Council in November last. The examina- tions for the 10(1 exhibitions in science, twenty-five for proficiency in art, and twenty-five for Proficiency in commereill subjects were held on the 14th and 15th June, 1893. The entries for these were also disappoint- ing, tbe numbers being—for science forty-four, art ei,thty-eiebt, end commercial subjects forty-seven, mak- ing a total of 179. Eighteen of the successful candid- ' ates have already held exhibitions offered by the County Council. The committee is only able to recommend that twenty-three of the science exhibitions be awarded, but aS the examiners advise that twenty-six of the candidates in the commercial subjects qualified for an I exhibition, the committee begs to recommend that one ot the unawarded science exhibitions be given for those subject v. The committee regrets to have to report that I the entries for the agricultural and horticultural scholar- ' ships and exhibitions were too few to justify the holding of the exanfinations, and they have, accordingly, been• postponect till September. The aelotint expended on technical instruction was : To local authorities, urban, £21.600 ; rural, £6.000 ; in special grants, £9,200 ; iii exhibitions and scholarship, £7,270; in management , expenses, £3,6so—total £48,620.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SMALLPDX IN LANCASEIRE.</head>
        <p>SMALLPDX IN LANCASEIRE.</p>
        <p>The Public Health Committee of the Lancashire County Council report that during the past three month. there were 480 cases of smallpox, With 43 deaths in the county ; in the preceding three months 383 cases and 21 (baths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SUPPER.</head>
        <p>SUPPER.</p>
        <p>ST. JAMES' CHITRCH.-ANNIVERSARY</p>
        <p>Quite 200 persons assembled in St. James' Church Schools, Latchford, on Tuesday night, on the occasion of the anniversary supper of the parish.</p>
        <p>The VICAR (Rev. F. Slater) occupied the chair, and at the conclusion of the supper proposed the toast of "The Church and Queen," :which was duly honoured._</p>
        <p>, Mr. HERBERT WOODS gave "The 'Bishops and clergy of the dioceses of Liverpool and Chester." His ac- quaintance with Bishops had been very slight, but be had always learned to look up to them and respect them as a body of very devout and learned men, alwayfi ready to take.'a leading part in everything that appertained to their sphere of labour. With regard to the clergy, he knew as much concerning their organizations as many people did. Let them take their own parish. Apart from the work that was done in the Church, the day schools, and the Sunday schools—which were tbe foqn- dation of Church life—there were other organizations which went to make up the life of the clergy, such as that of visiting the sick and attendint, to all the require- ments of each individual in tne parish. Then they had the Church Lads' Brigade—(hear, hear)—the Girls' Friendly Society, and others of a like kind, and he thought they ought to be thankful to have at the bead of all these organizations such an energetic and able vicar as the One who now reigned over them. (Ap- plause.) Then if they stepped across to the other side of the water, and went into the parish of Warrington, ! they found what a vast difference there was in. the , Church feeling of Vs arrington from what there was a few" years ago. People now took a greater 'interest in the welfare of the Church. They had now a dozen ready workers to help and back up the clergy, and 15 years ! ago it was a difficult matter to find three or four. He believed this change had come about in Warrington ow- ing to the able and energetic manner in which the Rector fulfilled his duties. (Applause.) The speaker then referred to the ministers of various denoMinations, remarking that they did a great deal of good in regard to social questioes. (Applause). The toast having been duly honoured, The RECTOR (Rev. F. W. Willis),.who was most cordi- ally received, responded to the toast. Referring to the Bishops of Chester and Liverpool, lie said no two men could be greater contrasts in many ways, in age and in site. (Laughter.) He, however, felt the highest respect for them both. lie took that opportunity of offering his sincere congratulations to the parishioners on having a new vicarage. and a possible increase of endowment, which he was sure their Vicar most sincerely deserved. (Applause.) He looked upon the parish of St. James as a representative one, as it taught clearly and distinctly the faith delivered to the saints. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN gave the health of Sir Gil bert G reenall, the patron of the living. Ile observed that they had not the same difficulties to face in the parish as existed 15 , years ago, when he first came to it. There was at one time the serious difficulty of insufficient school accom- modation, but that had been conquered through the kindness of Sir Gilbert Greenall and many others. At : one time be (the speaker) was the only clergyman work- ing in the parish, but now there was a curate. Refer- ' ring to the financial difficulty, he said everybody knew that the parish of St. James was not a rich one. When he came to the parish, 15years ago, the endowment was £9O, and the fees and pew rents brought it up to £3OO. The pew rents were discontinued, and two-thirds of the offertory were assigned to the viear, besides the Christ- mas and Easter offerings. That altogether made up , about £220 a year, and that had been the average in- ' come of the benefice for the last dozen years. Sir Gilbert Greenall had purchased the living, and during the past year lie had acquired for them a piece of land opposite the Vicarage, for which he (the speaker) had for many years paid rent, and had added to it £5OO in cash, lie offered this donation to the Ecclesiastical Commissioners in exchange for a grant, as was their custom. After investigation into the condition of the parish the Commissioners sent him airiest welcome letter saying they would give their highest grant, £6OO. (Ap- plause.) So that by that act of Sir Gilbert Greenall they had had added to the living a piece of land of consider- able value, and £1,200 to be invested, at three per cent., which would bring in £36 a year. Sir Gilbert had also purchased what was known as Mr. Arringstall's house as a vicarage for St. James'. (Applause.) He supposed the old vicarage and land would be sold, and it was probable that in the near future the endowment would he increased by £BO or £7O and they should be able to relieve the offertory. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>. • 'rite toast was honoured with aeciammation. Mr. JOHN Warrn returnei thank 4 behelf of Sir Gilbert Greenall.</p>
        <p>Captain IlurcimrsoN propo.ed "Success to the church and palish or St. It mea." 'Uhe toabt having been honoured, . .</p>
        <p>Mr. BURGESS, criiiirchwarden, responded, and after observing that £l5O was needed in addition to the offertories, fur repairing, cleaning, &amp;c., he said the schools were in a highly satisfactory condition; the teaching being above the average of elementary schools. The inspector had spoken of the conscientious and ener- getic work done by the teachers. the success of which had been proved by the large incre•ise in the grant, The numbers in average attendance were greater now than they had ever been. The average attendance was 420, against 400 in 1892, and 360 in 189/ Since last year they had been able to retruccl the fees, and he thought they would agree with him that the managers of the schools endeavoured to interpret the new Act in a broad and liberal spirit. (Applause.) Mr. GinsoN proposed the health of the Vicar, which was duly honoured, and responded to by the Vicar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>PARR' BANKING CO. AND THE &amp;LLI. ANCE BANK, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PARR' BANKING CO. AND THE &amp;LLI. ANCE BANK, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY GEVERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary general meeting ot the Parr's Banking Co., and the Alliance Bank Ltd., was held on Thursday, at the bank, Wina ick-st.,Warrington. Mr. Alfred Turner, chairman of the Board'of Direc- tors, presided. There were also present the follow- ing directors :—Mr. Cecil F. Parr, deputy chairman, Colonel Gamble, C.8.,' Mr. Peter Stabs, Mr. John Dun (general manager), Colonel McCorquodale. Mr. Alfred Hemlitt, Sir F. G. Frost. Mr. H. W. Meade King, Mr. Edward W. Nix, and Mr. N. H. Eckersley ; also the following—Shareholders :Messrs. R. Davies, John White, H. Greenall, W. Owen, H. Milling, A. Jolter, Jsmes Handley, James Robinson, E. Vincent, T. Walker, J. C. Stead, R. liarborne. John Hutchins, E. C. Turner, J. Hartley, H. Cram, Dr. Beaman, J. S. Hreen, John Murray, James Jolley, Willa)* J. Hartley Drs. C. White, and D. S. Smith.</p>
        <p>In proposing the adoption of the directors' report which was published in last week's " Examiner " the Chairman said it was very gratifying to the directors to meet once more in the old headquarters of the bank to submit a satisfactory report for the shareholders' adop- tion. He called it saticfactory because they had not only been able to declare their usual dividend, but to carry £2,500 to the property account; and £1,400 in ex- cess of the sum brought forward. The past six months had not been especially glowing for bankers, as would have been heard from previous bank meetings. The dul- ness had made it difficult for the bank to find satisfac- tory employment for their money, and the intense ex- citement had rendered it necessary that they should-take etre of their money, AS they did not know what calls might be made upon them. The result was satisfactory and he did not think the general balance called for any special remark. Generally there had been a moderate increase, showing that the tide of lansiness was still flowing with the bank. 'the' gross profits were con- siderably in excess of last year. The only increase which might he considered not satisfactory was in the ' working expemes, but three-quarters of that increase was duo to increased salaries of their officials and substi- tution si elecrrimlight for gas light. The general orn- gress must be considered satisfactory. for since 1890 they had issued additional capital to £92,000, and yet they had been able not nnly to maintain the dividend, but to increase it to 19 per cent., and to keep in at that figure. The outlook was not particulaily promising. The worst feature was the agricultural prospect. The Social and political subjects were also unpleasant to dwell upon. Neither strikes nor disruptions could con- duce to the safety of banking, but still the directors,and certainly he as chairman, had sufficient confidene,e in the stability of the Country and the commonsense of the people to believe that the compiny would tide through what mignt befall it satisfactorily, and at the next meet- ing would be able to show that they had .clone as suc- cessfully as in the past. • The Deputy Chairman briefly seconded. It was the end of their first full year in London. The res tit was satisfactory, although it was most unsatisfactory that their first Year should have been stich.a trying one. It gave great, satisfaction, to the directors that the confi- dence of the shareholders in the northern counties had been extended to them since the bank was transferred to London. • The report was unanimously and without discussion adore rd. - Mr. linbert Davies moved a vote of thanks to the directors. lie asked what had become of !lie guarantee (2100,000. given by the bank to the bank of Englhnd re Baring Bros, and Co.'s liquidation. Mr. Handley seerndrd the reaolution which was carried. The Chairman, in responding to the vote. when it had been put and nnanimotisly adopted, said that 88 regarded the airing guarantee, that bughear to bankers She London people had said that all danger of any calls being made was past.- That might be saying too Much, but he had heard one of his brother chairmen say that eventually there was not likely to be any call made rtp- on the guarantors for th • same. Colonel Gamble moved a vote of thinks to the man- agetnetit, and Mr. E. C. Turner seconded the resolution which wa _ . ' " "</p>
        <p>Mr. JclAn Dunn expressed the thanks of hitnself and his colleagues for this recognitinn of their services. After a short discussion on its Ina' technicalities'.• a snecial resolution to make an alteration on article No. 13 of the articles lf association was passed, and the meting ternuinated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>j THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>j THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The following paragraph which is culled from. the " Christian World, will be of interest tc those who knew the Rev. G. S. Reaney, wiles he was pits- tor of Wycliffe Congregational Church,in this town. "Rev. G. S. Reaney will take up his residence in Greenwich as Vicar of Christ Church in Septeinber. A correspondent writes : "I have it on the best authority that the appointment of Mr. Reaney to the living of Christ Church, reflects the highest credit on the Rev. Brooke Lambert, the vicar of Greenwich, who is the patron. Some Nonconform- ists have been inclined to think that the appoint- ment of Mr. Heaney was a dexterous move on Mr. Lainbert's part to win over liberal minded or lax • Dissenters to the Episco- pal Church. The fact is, that Mr. Lambert has generously come to the rescue of Mr. Reaney from the, let us say forgetfulness, of the 'Archbishop of Canterbury. When Mr. Reaney entered the "Church," the Archbishop promised him that in consideration of the position and stipend he Was re- linquishing, when he had been two years in the State Church a living should be found for him. Mr. Reaney has now been three years a curate, but the Archbishop has made no sign. Feeling that this was something in the nature of a breach of faith and an injustice to Mr. Reaney, the Vicar of Greenwich, on Christ Church becoming vacant, at once ap- pointed that gentleman. This reflects great credit upon the Vicar of Greenwich, but what about his Grace the Archbishop of Canterbury?"</p>
        <p>. Youths' temerity is about as proverbial as the spring poets' simplicity, only there is this differ- ence, you can see the former more on evidence than • the latter. For the past few days I have been watching an instance of the unlimited confidence which some Young Hopefuls of ‘Vilderspool,display in themselves whilst breaking trespass laws with quite as much sunny complacency as you might ex- pect them to show when breaking little trivial things—like windows or china crockery. In a field not far from the Church there is a notice to this effect : "Trespassers will be prosecuted." The youths played football on the field for a week. Then the notice was enlarged. The. football chi!, increased in membership; the notice board post was used for a goal post. Another notice board was put up; and then a cricket club came and pitched its wickets in the field—and one of the owner's men came to pitch the players out. But he went away again, and now 1 believe the youths are about to fix an entrance fee for spectators. After this I should say they will possibly want to let the land for building purposea or something equally indicative of proprietery rights.</p>
        <p>The man Farrell who was brought before the magistrates yesterday,was evidently a little mixed when he described himself as a bricklayer's clerk. He did not condescend to explain the duties of this office, bat they do not appear to be very onerous, as it was shown that he did very little work. He was also very thankful to the magistrates for giv- ing him an opportunity of working for his coun- try during the next 14 days,as he gave them a very low bow, and thanked them most heartily. When taken to the cells beneath, for the benefit of the court,he gave a jig with his clogs which could very distinctly be heard in the court room above. Thera is, you know, no accounting for tastes.</p>
        <p>The Band stand in the Park has since its erection undergone 1-everal alterations. It will be remem- bered that it was some months ago lifted from its bed, which necessitated an alteration in its con- struction. The latest improvement has been the shortening of the wooden railing round it, which was of such a height as made it impossible to see the bandsmen when playing,. It must be said that it has a clumsy appearance, and has since its erection, received a considerable amount of adverse criticism. Speaking of the bandstand reminds me of the collecting boxes, and I am glad to hear that the new system of going to the audience with the collecting boxes, from a pecuniary point of view is still a success.</p>
        <p>Warrington is endowed with a peculiar Board of Guardians. That is, no doubt, because the Tories have had the making of it, and have net done it well. At the meeting on Wednesday, the Local Government Board Inspector again urged upon the Board to provide a lieu as a playground for the numerous children at ['adore Ind listrlal schools, and also some gymnastic apparatus. These things have long Stflee been provided at most other insti- tutions cf a similar kind, and no doubt the Inspector thought he had only ict mention so reasonable a proposal for it to be agreed to. But he didn't know the kind of people that Warrington Tories have installed as Poor-law Guardians. One of the members ,at once became j,cular and suggested that they had far better have some • billiard tables and have Mr. Roberts, tbe champion, down to give le,SsOns. This little witti- cism, which smells of a second-claas billiard room; was quite enjoyed by the Guardians, and the In- spector was so much impressed by it that he pro- mised to convey it to the Local Government Board. How it will impress those in London with the gravity and wisdom of Warrington Board ot Guardians.</p>
        <p>The two inquests held on the body of the lad who was killea in Bewsev-e.treet on the horse fair 'day will have served a good purpose if the reqpni- mendations of the jury are brought home to our local rulers. It surely is, as Coroner Brighouse pungently put it, a remnant of the barbaric age to see horses Tanning up and down unprotected in public streets on the occasion of the horse fair. The wonder is that there have not been more Vic- tims sacrificed to this antiquated institution, when, it is remembered that the greater part of the streets on which the horses are made to gallop, are paved with the slippery granite setts. But I suppose it will almost need an earthquake to make the Town Council move in the matter. Or will they wait until an Alderman has been knocked down?</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>AN EXTRAORDINARY SCHEME.</head>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY SCHEME.</p>
        <p>TO BE BURIED ALIVE.</p>
        <p>It appears that the authorities in Chicago are going to allow Seymour, the thought-reader, to carry out his extraordinary scheme ot being buried alive and remaj,-.. ing under ground long eneugh for a crop of barley to be grown en his grave. The coffin is made in three, sections. one fitting inside the other. In it Seymour its to be buried six feet deep. The barley is to tle sown on the grave immediately after the burial, and he calcu- lates that on September 24 it will have had time to• grow, ripen and be reaped. signals are to he arranged by ineami of which he can coniraunicate with the soldiers guarding the grave, should anything go wrong. The- general impression seems to be that if Seymour carries' out his plan of being buried alive, he will probably remain underground fi•ir good.</p>
