      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BURT ON MINING INDUSTRIES..</head>
        <p>BURT ON MINING INDUSTRIES..</p>
        <p>Mr. Burt has been interviewed upon the results of the International Labour Congresses. In the Course of the, talk the Member for Morpeth said :—" I admit that the legal eight hours movement i.s making steady progress among miners ; whilst, on the other hand, the general' eight hours movement is, I think, receding. Personally,: lam not disposed to lay down any general principle. Every case in 'every particular trade should be decided on its merits. For example, the case of railway men,. to my mind, calls for exceptional treatnn nt. . It is certainly remarkable how the hours ot labour li.aw; been steadily lessened. My father frequently worked from 16 to 13 hours per day. I myself used to work 13 in the mine. . . . I cannot say the conferences have done much practically,. but they have done a great deal in that they have enabled miners to compare notes with each other,to judge how they fare in different countries, and to bring about a general sense of the solidarity of labour, all of which must bear fruit in the future. The English miners undoubtedly are the best off. They are better paid—not merely absolutely,with reference to the actual amount of wages earned, but also relatively, hay- ingreagartclettote trheedcuocsatteodf—thweitnh the necessaries of life. Then they re p the Germans, I should say the best eodsuscibalteer in Europe. of I suppose the difference in wages aught theoretically to, help foreigners to compete with us, but somehow or other it doesn't. We still export Northumberla rad steam ,! coal to the Baltic ; and, save the few instinees where I other countries have el:cep:lona) advantages ger-graphi- cally for a particular market, we are holding our OWII well enough. Labour, you see, is often cheapest where wages are highest. To some extent these conferences country decline, Engligtho., havebe benefit miners have more than once sent money to help foreign fittenadtedthteomake expense l arfu4hos n or ne in miners on strike. I believe also that if it were known beyond doubt that coal, for example, was b-ing shipped sees to break down a strike elsewhere, the men would refuse to work. But of course, in actuel practice, it , very difficult for workers to ascertain the destination of 1 the output."</p>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING CO.</p>
        <p>(LIMITED). LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>Anzerican Depot—Mß. R. D. WILLIAMS,</p>
        <p>Pharmacist, Plymouth, Penna.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A DARING PICKPOCKET.</p>
        <p>Edward Chetwyn, 64 Church-st., was brought before the Warrington magistrates on Monday, on the charge of stealing from Theresa Dolan a purse containing 18s. on Saturday morning.—Prisoner was a chain maker at Cradley Heath near Birmingham, but since March he had lodged in Church-st., Warrington. On Saturday morning, Miss Dolan h4d been to the- market and was returning home. When she got to Golden Square, prisoner was observed by William Owen and Charles Carney to be following her very closely. She was carry- ing her purse in her hand when prisoner suddenly snatched it, and made off, but Carney and Owen fol- lowed and took the purse off him, but failed to hold him. He was captured subsequently by P.C. Barlow and locked up. His lodgings were afterwards examined and a large number of purses were found. As during the past three months,there had been a great number of purses stolen' it was very probable that prisoner had something to do with them.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for three months' hard labour, and the two men Carney and Owen were allowed 2s. 6d. for lose, of time, and complimented upon their praiseworthy behaviour in being so prompt in giving chase to prisoner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>THREATENING TO SHOOT A _POLICEMAN.</head>
        <p>THREATENING TO SHOOT A _POLICEMAN.</p>
        <p>At Daresbury Petty Sessions, on Tuesday, Tom Barber was brought up on a charge of threatening to shoot P.C. .Millington, stationed at Grappenhall, on the 7th November, 1892. Prisoner pleaded not guilty. P.C. Millington said on the date named he saw the defendant and another man named Nelson in a field in the occupa- tion of Mr. Wm. Bradford, farmer, Grappenhall. Each of them had a gun, and when they saw the officer they stopped. He shouted he Barber and told him it was no use standing there he had better come along with him. Barber then said with an oath that if the officer came any nearer he would shoot him. He then put the gun in a shooting position. Soon afterwards the two men ran along the field. Witness arrested Barber a few days ago, on his release from gaol. The prisoner in his de- fence said at 6.30 on the 7th November last it was very dark and foggy, and he was out with Nelson shooting, when they lost their way. He picked up a piece of wooden railing near the canal bank, and this was what , he pointed at the constable.' He had never had a gun of his own, and said it was his companion that threatened the constable.—Prisoner was bound over in two sureties of £5 each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>THErr OF PaoyErmse.</head>
        <p>THErr OF PaoyErmse.</p>
        <p>John Bridge was charged at the Bmough Court, on Wednesday, with stealing from the stable of Thomas Pilkington a quantity of provender, the previous night. From the statement of the Chief Constable it appears Mr. Pilkington's stables are situated in Edgeworth-st., off Bewsey-st., and the prisoner was seen by Ernest Pilkington, son of the complainant, to come out of the atableyard carrying a bag with something in it. The lad'asked him where he was going,to which the prisoner replied, "Go away, or I will break your neck for you." The boy watched the prisoner, and saw him go into a stable occupied by Mr. George Boardman. The lad went and told his father,who went to Mr.Boardman's shop. The prisoner was found in Mr. Unsworth's bake house, in Legh-st., and in reply to the charge of stealing said he was sorry for taking it, and was willing to pay Mr: Pilkington again.—Mr. Pilkington said he went to Boardman's shop, and asked who was look- ing after their pony, and Mrs. Boardman replied, Jack Bridge. She said that she had given him 2id. to buy something for it, to which he replied that would not keep a pony. He had missed a considerable amount of provender lately. The prisoner was fined 103. and costs, or 14 days' hard labour. A CORPORATION' GAS FITTER ASSAULTED. _ _</p>
        <p>James Wilkinson and Phabe Wilkinson, Longford-st., Amy Wright, 4 Warburton-t., Elizabeth Spruce, 32 Warborton-st., and Mary Jane Morris, 3 Portland-st., were each charged at the Warrington Borough Court with assaulting and beating Edward Spendlow on the 15th inst.—The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) who prosecuted said that prosecutor was a gas fitter em- ployed by the Corporation. Defendant, Wilkinson, was a gingerbeer manufacturer in Longford-st. and the other defendants were employed by him. Mr. Wilkin- son had neglected to pay his gas bill, and Mr. Spendlow went to demand the money. As it was not paid, com- plainant proceeded to cut defendant's meter, and while be was doing so, Wilkinson struck him, and it was also alleged that the remaining defendants assisted him. Mr. Browne who appeared for the defence had a sugges- tion to make, and he (the Town Clerk) hid agreed to that suggestion.—Mr. Browne said that he was in- structed that the female defendants instead of assisting Wilkinson to assault Spendlow, were really endeavour- ing to prevent him from doing so. Wilkinson had how- ever, promised to pay two guineas to the Infirmary and also pay the costs if the summons were withdrawn, and as the Town Clerk had agreed to these terms, he hoped the magistrates would do also.—The magistrates accord- ingly sanctioned the agreement. • INCORRIGIBLE WARRINGTON LADS. ' •</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Wednesday, three Warrington lads named Patrick Leonardy, James Riley, and Benjamin Jaundrell. were charged with steal- ing two pairs of girls' boots, the property of James Bothwell, and also with stealing from the coal office of Thomas Eaude, Marson-street. a sprig-bit, a piece. of soap, and a quantity of nails value ed. The Chief Con- stable said James Ilalliwell, occupied a lock-up stall in the market, and lived at Middleton, near Manchester. The stall was locked up safely on Saturday night by the complainant. On Monday morning a man was painting in the market when he hoard a noise and observed the three boys running away by the way that leads into Flag-lane. Be also found one pair of boots and gate them to Sergeant Rowbottom, The matter was reported to D.S. Monaghan and he artested the three boys the same night, and found that the other pair of boots had been taken to Mr. Leslies in Mersey-street by one of the boys for sale. Two of the bpys pleaded guilty but Leonardy pleaded not guilty. Theywere brought before the magistrates on Tuesday but were remanded, as it was thought they were complicated in a coal office theft. Mr. Eaude, who keeps a coal office in Marson- Street, on entering his office on Monday morning found that the door had been opened, and the office in a dis- ordered state, with a few articles miming. The three lads admitted being together in the office. Mr. Fran- comb's coal office had also had three panes of glass broken and Jaundrell admitted having thrown and broken them. Mr. Riley, school attendance officer, said the boys WPM the worst in the district. Two of them had been summoned by the schoolattendance committee and their parents were unable to manage them. He thought it would be better to remove them to a reformatory. The magistrates sent the boys to prison for fourteen days, and afterwards to go to a reformatory for five years.</p>
        <p>- - CHABGN AGAINST A GRATPBNHALI. PUBLICAN. Sohn Hewitt, landlord of the Ram's Bead, Grappen- hall, was charged with permitting drunkenness on his licensed premises on the 18th June. and Michael Curley was charged with being found drunk on the premises at the same time and place. Mr. E. Wynne Humphreys of Runcorn, prosecuted on behalf of the police, and Mr. A. Browne, Warrington, appeared for the defendant.— Mr. Humphreys in opening the case said at 9 o'clock on Sunday the 18th June, the defendant Michael Curley was in the Barry Arms, Grappenhall, and in consequence of his riotous conduct he was turned oat by the And- lord, Charles Heaton. He then appeared to have gone AO the Rams Head, and whilst there was served with</p>
        <p>some drink. The constable who saw him turned out of the Barry Arms, found that ho was still in a riotous condition at the Barn's Head, and also had been served with beer. Be drew the landlord's attention to him and he, replied that he had only been served with a quart of teen The constable told him "that sort of work would not do" and then went out. Be again called at the house a little later and found that Curley had been served with another quantity of beer. Other evidence would show that Curley was drunk, and that the land- lord had his attention called to it on the constable's first visit.—P.C. Millington, Grappenhall, gave evidence in corroboration of the above statement.—Charles Hewitt, landlord of the Barry Arms, said Curley was in his house on Sun:ay evening the 18th June, and he turned him out as he wanted to fight another man there. The man was not drunk then but had had sufficient.—Joseph Fairhurst said he was outside the Ram's Ilt•ad when he saw the three men coming out. One of them tumbled but he could not identify the man.—Enoch Woods, residing at the huts at Thelwall, said he was in the Ram's Head when Michael Curley came in. He called for a pint of beer and was served by the landlord's son. Two other men came in about five or ten minutes after- wards. They called for a quart which was paid for by Curley and supplied by Mrs. Hewitt. He thought Curley was about" half and half" when he came in. If he had been sober he would not have used the language ha did.—Mr. Browne contended that it was admitted by the witnesses for the prosecution that the man was not drunk when he entered the house, with the exception of time police officer Millington. The publican had a very difficult duty to perform ; it was difficult to say when a man was drunk. His client repudiated the statement of the officer that he told him the man had been ejected from another house the first time the officer came. Be also accounted for Curley's stumbling on leaving, the house, by the steps leading up to the house not being in good condition. Mr. Browne concluded by asking the ; Bench to give his client the benefit of the doubt.—Mr. , Hewitt, landlord of the Ram's Head, said Curley was • not served witla a pint of neer previous to the quart which the officer saw, which was all that was served in I his house for the three men. The beer the men was drinking the second time the officer came was the ' remainder of the quart.—The magistrates fined the defendant the costs, and Curley was fined 2s. ed. and costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne afterwards applied for temporary auth- ority for John Ashley, farmer, to sell at the Ram's Head, in place of Mr. Hewitt, adding that Mr. Hewitt was going to leave the house had not the present case arisen. The application wasgranted, Mr. Long remarking On the present tumble-down condition of the house and asked when the new one would be erected. Mr. Browne said Messrs Greenall's hoped to have the new one built.in three months.</p>
      </div>
