      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ASSAULTS.</head>
        <p>ASSAULTS.</p>
        <p>TJohn Harris, 28, Jockey-street, was charged at the Warrington florough Court on Friday, with assaulting and beating Lydia Vale, 33, Warburton-street, on the 24th inst.----Lydia Vale stated that she had had a quarrel with defendant's mother, and he came to her house and struck her in the mouth. Several witnesses were called for and against the defendant and the magistrates fined defendant in the costs.—Peter Farrell, 1, Crossley-street, was charged for beating his wife Bridget Farrell on the 21st inst. ,The wife stated that her husband was always getting drunk, and on the above date he came home drunk, and when she told him about his condition, he struck her a violent blow and knocked her down. She ' wished to have a separation order as she had no comfort at home.—The magistrates sentenced prisoner to 14 days' hard labour, and ordered him to pay 7s. a week towards his wife's maintenance. Prisoner's behaviour during the hearing of the case was very peculiar, des- cribing himself as a bricklayer's clerk, and when be was taken into the cells, be could be heard dancing a jig down below, and then came upstairs again shouting through the kethole that he would sell every stock of furniture when he came back.—ThomasTurner,butcher 10, Market-street, was charged with assaulting his wife Elizabeth Ellen Turner, OR the 24th inst. It appeared that there has been some dispute for a long time about Mrs. Turner not getting up in time to make her husband's breakfast, and on the above date, Turner gave her a blow in her side woile she was in bed, because she had not got up. She also wished for a separation.—The magistrates however declined to grant a separation order, but fined the hus- band £1 and costs or 14 days. Jesse Weedall. 29 Ernest- St., was charged with assaulting Clara Keenan, 9, Bostock-st., on the sth inst. Complainant said that she was going along Green-st., on the above date when Weedall came behind her and struck her on the back of the head two or three tirnes7 and a policeman had to separate them. There had bean some illfeeting between them for eighteen months.—Several witnesses were called by complainant but only two little children saw the assault committed.—Defendant denied committing any assault at all.—Defendaut was bound over for six months, and was ordered to pay the costs 10s. Coun- cillor Roberts dissented from the decision of the other magistrates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Alderman Holmes (in the chair), Alderman Chandley, Messrs. F. Monks, Jotn Burgess and Captain Ridgway. DAMAGING THE" ROYAL EXCHANOB."—Patrick Foy and William fracey were charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Market-street. P.C. Barlow said he was called into the Royal Exchange publichouse and found the prisioners had pulled up some of the bars, which protected the window. The were both drunk. The landlord of the Royal Exchange said the two men came into WS house and asked to be served with beer, but as they were drunk he refused to serve them. They then commenced pulling up the iron bars at the window, and said they would pull the house down. He brought the constable to eject them. The magistrates fined the defendants 55. and costs each or 14 days. AN OLD OFFENDER.—Annie Ryan, a well-known character, was charged with being drunk in Bridge-street on the 19th inst., and also with being drunk and dis- orderly in Buttermarket-street, the previous evening. She was fined ss. and costs in each case, or 14 days' imprisonment. Morrney.—:Before.Mr. F.:Monks (chairman), Alderman . . .</p>
        <p>Harrisen, and Messrs. W. Pierpoint and S. Wright. Mu= AND DISORDERLY.—For committing this offence on Saturday night, Thomas Pannell, 9, Angel Yard,Thomas Makinson, Bedford Leigh, Annie McGuire, 6, Berry's Yard, and Patrick Burke, 16, Silver Square, were fined ss. or seven days ; Annie Browne, was ordered to leave the town ; Peter Higgins, 7, Ship Yard, was fined 53. and costs for committing the same offence in Bridge-st., on July 19. " Knocir OFF Duca."—John Whittaker,Friars' Green, was charged with throwing stones to the danger of pas- sengers in Parker-st., on the 14th inst. P.C. Corcoran proved the case.—Prisoner said that he was merely playing " knock off duck," and was not endangering anyone's safety. The magistrates inflicted a penalty of 53. or seven days' bard labour. WINDOW SMASHED BY A DRUNKEN MAN.—Wm. Butler, 2, Pennington's Yard, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Eforsemarket-st., on Sat urday night, and also with damaging a window the property of Mr. Chantry ou the same date,—P.C. Flollingsworth said the prisoner was in a helpless state of intoxication and fell into Mr. Chantry's window. Witness had great difficulty in bringing him to the police station —Prisoner was fined ss. for being drunk and the other charge was, dismissed as it was not a wilful damage. AN ALL-ROUND ASSAULT.—A soldier named John Moses Jones was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly on Saturday night, and also with assaulting P.S. Taylor, P.C. Lockson, and J. Cliffe, a soldier. Pri- soner was drunk in Horsemarket-st., on Saturday night and appeared to be wishful for a row with everybody. He met two other soldiers one of whom WAS Cliffe, and knocked them down,when P.S.;Taylor and P.C.'sLockson and Hampson came on the scene. When requested to go away he struck the sergeant in the face, and also kicked him and P.C. Lockson on the legs. They had the greatest difficulty in getting him along to the bridewell. —Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs er 14 days' hard labour. PITCH AND Toss.—Wm. Boardina.n, 74, Stamford-st., was fined 59. for playing pitch and toss in Stamford-st., on Sunday morning.—P.C.'s Clough and Barlow proved the case.</p>
        <p>SLEEPING IN A HAYSTACIL.—John Harrington and Jos. Clarke of no fixed abode, were each charged with being rogues and vagabonds, found sleeting in the haystack of Wm. Owen, Arpley fields at three o'clock on Monday morning. P.C. Bell proved the case, and Wm. Owen said that he was continually having his hay spoiled, and also his cattle tampered with by this class of men. He had also had some stacks of ha, burnt down.—Prisoners were fined ss. and costs or seven days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>AN UNGRATEFUL BEGGAR.—John James Higharn,-;as charged with stealine• from the shop of Flcnry Goldsten, 157, Mersey-et., a man's cap, valued 'kid., at 6.55 the same morning. Prisoner had gene in the shop to ask for some bread, and Mrs.Goldsten went into the kitchen to bring him some. After he went away, Mr. Goldsten missed the cap, and went after him and he was given into custody. Prisoner was tined 103. and costs or 14 days.. MESDAY.—Before Mr:11. Garnett (chairman), Mr. E. J.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>(Inuit and Dr. Smith.</head>
        <p>(Inuit and Dr. Smith.</p>
        <p>THEFT or HAM.—GeorgeWilliams was charged with stealing from the shop of Thomas J. Lipton on Monday morning four lbs. ham valued 3s. The Chief Constable stated that prisoner had been a marine fireman but was now following the trade of a baker, and lodging in Ship Yard. On Monday morning, P.C. Hollingsworth was standing near Toft's shop when he saw prisoner handle the ham in Lipton'sshop and then put it under his coat and walked along Friars Green. Be dropped it on the ground after going a little distance but before he could pick it up, the officer had caught him and took posses- sion of the ham. Prisoner was very drunk at the time and was brought to the police station.—Prisocer was sent to gaol for 14 days.</p>
        <p>A VIOIENT CIIARACTE- R.—Thomas IKPrtnv was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Union-st. on Mon- day night, and also with doing wilful damage to a win- dow of the Shakespeare Inn to the extent of 103.—P.C. Barlow said that he was requested to go to the Shakoa- peare In in consequence ot the violent behaviour of the</p>
        <p>rprisoner. He was creating a disturbance and coming out with some fearful language because the landlady had put hirr. out. Lie had knot:Red the plate glass window into pieces.—Prisoner S4id that he did not remember anything about the affair.—The magistrates ordered prisoner to pay ss. and costs and also 10s. the cost of the window or go to gaol for 14 days. _ _ _</p>
        <p>HAWKINO CLOTHES SUSPECTED TO HAVE Bees STOLEN. —Mary; Rooney was charged with having in her posses- sion a quantity of children's clothing without giving a satisfactory account of them.—Detective Monaghan said that he saw defendant offering the clothing fur sale at Gibbon's second band shop on the previous day. As she gave no satisfactory account of how she got them he brought her t) the police station, Witness had often seen her selling pins and other articles but never saw hor selling clothiog before.— In court defendant expressed her abhorrence, at the idea of stealing but she still failed to satisfy the ' magistrates as to how she got them and she was bound over to appear when called upon, the clothes in the meantime befog detained. Assanzv ON &amp; Mcrraza.—Patrick Kilroy was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Jolley's Yard on Monday night and also with assaulting his mother, Elizabeth Kilroy at the so me thee and place.—Mrs. Kil- roy said that her son came into the house very drunk, and knocked her to the floor and jumped on her. "She hoped that the court would give him the full strength of the law as he was a bad one."—P.C. Lockson gave corroborative evidence and prisoner was sent to gaol for two months.</p>
        <p>SLEEPING OUT.—William Clare and John Lee were sent to prison for 14 days with hard labour, for sleeping on .a haystack on Monday night.—P.C. Bell proved the ease.</p>
        <p>ARSENTEES.—WiIIiam Hanlon, Adam Barber, William Timlin, John Gorton. and Thomas Sweeney, wore all I charged with absenting themselvesfrom militia training last year.—P.C. Dean said the defendants were banded over to him at Orford Barracks on Monday.—Defendants were fined 40s. and costs each, or in default 7 days' imprisonment. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, Councillor Roberts. PAWNBROKER'S LlCENSll.—William Briggs, Powys-st., was granted a renewal of his pawnbroker's licence. Trtarr FROM TIIE "Fox."—Joseph Woods, 37 School Brow, was charged with stealing two speaking tubes valued 2s. 6d., the property of Elizabeth Dutton, on Thursday.—ln the statement of the Chief Constable it appeared that a sale was being held in the Fox Inn by Mr. Robert Lowe. During, the sale, prisoner was ob- served to pick up two speaking tubes. He afterwards went towardsc;hurch-st. A young man named William Henry Harper informed P.C. Monks who captured pri- soner. Prisoner in answer to questions said that the tubes were in his father's shsp in Church-st.' and the officer found them there.—Harper and Robert Lowe cot- roborated the statement, the latter saying that the tubes were to remain in the house after the sale.—Prisoner said that he was teld by a purchaser to take down some boards, and he put the tubes in his pocket not thinking what he was doing and took them home. When he found them in his pocket, he at once determined to take them back, but before he could dO so he was arrested.— Prisoner was fined 103. including costs or seven days. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—ThOUIRS Edwards, 'bus driver, New-road was charged with cruelly ill-treating a horse by whipping it, on the 6th inst.—P.C. Dowtv, R.S.P.C.A., said that he saw defendant driving a 'bus horse and whipping it most unmercifully. When he questioned the driver he said that he wanted the horse to go. The horse was sweating very much.—P.C. Delehay corroboratod —Defendant was fined 10s. and costs. WINDOW SMASIIING.—Ann Pe era, 59, Ffamilton-st.,was summoned by WM. Bostock. builder, Wilderspool, for breaking a glass window in Hamilton-st., doing damage to the extent of 4s.—Wm. Bostock stated that defer- dant was drunk on Monday, and sho smashed the win- dow pane belonging to some of his property. Witness went to her to stop her when she said that she would smash some more windows for his. He had had a large number of panes broken recently in Hamilten-st.—De- fendant so far from expressing contrition for what she had done, expressed regret that she had not broken more windows.—She was ordered to pay the damages and the costs, and also to be kept in custody until this ( ;aturday) night. VIOLENT LANGUAGE.—James ROWSOR, 4. Back Stam- ford-a., was summoned at the instance of Ellen Birchall, of the same street, for threats alleged to have been made to her on the 24th inst.—Complainant said that on the above date, she was standing on her own'doorstep when defendant came to her and used some violent threats. He was a night poacher and suspected her of telling the police, which, however, was false.—Defendant did" not deny using the words lie was accused of, but alleged that he was provoked to it.—He was bound over to keep the peace for six months and pay the costs, or in default 14 days.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TIIESDLY.—Before. Mr. W. Long On the chair), Messrs.</p>
        <p>H. Neikl, H Talbot a7nci C. Matson..</p>
        <p>THE ASSISTANT OVERSEERSHIP.—Mr. J. L. Tunstall an- plied for and received the magistrates' sanction to be as- sistant overseer for the township of Latchford. _ _ .</p>
        <p>DOG OWNERS FlNED.—Birctirall Frith was fined the costs, 3s. 6d., for allowing his dog to be at large in Nantwich-road, Antrobus, on the Bth June.—James Green, 6. Peter-street; Latchford. was charged with al- lowing his dog to be at large at Grappenhall. He was ordered to pay ss. including costs. A SLEEPY Irosivisn.—Robert McQuinn, a carter in the employ of Mr. Isherwood contractor, Warrington, was charged with being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and cart conveying beer, on the 9th inst at Preston-o'- hill. The carter said be had had some very long Journeys lately. He was fined ss. including costs. DRUNK AND DiscoinitazY.—James Fitzpatrick was Or- dered to pay ss. including costs for being drunk and dis- orderly at Moore on the Ist inst. IN Szapcn OF Gsma.—Frank Price, a boatman em- ployed by Mr. John Horsfield, Runcorn, was charged with having on the 19th inst., at Walton Superior, entered the land of Sir Gilbert Greenall, in search of conies.—William Lockett, keeper for Sir Gilbert Greenall, said he saw the defendant at ten minutes to four in the morning with a dog. The defendant had left his boat, and his dog rushed into the field. He (the keeper) bad to shoot one rabbit. When the defendant saw him he went away and refused to give his name. He followed him up to Runcorn and a police officer at Runcorn gave him his name. Defendant was fined 10s. including cot 5.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT Cusvomva.—Thomas Quinn, a French polisher, was charged with being disorderly on the premises known as the Cat and Lion public-house, Stretton, and refusing to quit. He was also charged with aszaulting Mr. William Ostler, landlord, and P.C. Bratt. Mr. Ostler said the prisoner along with another man and woman came into his house on the 4th inst. The woman wanted to sell the barmaid a pair of boots, and came into the private kitchen. He (witness) or- dered her out of the kitchen. The man then came into the kitchen, followed by the other man and refused to go out. He afterwards struck witness several times, and prevented him from leaving his house by the front door in order to bring a policeman. Witness went to P.C. Bratt's house, the back way, and the officer came to the prisoner in his shirt sleeves. He then assaulted the officer and butted him in the stomach with his head and knocked him full length on the road. He was very rough when being brought to the police station. Prisoner who had already been imprisoned for three weeks was fined 20s. and costs or one month's imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. C. Reynolds .and Mr. A. Thomas.</p>
        <p>Dumix AND DisoßneßLy.:—For this offence fines were imposed upon Mary Taylor,John Ford, Mary Ford,Thos. Minshull, James Wm. Caton, and Wm. Bayley. PAWNBROKERS' LICENSES were granted to Samuel Dodd, Princess-st. ; Henry Dodd, New Egerton-st. ; William Craddock, Church-et. ; and John Arthur Smith, Allcock- street.</p>
        <p>A WESTON SQUAEBLE.—WiIIiam Pritchard, sen., an elderly man, was summoned for assaulting Ann Davies, and Frank Holliday was summoned tor assaulting Wna. Pritchard, jun. Mr. Burton appeared for Davies and Holliday. The parties all live in Bank Cottages,Weston, and on Saturday, the 15th, some dispute arose about a bucket. It was alleged that Pritchard and his two sons threw Holliday to the ground, knelt on his chest, and abused him. Mrs. Davies, nho is Holliday's mother-in- law, screamed murder, and Pritchard came up to her and struck her on the jaw, ca-Jsing her to fall on the gibund. It was alleged that on the Monday following Holliday waylaid Pritchard's son coming from the soapery, knocked him to the ground and thumped him. After hearing the evidence the magistrates dismissed both cases.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>•.* Secretaries of Criecet Clubs are requested to note that °porta of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the ead office, Bewsey-street, Warrington, not later than ten o'clock OD Weduesday morning.</p>
        <p>CRICKhTERS. Lawn Tennisers, and Golfers are reminded that GEO. G. BUSSEY dc CO., manufacture high-clus requisites. Their new catalogue is obtainable from Local Dealers, or the Sports Manufactory, Peckham Rye, B.R. Trade EGfieft Mark.</p>
        <p>&amp;rearms' BEATH v. Lrans.—This match was played ,at Lymm, on Saturday, and resulted in an easy victory for the visitors. Both teams played 13 men. Scores Lymm 37, Stockton Beath 92. ,</p>
        <p>SERGEANTS 3RD SOUTH LANCASHIRE REGIMENT V. DomaN's Wonics.—This match waq played at Stafford on Saturday. Score :—Sergeants 61, Dehnan'a Works, 52.</p>
        <p>SANEST WEALEYANS 2ND V. BRICK-ST. 2,4l).—The for- mer team beat second Brick-st.by 49 to 38 runs. Highley, who went in first. scored 10 and came out fourth. The I first eight Brick-st. men, thanks to the good bowling of Catteral and Booth, were brought out for 20, but the last two wickets gave so much trouble that they raised 1 the score to 38. WARRINGTON V. BOLTON.—Warrington batted first.l The first start was a disastrous one, for with not a run I en the board Smith was bowled off his pads, whilst' Sugg failing to get hold of one two oven later, fell to a catch at mid-on, thus two good wickets being down and not a run scored. Griffin joined Crosfield, and cautious play was indulged in, the initial batsmen doing the hulk of the scoring. With eleeen runs chr„nicled, 'Griffin played on. Clarke came next, and essayed to stay the rot, and for an over or two runs came freely. At 27 the last comer was out to an unsatisfactory deci- sion of lbw, the decision being hailed with ironical' cheers. W. H. Podmore stayed whilst nine rune were added, and allowed Ellis to secure his first wicket;but I the best stand was made when Crosfield had Bibby for a ' partner. Smith hurt his fingers and Shepherd was de- I puted to figure as wicket-keeper, the Warrington cap..l tam n generously allowing Dr. Mallett to fill the breach. Crosfield hit out vigorously, and having been let off twice he hit Ellis twice in quick succession to the boun- dary, but Halley had the satisfaction of securing him at 50. Crosfield's contribution being 31. Bibby was next taken at slip, and Bell being badly run out and Parry caught at 55 and 56 respectively the end was soon ap- parent. However, Rushwood and Woods carried the total to 85 before the former was c and b to Ellis. At the close of play tho Bolton score was 142 for eight wickets. Score and bowling analysis i WARRINo TOW. BOLTON. S M Crogfield b Halley 3l W H Walker b Sum 5 It Smith b Halley 0 Elli c Parry b Sugg Sugg c Shepherd b All Stanley bßlt,by Halley 0 Et Slater bS H Crosfield... 26 H Griffin b Halley G Clarke lbw b Halley 8 FR &amp;Lanett b &amp;lax ..... • ••• 33 W H Podmore bßilis 2 Halley et Bushworth b W BIM?? c Halley b Po more 3 Eha 3 C Stanley not out 1 R Bell run out. 2 J Laldlaw b Sun 0 H W Parry c Hills b U Shepherd not out 1 Halley 3 J Ruebworth c and b Bills 5 B Woods not out 4</p>
        <p>Extra s 6</p>
