      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JULY , 29, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EXPRESS VERSUS ELGEON.</head>
        <p>EXPRESS VERSUS ELGEON.</p>
        <p>The series of long distance competitions between Ber- lin and Vienna will be closed on Sunday by a flight of carrier pigeons. In Vienna much interest is being felt in this competition, and the record which is looked forward to is eight hours. The fastest express does the journey in fifteen hours, but then there are the stop- pages and the deviations from the straight line to be considered. Quite lately the Emperor's carrier pigeons have arrived from Reichenau, at the foot of the Sem- mering, after an hour's journey, whereas the fast trains take two hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EASY DIVORCE.</head>
        <p>EASY DIVORCE.</p>
        <p>Easy and quick divorce is evidently to be had in Oklahoma (U.S.) An attorney at Kingfisher, in that Territory, has issued a circular which points out that the statutes at Oklahoma specify no fewer than " ten separate and distinct Call At's for any on or more of which a divorce may be obtained,'! including "gross neglect of duty ; " that the Probate Court of each county, "which is always in session," has jurisdiction in actions for divorce, "which affords ligitants an opportunity to obtain relief very speedily and without having to await the slow process of the District Comt ; ' that the statute requires only three months' residence in the Territory ; and finally, that " persons coming to Oklahoma will find the city of Kingfisher, with its 4,000 inhabitants and all modern improvements, a very plea- sant place to live in."</p>
      </div>
