      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>APPLWPOIS PAROCHIAL COMH-ITTRR.</head>
        <p>APPLWPOIS PAROCHIAL COMH-ITTRR.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee held on the 24th inst. a letter was read from the Local Government Board sanctioning the loan of £1,470 for works of sewerage in the township of Appleton. It was resolved that Mr. Percy Silcock be instracted to prepare the working drawings for the sewage extension works, at Stockton Heath and Wilderspool, as approved by the Local Government Board, with specification of the work to be executed. It was decided to request the Ship Canal Co. to make necessary alterations of the syphon at Twenty Step Bridge so as to adopt the same to Mr. Sileock's scheme. The minutes were confirmed on the motion of Mr. Davenport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>HEISEY PAROCHIAL comarns.</head>
        <p>HEISEY PAROCHIAL comarns.</p>
        <p>The minutes of a meeting of this committee showed that it was resolved to ask Mr. Silcock of Warrington to prepare a plan of the township of Belsby showing there- on all buildings erected at the present time, and that the Clerk write to the Local Government Board stating that such plan was being prepared with the view of showing the present system of drainage in the township and the steps the authority propose to take for providing a sufficient means of sewage.—On the motion if Mr. Brandreth the minutes were adopted. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>TEE HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>TEE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The Infectious Diseases Hospital Committee at a meet- ing on the 19th inst., recommended that a committee be appointed to consider the best course to be adopted with respect to the storage of the furniture &amp;c., at Moore, and as to the removal or otherwise of the dis- infector. The inventory of the furniture of the hospital was correct. It was resolved that R-150 be paid to Dr. Adams for the services rendered by him from January 4, to August 6, 1893, in negociating for and superintending the construction of the hospital and furnishing it. in engaging nurses and attendants, and in providing medi- cal attendance, and performing the' duties of medical attendant consequent upon the smallpox epidemic. It was resolved that the best thanks of the meeting be given to Mr. Williams and Mr. J. L. Jones, for their services in taking the inventory of the furniture—Mr. Williams moved the- adoption of the recommendations and said that ha intended to submit a resolution that the item with regard to the payment of the amount named to Dr. Adams be carried into effect. He would state his reasons for doing so, and he thought he would satisfy the Board that the payment was reasonable and proper. —Mr. Heywood seconded the motion which was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth submitted the report of the Finance Committee showing that the balance in hand was £653 is. 7d. The accounts recommended for payment, amounted to £5l 18s., included one of £6 odd for disin- fectants. They had a large supply in hand, and by application, the people could have them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>• HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>• HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr." Adams said that the only information he had to give to the Board was that on the previous aay he had received -notification of an outbreak of typhoid fever at Weston Point, and one case at Kingsley and Frodshain. The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>REIIMIKABLE 'ACCIDENT AT EARLEBTOWN.</head>
        <p>A REIIMIKABLE 'ACCIDENT AT EARLEBTOWN.</p>
        <p>An accident of a rather remarkable character, oc- curred at the Vulcan works on Thursday morning. A youth named Richard Derbyshire, employed in the boiler yard, was engaged on a radial drilling machine. Be stood on a small platform some 12 feet above the ground. Through the platform and down to the floor runs the boring spindle, which makes some hundreds of revolutions in a minute. Somehow,the youth himself does not know how, the sleeve of his shirt got caught by the spindle; He was snatched off big legs in a second,and whirled round and round at a terrific rate, while every revolution of the spindle tore. a remnant of his Clothes from him. As his bsdy was whirled about it- struck Derbyshire's companion, and knocked him from the platform. Finally, the youth became disentangled, and fell to the floor almost nude,and bleeding and bruised to a painful extent. .He was conveyed on an ambulance to Dr. Dombey's surgery, whero it was ascertained that his arm was broken in three places below the elbow. He was bruised in several parts of the body, and sustained two serious scalp wounds. Dr. Dorniey. with some as- sistance from Mr. Wm. Branner, of the Vulcan, set the arm and dressed the wounds, and the youth was con- veyed to hie home at Burtonwood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>NEW VEGETABLE.</head>
        <p>A NEW VEGETABLE.</p>
        <p>A new vegetable, says "Science Siftings,," is about to be introduced to the people of the United States, through the Department of Agriculture. It is the root of the calla lily, which resembles somewhat in appear- ance the ordinary Irish tuber, with the addition of a few fibrous roots that have nothing to do with the qualities of the article as an esculent. It is more elong- ated, and when cut the interior is a trifle more viscid. But a section of it is so potatoe-like one would not be likely to distinguish any difference. In cooking. it has first to be boiled in order to destroy certain acrid pro. perties, after which it may be fried, roasted, baked, cr what not, according to taste. The farmers in Florida have begun to raise these calla roots for market. The plants grow readily in swamps, and so thickly that the yield of a single flooded acre is enormous. They repro- duce themselves by the multipication of their bulbs under ground, so that the grower has simply to dig up the offshoots and leave the parents to propagate anew. for centuries the Egyptians have cultivated a similar crop during the seasons of thti Niles overflow, and at the present time cala lily bulbs are a common vegetable in Japanse markets. So prolific and palatable are they that their propagation in many parts of the United States, where conditions are favourable. may reasonably be looked forward to as an agricultural industry of the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ALLEGATIONS AGAINST NUNNERIES.</head>
        <p>ALLEGATIONS AGAINST NUNNERIES.</p>
        <p>Allegations of poisoning and gross immorality in certain convents having been publicly made at Bourne- mouth by a Miss Ellen Golding, an "escaped nun," a preliminary meeting has been held, and 1, committee of investigation nominated. The Rev. Father Cooney, S.J., on behalf of the Roman Catholics, nominated Cardinal Yaughan, the Roman Catholic Bishop af Portsmouth, the Duke of Norfolk, Mr. W. S. Lilly, secretary of the Catholic Union, Mr. Justice Mathew, Mr. Justice Day, Mr. Henry MatthAaws, Q.C., Mr. Dikdley Leathley, Lord Clifford, and others on the committee; while on the Protestaneside were nominated Colonel Sandys, presid- ent, Colonel P. Carteret Hill, chairman, and A. H. Guinness, secretary ef the Protestant Alliance ; Captain Williams, secretary of the • Protestant Reformation Society ; the Rev. E C. Britten, Surgeon-General Part- ridge, and others. The investigation is to be pushed forward with all possible expedition. Miss Golding has given the names of ten convents in which, she asserts, the poisoning and immorality were carried on while she resided in them, at certain dates which she also gives.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC NOTICE.—Not one but HUNDREDS of people of both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutions are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson, 3 and 4, Fitzallan Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advice free when suffering from weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indiges- tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general debility. Posi- tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or consti- tutional. Sixty year.' experience. One address (as above) for over3o years. The" A I.Miature," 4s. 9d. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, four stamps, Sufferers disap- pointed with Electric Belts should write to us at onoe.—Advt</p>
      </div>
