      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>iiqf) 10'4</head>
        <p>iiqf) 10'4</p>
        <p>ALLLUED 'SHOCKING CRUELTY TO A BOY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, Mote Mr. C. Reynolds, and Mr. A. Thomas, a boatmai. named John Storer, and his wife Ellen, of the nai- row boats Pan' and Jove, belonging to Messrs. Simpson, Davies and CO., were summoned to answer charge of having -on the 10. h July, and divers other days within'siit months prior thereto then having the custody of Joseph Nash, aged seven years. did unlawfully,wilfully ill-treat, the boy in such a man- ner as to cause him unnecessary suffering or injury to his health.—Mr. F. Horatio Lloyd, solicitor of Chester, prosecuted on behalf oc.the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children.—Storer admitted • that he had occasionally beat the child, but pleaded not guilty for cruelly doing so.—Tbe woman also pleaded net guilty.—James Uphill, an inspector of the N.S.P.O.C.,fer Warrisgeon district, deposed that on the 12th July, he went to the house o f Mrs. Chesters in Nelson-st., and there saw the boy Nash. The boy was clothed only in a iguernsey. His body Was very dirty, and poorly nour- ished, and was very. much bruised. He had also a cut on the head. Witness took him to Dr. McDougalls sur- gery, where Dr. Burntoa made an examination of him. I Afterwards in company with Sergeant Woolley he him the boy weighed his weight was 32 lbs, 5 ounces. -Teel normal weight of a boy of seven years of age was 431bs. 5 ounces. At the Runcorn dock, witness and Sergeant Woolley saw the male defendant, who . said be had beaten the boy on the day previous with his waist belt which was two inches wide, with a steel buckle, fastened with a copper stud. The defendant further admitted that he had chained the be down in the cabin for beg- ging. On the same day the Inspector took the boy to the local shelter at Warrington. The boy now weigLed 33/lbs. Storer had informed wituesa that the buys' mother, Annie Nash, gave the boy to him in February 1892.—P.5. Woolly gave cOrroboretive evidence. He said the boy's body was bruised from the neck to tee hips. The male defendant had stated that the boy fell asleep when chained in the cabin.—Mary Cheaters, widow, 66 Nelson-st. Runcorn, stated that an Saturday, the Bth inst., the boy came to her house looking Mr his mother. On Monday the boy came insane he said he had had nothing to eat, and she gave him his breakfast. The female defendant came afterwards and knocked him under the table with a blow of her hand. She , threatened. that when she got him on the boat she " would kick the--guts out of him." Witness exam- ined the lidy and found bruises on his neck, head, body and:legs. The boy said the mit on his head was caused by, Mrs Scorer striking him with the polcer. There were also two scars on the boy's nose. These he said. had been caused by Mrs. Storer striking him with the Mead knife —Mary Booth, wife of Samuel Booth„ labourer, Nelson-, at., said on Monday the 10th, she was standing on the canal bank. She saw Mrs. Storer walking along-the canal bank with the boy. She pulled him up the steps leading from Nelson-st. like a dog, and then kicked him two or three times. She said " I'll kill you, you---, when I get you to the boat." On Wednesday morning the boy rushed past her. He was naked, and Storer and another boatman were running after him. He went into Mrs. Cheaters and witness Sollowed him. She examined him and found he was bruised from the back of his head to the soles of his feet. Where Mrs. Storer had kicked him on the hips, the -flesh was as " black as a pot."—The .boy was called and made a statement. He said he had been with the defendants ,some time and he had to sleep on the locker, on a shirt. shawl and pair of breeches. He was fed with bread aud butter for brecikfast, sometimes bread anti beef. For dinner lie had bread and beef and sometimes potatoes. He had enough to eat on the boat.: he ran ,away because he did not want to stop. The slay Mrs. Scorer fetched him from Mrs. Chester's, she chained him up in the cabin 'and afterwards Storer beat ,him with a strap. There was a dog in the cabin and the animal bit him because " he kept pinching it." He had only been to Sunday echool. The wound on his head was caused by Mrs. Storer striking him with the poker. The mark Oil his nose was caused by the bread knife. Mrs. Storer did that. It was the buckle-end of the strap that Mrs. , Storer struck him with.---En further examination the boy said when Storer was on the boat Mrs. Storer gave him bread and butter, and when Storer was not there she gave him dry bread. He went begging because he Was hungry.,-The two defendants cross-examined the boy to show that his head and nose had been injured by falling and that he was-well fed.—Dr. Brunton, assistant to Dr. McDougall, deposed that he made an examination of thetoy on the the twelfth inst„, and lie described in - detail the many bruises on the boy's body. Varying in size from an inch and a half to half an inch in length. There were on the right leg 12 small scars which might have been caused by the teeth °fa dog and also three similar scars on the left leg. Ou the left side of the head there was a cutiin. long caused by a blow from some blunt instrument. It was highly improbable that It was caused by a fall. Over the bridge of the nose ' there was a linear contusion about halt an inch long. Most of the bruises were about can average of 5 orG days' standing. Some of the bruises were very recent. They were not of a serious nature. Unnecessary violence would be needed to cause such -bruises. The boy was in . a filthy state and not very well nourished. He was also undersized ; this might have been caused by improper feeding when he was younger, and it might to a certain extent be hereditary.—On the application of Mr. Lloyd, the Bench committed the defendants to take their trial at Knutsford Quarter Sessions. Bail was allowed, the defendants in £2O and two sureties of ElO each. The Society were allowed to have the custody of the boy.</p>
        <p>THE FORGERIES BY A 'BANK MANAGE I. _SENTENCE OF. PENAL SERVITUDE.</p>
        <p>At .linutsford Assizes on Tuesday, Alfred ,Orrett, middle-aged, respectably connected, who pleadeo guilty to extensive forgeries, amounting to about £7BO, during the eleven years he was manager of the linutsford branch of Parr's Banking Company, was brought up for judgment. It was stated on Monday tor the prosecution that prisoner had alto- gether appropriated over £5,000. The Judge said he had taken time to consider what sentence he should pass in the hope of finding some mitigating circumstance, but he regretted he had not been able to find .any. On the contrary, it seemed to be a very serious case. It was impossible to lose sight of the tact that in cases of this kind some considerable force mustbe given to the con- sideration that it was necessary to warn other people who might be exposed to the like temptations, and placed in like circumstances as the prisoner. Prisoner's education, his training, his habits, and posithan were all so many everlasting warnings against the Course which he pursued, and his experience must have taught him that such a career as he entered upon was perfectly hopeless, and that he could not possibly in the tong run escape detection. It was a case of a very great fraud carried on for many years with the help of forgery and falsification of books. Be was endeavouring in the sen- tence ha was about to pass to temper justice withmercy, but after toll consideration the least sentence which he felt he had the right to pass without failing 4n his duty to seciety at large was that the prisoner be kept in penal servitude for the term of four years.</p>
      </div>
