      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Eettero to tbr atter.</head>
        <p>Eettero to tbr atter.</p>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE NEEDLEWORK GUILD. Dear Sir,—l shall be much obliged if you will kindly tet ins inform the Vice-Presidents and Associates of the South Lancashire Needlework Guild who subscribed to the presentation silver bowl for the Princess May's wed- ding present, that Lady Wolverton our Foundress and General President, has received a most gracieus acknow- ledgment of the same from the Duchess of York. Lady Wolverton kindly offered to be the official medium for the gift and suggested the form it should take. It was a massive silver howl, weighing 750 z., 14 inches in diameter, and 7 in deep and stood on an ebonizei plinth, and Messrs. 011ivant and Botsford, of Manchester, engraved the following inscription round the rim :—" Presented to Her Serene Highness the Prin- cess Victoria Mary of Teck on her marriage to His Royal Highness the Duke of York, by the President, Vice- Presidents and Associates of the South Lancashire Needlework Guild, with loyal good wishes for their hap- piness. July 6, 1893." A financial statement of the presentation, and a photo of the bowl have been sent to each vice-president who collected.—Yours faithfully, JOSEPHINE F. ROYSE, President, S.L.N.G. Newton-le-Willows, July 18, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SANKEY BROOK NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE SANKEY BROOK NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—You kindly permitted me the use of your paper on two or three occasions, to call the attention .of the public in general and the district sanitary authorities in particular, to the still disgraceful and pestilential condi- tion of this brook. " I came, I saw, I conquered " wrote Cmiar to his friend after winning a great battle. Many have come, many have seen—they came and saw—they smelled and went away. All sorts and conditions of men have seen and smelled at this standing disgrace to civilization—clergymen, doctors, sanitary inspectors, tramps, &amp;c., &amp;c.—and there has been one consensus of .pinion all round. They never saw the like before, and hoped they never may again. During the late hot weather the stench and colour has bon something dreisdful ; and now while I write (7 p.m. Thursday, 20th) it is simply horrible. I believe some months since a gentleman took a sample away, but he did not visit the brook at the right time to get a fair Sample. To see it in the full flow of its impurity you should visit it at 4 to 5 o'clock in the morning, it looks at that time like a stream of lead-colour paint, and oh! the stench ! If you were to smell it then, you would never want to do so again. It is like nothing else under I the sun of Heaven, but Sankey brook for foulness and flavour I challenge the universe to compare with it—it bears the palm for stinks. As the colours change the stinks vary, and each fresh one is worse than the last. We have lead colour, grey, green, and jet black, and each has its peculiar flavour and abomination. At the Post Office on the bank of the brook, they get their full share and suffer accordingly, and so does everyone who is compelled to remain day and night in close ' proximity to it. How long is it to continue? and echo asks, how long? Cupidity and selfishness has fouled it. Are there no counteracting purifying principles to bring it back to the state in which God gave it before man spoiled it? Hoping to see a great and radical change for the better, and that sneedily,l am, sir, yours respectfully, Saskey Bridges.</p>
        <p>CYMRO.</p>
        <p>THE PREMIER OF NEW ZEALAND ON THE TAXATION OF LAND VALUES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln a recent speech the Premier of New Zealand, the Hon. R. Seddon, bears such remarkable testimony to the benefit accruing to a nation from the taxation of land values, that I trust you will permit me to place it before your numerous readers. The speech was delivered on the 17th May last, at Napier, New Zealand. . .</p>
        <p>First, let us follow the Premier in what he says as to the state of New Zealand some years previous to the in- troduction of the taxation of land values. "From 1879 to 1884 the different administrations borrowed £7,500,000 in the London market. Then in 1887 to 1890 another £1,500,000 was borrowed. They even did m.,re than borrow. They placed upon the unfortunate people ad- ditional taxation to the extent of £680,000 raised by in- creased Custom's duties and increase in the Property Tax. As a result of all this squandering New Zealand was fast being depopulated. There was no employ- ment, commerce was stagnant, and property had lost value. During 1889 and 1890, 14,000 souls left the colony. Such was the state of things the present Government had to grapple with. They grappled with it in this way. They said to the struggling settler they would exempt his improvements. To those who held large tracts of country, mostly unimproved, keeping cattle and sheep where there should be smiling homes and happy people, they said "Pay a little more to the taxation of the country." Their opponents said "they were going in for confiscation," " there was going to be a revolution," there would be no money and no employment in New Zealand, and ' it would go down and down until it was last in the Australian group." What was the result to-day ? New Zealand is at the top and the other colonies down be- low. Instead of leaving, population is now returning. During the past few months we have gained over 5,000. Ploughmen have work, carpenters have work, there is an improvement in the social, commercial, and political pesition of the people. Instead of our opponents cry becoming true that money would leave us, the Government has a surplus of half a million at the end of the financial year. Above all, and this is singular. the owners of large estates are satisfied. There are in the colony 14 persons owing an average of over 200,000 acres, valued at £2,650,000 prairie value, or, with im- provements, at £5,538,000. To show you that they are contented and not taxed out of existence, we find them still going on bue ing land and increasing the size of their estates. If the taxation was confiscatiOn, if it in- jured them, the first thing they would do would be to subdivide instead of increasing their holdings. One man, non-resident draws from his lands no less than £85.000 a year, There are five properties averaging 80,985 acres and on these the total population is Just 110 50u15.2,b28 persons own land to the value of £32,406,000. Just imagine that !" Your readers, Sir, must " Just imagine that ! " for space is limited and the Premier's speech long. But it now requires no imagination to see that,given Land Laws carried out in one of our newest colonies as they are carried out in this the Mother Country,and we shall have results like those here—men leaving the country, strikes, commerce stagnant. By taxing its valm-, and thus making it unprofitable for men to keep land out of use, New Zealand has cut the Gordion knot. Instead of vast improved estates they are bringing back " the smil- ing homes and happy people." Over 40 years ago. Her- bert Spencer wrote in reference to allowing private pro- perty inland. " Observe now the dilemna to which this leads. Supposing the entire habitable globe to be so en- closed, it follows that if the land-owners have a valid right to its surface, all who are not landowners have no right at all to its surface. Heace such can exist on the earth by sufference only. They are all transpassers. Save by permission of the Lords of the soil, they can have no room for the soles of their feet. Nay should the others think fit to deny them a resting place, these landless men might equitably be expelled from the earth altogether. The Government of New Zealand have settled that question by saying in effect that the land of a country belongs to the people of that country. It cannot in equity become the " Property" of any one man or of any number of men. That of course does not interfere with its occupation or cultivation. It only gives all an equal right. This right can best be recognised by the t txation of !Ind values, apart from improvements. If we can only bring this about in Britain ; we shall see results similar to those in New Zealand. Plenty of work for workers, no room for idlers.—Yours truly, GEORGE LAMB. 1 (Member of the Council of the Financial Reform Association.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>DANGER OF EATING ICE CREAM.</head>
        <p>THE DANGER OF EATING ICE CREAM.</p>
        <p>Parents shrmld have a care of ice cream. Emily Jane Matthews, the two-year-old daughter of a cabinet maker, living at Bethnal Green, London, partook of some of the chilly sweetness and died of actute gastri- tis. A doctor told the coroner who investigated the circumstances that the gastritis was due to the child's eating the cream when she was in a great heat. Singularly enough, Dr. Danford Thomas, at Marylebone, was at about the same time investigat- ing an almost exactly similar sudden death in his dis- trict. The victim in this Case was not a baby, but Rebecca Wright, a girl of 17 years old. Being tempor- arily out of a situation she was residing with her parents at 8, New-sr., Mews, Dorset Square. Last Thursday she suddenly became ill and vomited excessively. The in- disposition she attributed to her having eaten two ice creams in the street on the previous night. Next diarrhoea supervened, and when she was seen by a doc- tor on Saturday she was in a state of collapse. A few hours later she died, as Dr. M'Cann said in his evidence on Thursday, from acute peritonitis with inflammation of the stomach and intestines. Did the doctor think the ice czeams might have set up the inflammation P inquired the Coroner. Yes, he thought so. He found no trace of poison. "There does not seem to be anything of an actually poisonous character in this case," observed the Coroner, "but I have known instances where the ices having been made a cenider- able time and the milk gone sour, fermentation in the ices followed, and a sort of blood poisoning has been set up in those who partook of them. Here, however, chill consequent limn eating the ices seems to have eaueed all ,the mischief." The jury agreed with this view.</p>
        <p>NUTtiS oN GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>If I were the w:iole of Warrington first eleven—which lam not—l would feel slightly disgusted with myself. Not satisfied with the defeat sustained last week they must needs go to Bolton for some more defeat on Satur- day. Bah! Why go so far for a defeat now-a-days ? Why not invite a minor local club down, and get the , home article ? True, Bolton fixtures before all others I ate regarded by Warrington as sure defeats. This seems to be the outcome of the repeated victories which Bol- ton have enjoyed for years.</p>
        <p>I simply don't care what anyone says in vindication of Warrington's play on Saturday, I am quite satisfied that it was bad. Their score was 65 against 142, and out of that 65 S. M. Crosfield scored 31 ! And what is mere, both R. Smith and Sugg went out for "ducks.' When Smith's wicket went down Warrington men felt' that they were wearing wet blankets. By-the-way, the team did not reach Bolton until three o'clock, and the Bolton "Chronicle" says :—" As usual, the railway company got the blame for this, but it would appear there was fault on the other side for not leaving by an earlier train." This is an uncharitable remark,and lam firmly of the belief that the Warrington eleven are get:. I ting " slanged " enough by Warrington contemporaries without Bolton organs going out of their way to do it. But seriously, our men were not to blame because the train they left by was timed to arrive at a suitable hour, but it was a quarter of an hour late in starting and was delayed on the journey.</p>
        <p>In fielding and bowling Warrington were certainly not much better than they were in batting. There were chances innumerable which were lost, both in bowling and in fielding. Mr.G.Clarke,for instance, was pursued by an evil genius, which is sometimes his companion; it is a genius which persuades him to drop a ball when he gets a good catch. Last Saturday the captain "chaffed" him about having done some good catching lately, to which the genial George replied, "I have S,nen success- ful in dropping a lot." Well, it seemed a trifle funny when later on a ball came descending down to Clarke, and he complacently looked up at it, opened his hands, and let the ball glide through his fingers to the ground. Even the spectators laughed, and Mr. Clarke was heard to say, " I'm too old for cricket," which is a story.</p>
        <p>The bowling -showed nothing particular. Crosfield took one wicket for 38 runs and 14 overs and W. H. Podmore t9ok one for 12 runs, and seven overs ; Sugg for 51 runs took five runs, and he had 21 overs. R. Smith for eight overs and 14 wickets, failed to take anything—unless it was the hint to give some one else a chance. Podmore did very well, and I should like to point out again as I did last week, that the more chances he receives of bringing into play his peculiar science, the more he emphasizes the fact that a better position on the bowling list ought to be given him. However, lam in- clined to think, and am not doing it in a wagging spirit —that there is a lack of genuine interest for the club's welfare. I think that most of the good players, the acknowledged ones, of the team are more anxious to distinguish themselves in other teams than their own. Hence it is that we are now getting so many poor teams made up chiefly of second eleven men.</p>
        <p>The second eleven beat Bolton second by 95 runs to 48. Brown and Unsworth did the chief scoring and Connor took most of the wickets.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyans made a sorry exhibition of themselves on Saturday when for the second time this season they were beaten by Brick-street and Friar's Green. At the first encounter Brick-street came out tne right side by 11 runs but this time they scored 84 runs and got the Wesleyans out for 28. Their batting and fielding were far superior to the Wesleyans, and El. H. Dutton played a very fine innings for 31 not out. The Wesleyans have done extremely badly of late,and if their fielding does not improve, they will continue to do so.</p>
        <p>" Another currant has been added to the Sankey Wire Mills bun." So said the affable juvenile man, when he read that the wire pullers had beaten Widnes Victoria on Saturday by 67 to 18. Luck has taken a kindlier turn with them, there is no doubt, whilst it seems to be all up th: figurative gum stick " with, some other junior teams. During the past month I learn that no fewer than 15 small clubs have died in town after a severe struggle amongst the members in the confiscation of club property.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>I learn that the Warrington Cycling Club are all up to fever heat in respect of this championship that is to be competed for at ale Football Sports on August Bank Holiday. Many of them are getting pretty well tired of their training, and the deciding of Hit, events will be welcome—especially to those who arc "going to put something on the winner."</p>
        <p>Saturday, a number of the members wheeled it to Chester where they spent a pleasant time—and some money. It is curious to observe that when the mem- bers do spend a pleasant time they usually spend money ; but they do not spend many pleasant times. It is offieally stated that they " amused themselves with boating and the Cestrians with an aqua: ic display." I was sorry to hear it ; - but they never go away from home withont a seeming to want to do what they wouldn't do at home. Why don't they amuse us with an aquatic display. People know them here I suppose ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>SWIMM I NU.</head>
        <p>SWIMM I NU.</p>
        <p>The meeting of Lancashire v. Surrey has been looked forward to with intense interest in swimming circles as one of the chief polo matches of the season, especially in connection with the county championship. Unfortu- nately for the crowd which assembled at the• Fleywood Baths on Saturday evening, the Southerners did not turn up in full, and consequently J. Hunt (Mayfield) and J. B. Windsor (Manchester Gentlemen) were substituted for Burgher(' and Reed, they being unable to get away from London in time to appear in their places. The homesters defended the deep end in the first half, and the half-time score was Lancashire 3 goals, Surrey 1. On changing ends Lancashire forced play, and although the defence of the visitors was good the forwards of their opponents were too good for them. hick r) and Atkin- son made up the 5 goals, to the credit of Lancashire. Final result :—Lancashire 5 goals, Surrey 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>H. Webster, Wairiugton, came in third in Vie 120 yards invitation hurdle race at the Manchester Athletic Snorts on Saturday. Godfrey Shaw, the hurdle cham- pion and winner of Saturday's race is of opinion that Webster "will iR time make a really good hurdle jumper." I think he is a pretty fair one now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SERIES OF ACCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>A SERIES OF ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER LAD KILLED.</p>
        <p>Since our last issue there have been a number of accidents to people driving in vehicles in or near Warrington. The first accident was to a 'bus driver named Thomas lowlands, residing at 7, Hart's- place, Bridge-sc., and in the employ of the Warring- ton Carriage Company, which occurred about eleven o'clock on Saturday night. ft appears he was returning from Penketh with a 'bus and three horses, and when near the end of Legh-st., in Sankey-st., he tried to put his foot on the treadle of the brake, and missing his footing, he fell between the horses and the 'bus, the wheels passing over his chest. Inspector Cox was near at hand, and at once obtained assistance, and conveyed the maw to the Infirmary, where he now lies in a critical condition.</p>
        <p>On Monday a boy named Harry Bracegirelle, aged 12, son of a bricksetter, residing near Tom Pain's Bridge. Chester-road, Stockton Heath, received a ride from a Warrington carter, when the cart tilted over and both the lad and the carter were thrown to the ground, the lad's head striking the kerbstone. The lad was taken home, and Dr. Parker was called in, but the lad never recovered consciousness and died from the injuries received on Wednesday. Mr. J. Worsley held the inquest at the Saracen's Head yesterday. Henry Bracsgirdle, father of deceased said that his son was eight years of age. He was brought home on Mon- d y suffering from severe injuries. Dr. Parker attended him until his death.—Dr. Parker ascribed the death of deceased to concussion of the brain.—James Sheridan, 69, Ellesmere-street, said he was a carter at Tom Paines and Twenty Step Bridges. He had made friends with the boy, and often gave him a ride. On Monday he was carrying stones from the Stag Inn, and the boy was with him. On the way back.the cart tilted over and threw de- ceased out at the back. He was picked up unconsciousond taken home in the cart. The only way the witness cou!d account for the cart tilting over was that the pin came out of the" kecker "of the cart.—Thelury returned a verdict df " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>Another trap accident occurred on Wednesday morn- ing at Stockton Heath. A farmer and his son from Barnton, near Northwich, were driving to the Wa'rring- ton market, when the backbend of the trap broke, and the son, a lad of about sixteen years, was precipitated to the ground. The accident happened opposite Stock- ton Heath Church. The lad was taken into a neighbour ing house, where Dr. Parker attended to him. The injuries proved to be serious, consisting of a severe con- tusion on the left knee and deep scalp wound, caused either by the horse's feet or wheels of the trap. The lad was subsequently conveyed home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MISSING JABEZ BALFOUR.</head>
        <p>THE MISSING JABEZ BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>A long serifs of telegrams and correspondence between Rnsebery and the representatives of the British Govern- mEnt at Buenos Ayres and Monte Video with 'regard to the proposed arrest of JAhez S. Balfour wai on Thursday. The efforts made by the Home Govern- rnont,pending the completion of a reciprocal extradition treaty, are detailed. Balfour appears to have kept him- self informed of the progress of these negotiations, and when they were completed lin declined to go down to Monte Video, preferring the safcr retreat of the suburbs of Buenos Ayres.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JCLY 29, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JCLY 29, 1893.</p>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL CaURCHES,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>There was a larger attendance- than usual at the P.S.A.mesting in Wycliffe Church,on Sunday afternoon. The total inP rn be rshi p still goes up, thirteen new mem- bers being enrolled on Sunday, which brings the total membership to 681. The chair was occupied by Mr.J.VV. Hayward, J.P. Mr. Taylor, of Dukinfield, formerly of Warrington, delivered an address on "Headlights." Miss B. Rathbone contributed a solo,and Mr. R. H. Pear- son rendered a vi.lin solo, accompanied by Mr. W. H. Payton on the organ. The Rev. J. Yong° announced that the money placed i a the benevolent fund boxes that day would be given to the widows and orphans caused by the recent colliery disaster at Thornhill. The amount collected was £4.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN CHAPEL. Special services were held at Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday. the preacher, morning and evening, ' being the Rev. J. Bennett. The proceeds of the service are to go towards the choir fund. There were special anthems well rendered by the choir at each service, the solo part of one anthem being taken by Mr. R. Entwistle. In the evening Mr. Levi Richardson sang Cardinal Newman's well-known hymn " Lead kindly light" to the tune Sandon, with a very good effect. The congregation at the evening service was very large, and they evidently enjoyed Mr. Bennett's vigorous preaching. In the afternoon an open air service con- ducted by Mr. Roylance of Moore was held in the village. SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION. . _</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Sunday School Teachers' Association was held at Padgate on Saturday. A tea was provided at the schoolroom, and about 80 were pre- sent. Following the repast an adjournment was made to the Vicarage grounds, where tennis and kindred amusements were engaged in. A service was afterwards held in the church, and was conducted by the Rev. J. J. Langham, vicar. An address suitalile to Sunday School teachers was delivered by the Rev. Canon Diggle, Mossley Hill. The rev, gentleman throughout his address laid stress on the fact that as there were pro- bably as many children attending Sunday schools as the elementary schools the position of the Sunday school teacher was fraught with many serious responsibilities. Canon Diggle then proceeded to give some sage advice as to the manner in which teachers should control their charges, kc. - ST. ANNE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Davies, curate of Emmanuel Church, Everton, has been appointed to the curacy-in-charge of St. Ann's, Winsvick Road, Warrington. RECOGNITION MEETING AT LEGA-STREET PRIMI-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>TIVE METHODIST CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>TIVE METHODIST CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>There were good congregation; ir-i-lii7morning and evening on Sunday, at Legh-st.. Primitive Methodist Chapel, when the Rev. W. J. Kirkland, one of the new circuit ministers, was the preacher. The collections were in aid of the trust fund.—A tea meeting was held in the schoolroom on Monday evening, after which a re- cognition meeting was held to welcome the new circuit ministers, the Revs. W. J. Kirkland, and W. T. Lead. Mr. J. Whitby, the circuit steward, took the chair. A resolution of welcome from the Warrington, Latchford and Hatton chapels was passed unanimously, on the motion of Mr. Crampton, seconded be Mr. J. Griffiths, and supported by Mr. Bissell, to the Revs. W. J. Kirk- land and W. T. Lead, and also to Mrs. Kirkland and Miss Kirkland, the latter of whom was stated to be a good worker in the Sunday school. The two ministers replied and the meeting closed with the singing of the doxology.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wed- nesday. Mr. R. Heaton presided, and there ware also present, Messrs. Muir, Aukland, Shaw, Davies, J. Houghton, A. J. Jolley. A. Jolley, Pilling, and Dr. Patterson, and Mr. J. C. Sutton (clerk), Mr. S. Sut- ton (assistant clerk), Mr. C. H. Ward (master), and Mr. Mines (superintendent of Padgate Schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PRESENTS OF FLOWERS.</head>
        <p>PRESENTS OF FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>The Master's report contained a record of the receipt of fl-ewers from St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>THE ROYAL WEDDING AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mines' report stated that the children at Padgate Schools had a treat in celebration of the Royal Wed- ding, the day after the event. Everything went off successfully. The children were allowed to indulge in games and sports, and a special dinner and tea were provided.</p>
        <p>THE PADOATE SCHOOLS. Mr. Jenner Fust, Local Government Board Inspector, attended to report upon an inspection he had made at the Padgate Schools. Among other things he was glad to see that his suggestion that the boys be taught kait- ting had been carried out. He also advocated that a field should be provided for the children, and also that gymnastic apparatus should be provided for them. These were things which most other schools possess.— Mr.A.Jouxy thought that the present recreation grounds were sufficient.—Mr. JENNER FUST : I do not think so. I think it would be much the better thing to pro- vide a field.—Mr. Jor..r.ur : I cannot see why we should.—Mr. JEERER FUST : Well, simply from a point of health, I advocate it.—Mr. JOLLEY (face- tiously) : Oh, well now, we might do that, and w might get some billiard tables, and then have Mr. Roberts, the champion, to come and give the children lessons. (Laughter.)—Mr. JENNER FIIST (sotto voce) : Very well, sir, your suggestion shall be conveyed to the Local Government Board in due cmrse. (Loud laugh- ter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>NOT SPENT TOO MUCH.</head>
        <p>NOT SPENT TOO MUCH.</p>
        <p>Mr. JEER FIIST also announced that he had been drawing up a comparative statement of accouncs and general returns for the year 1892. He had collected the accounts and returns for the preceding five years as a comparison. They would be placed before the Board in due course,but he could mention now that the accounts were very satisfactory to the Local Government Beard ; the expenditure was below the average for the five years, and altogether, he could promise them that the report would be a very satisfactory one. (Hear, hear.)—This was all the public business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>AN INDIAN PREACHER IN WA R RIN(ITON.</head>
        <p>AN INDIAN PREACHER IN WA R RIN(ITON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. B. Nagarkar of Bombay. who is occupy the pulpit at Cairo-street Chapel to-morrow (Sunday), is a minister of the Brehm° Somaj or the Church of Indian Theism, founded by the late Raja Ram Nohan Roy some sixty years ago in Calcutta, and associated especially in this country with the eminent name of Keshub Chunder Sen. The object of the Brahmo Somaj is to promote the spiritual worship of the One True God, and consequently it wages a severe war against image-worship and the institution of caste. As a movement it is especially interesting as being the product of the noblest I Hindu minds and not in any sense a foreign importation. Mr. Nagarkar has been working for the last ten years in Bombay, the commercial capital of India, and has travelled far and wide through his native land preaching and lecturing on questions of religious and social reform. There will doubtless be many in Warrington who will he glad to have this opportunity of hearing one who is so well qualified to speak on religious progress in our great Indian Dependency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT HOMING</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT HOMING</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>This club held its first young bird race from Wor- cester, on Wednesday, a distance of 84 miles. 250 birds were despatched and liberated by the con- voyeur of the Manchester Flying Club at 9 a.m, in a strong N.W. wind. The first bird to arrive home belonged to Mr. Peter Leicester of Stockton Heath, and was wired in at 11.55 a.m. The next race is from Cheltenham 106 miles on the 2nd of August. Result. Name. Velocity yds. per minute. I—P. Leicester 2—E. Burrows 3—E. Burrows 4—. T, Houghton 727 5—C. 1). Parktnson 6—E. Burrows 7—E. Burrows B—W. Lamb 9—John Buskley 10—H. J. Longton 687 11—H. F. Greenough 675 12-11. F. Greenough 675 13-11. J. Longton ....... ........... 14—C. D. Parkinson 15—E. Burrows 16—E. Burrows 17-11. Clire 648 18—H. Clare , 648 19-1. Naylor</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MARRIAGE MARKET.</head>
        <p>THE MARRIAGE MARKET.</p>
        <p>THE FIGHT IN THE HOUSE OF COMMONS.</p>
        <p>It was, says a Parliamentary correspondent of the Manchester " Guardian " impossible for one pars( nto note all the incidents of the disgraceful fracas w hich took place in the House of Commons on Thursday even- ing. Still, one who from a good position watched it from the commencement to the close was able to gather a good deal. The writer of this account was in that position, and the first thing he feels prompted to say is that never since the time of Cromwell has the House presented such an extraordinary spectacle. Then it should be noted that from beginning to end thee' gen tlemen of England " with their " loyalist " allies were the aggressors. It was Mr. Chamberlain's reference to Herod as furnishing a parallel to the proceedings of his opponents which provoked the cries ot " Judas ;"and let it be ebserved that not only Mr. T. P. O'Connor raised that • cry, but the whole body of Irish members. Then, amid the uproar which ensued Mr. Carson made himself conspicuous by shouting insult at the chairman, addressing him as" Mellor," and de- nouncing his conduct as disgraceful. It was this lan- guage which privoked Mr. Logan. He approached Mr. Carson, who was seated on the front Opposition bench. and entered into an altercation with him. What they were saying to one another it was impossible to make out amid the terrible din prevailing. It was,how- ever, evidently something exciting. Some persons de- clare that Mr. Carson replied to Mr. Logan's remon- strances by repeatedly shouting "You go to the bar." Instead of going to the bar Mr. Logan sat down beside him on the front Opposition bench. This was too much for the excited gentlemen behind. It had been rumoured earlier in the evening that the Tories had come to the House determined to have a " row ' when the closure came into operation, and their conduct certainly seemed to justify the allegation. Be it said to the credit of Lord Randolph Churchill and Mr. Jackson, that they tried hard to subdue the belligerent feelings of their followers, without, however, the slightest success. Mr. Logan's action in seating himself beside Mr. Carson was the signal for personal violence, and, of all the people in the world, Mr. Wyndham and Mr. Hayes Fisher (Mr. Balfour's private secretaries), gentlemen whose personal conduct is usually irreproachable, began the fight. They were seated behind the front Opposition Bench, and the moment Mr. Logan took a place there they pounced upon him, although they had nothing to do with the quarrel between him and Mr. Carson. Seizing Idni by the back of the neck they endeavour:d violently to thrust him off the bench. Mr. Logan naturally re- sisting the assault, Mr. Harry Foster hurried up to reinforce the original champions of law and order, but had difficulty in obtaining a grasp of Mr. Logan, the whole available area being monopolised by Mr. Wyndham and Mr. Carson. The Irish members, stirred to the heart by- the sight of a champion so ill-used in their cause, rose in a mass and tried to come to his rescue—a physically im- possible enterprise considering the crowded state of the benches. Besides, they reckoned without Colonel Saun- dersou„ who rose in his might and his height and dealt cuffs among them with the utmost liberality. One of these buffets fell on the head of Mr. Crean, an Irish member of slight build, whose pale face was conspicu- ous in the surging crowd. Even were Mr. Crean A robust man he would be no match for the Colonel, but the blow made him forget physical disparities. While making a well-meant effort to return it he was baulked by one of the billows of the furious human sea raging an the Opposition side of the House. The billow carried him away from the Colonel, but the next moment an- other brought him back and enabled him to deliver on the loyal cheek of the gallant member for North Armagh a blow which could be heard even above the shouts of the combatants. It was like a battle of the Middle Ages or of classic antiquity. There were no great general movements. Individual heroes met in single conflict and wrested laurels from each other. Thus Mr. Timothy Healy met Mr. Gibson Bowles in the gangway and had a brisk but brief sand indecisive struggle with him. Nobody can tell who would have won if the contest had been prolonged. Mr. Healy is fiery, but Mr. Fiwles is solid. It was as well that a rush of other warriors separated them. One honourable gentleman, felled by an unrecognised hand, disappeared completely. Whether he was trampled on or not it is impossible to say. Mr. Condon, being a tall man of his inches, seemed to be trying to reach Colonel Saunderson through the press, but did not succeed. The whole Opposition sid3 was, in short, a dense mass of battling humanity, in which many more blows than those specified above were given and received, but by whom it is impossible to say. The fight was so hot that the ridge crowning the back of one of the benches—a piece of timber five or six yards long and serving to form a receptacle something like that for prayer-books at Church—was wrenched clean away. On the opposite side of the House Mr. T. W. Russell burned with such ardour to help his friends that he had to be forcibly held back by other Dissentient Liberals. In the centre of the floor Dr. Tanner displayed equal eagerness " to be at 'em," and was equally restrained by an admiring but anxious circle. The disgrkceful nature of the scene made such an impression upon the two or three hundred spectators in the Strangers' Gallery that they loudly and persistently hissed and cried " Shame."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CJLONEL SANDERSON'S STORY.</head>
        <p>CJLONEL SANDERSON'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Colonel Saunderson described the scene to a " Globe " representative yesterday. After referring to the earlier incidents of the disturbance, he says :—" The next thing I felt rather than saw was an Irish member who came and flopped down on top of me, and knocked my hat off. I have since learned that it was owing to his slipping, and it was with no intention of assault- ing me. I naturally resented this violent intrusion into my seat, and seized this lion, member by the collar—he was on the top of me at the time—and when I stood up, still holding him by Ais collar, Mr. Crean, who I did not know was in my neighbourhood at all, hit out behind me. I turned round and struck at him over the shoulder of a Conservative member, and hit him. You can see the blow he gave me on the tem- ple." Colonel Saunderson pointed out to the left side of his head, which was considerably cut and bruii e " Mr. Crean's hit at me," continued the colonel, "was the first blow struck, and my impression is that had it rut been for the blow being struck nothingiof the kind would have taken place. Of course I :returned the blow, and then the fight became general."</p>
        <p>" Then it was in the- general fight that you received your other bruises ! Who caused them ?" " I can't say. There were three pumm citing at me at the same time. Undoubtedly I struck out then. I do not deny it, nor am I the least ashamed of it, and that is the whole row. My impression is, undoubtedly," added the Colonel, laughing, "that when the Irishmen below the g,stigway saw the scrimmage—for it amounted to a scrimmage—on the front Opposition bench, the natural combatant instinct of Irishmen, which is inher- ent to the race, overcame all restraints, and they were hopelessly unable to resist the temptation of joining in the scrimmage, and they came in a sort of wave and threw themselves across the gangway."</p>
        <p>• PENKETH.</p>
        <p>THE flauvEsv.—The farmers in this district are now busily engaged in securing their crop of corn. The crop this year is rather above the average, and satisfacrory results are anticipated if Vic weather continues favour- able.</p>
        <p>THE LATE Hoy. LEOPOLD Fowys.—On Sunday even- ing at the close of the service at St. Mary's Church, Sankey, Miss Standish, the organist, rendered the Dead March in' Saul " to commemorate the death of tilt Hon. Leopold Powys, who was a patron of the church. The piece was finely rendered and was ob- served with the customary marks of reverence by the congregation.</p>
        <p>CHORAL SOCIETY TRIP.—The members of the St. Mary's (Sankey) Choral Society held their annual trip on Saturday. The place selected was Southport. About 50 persons proceeded from Sankey station, and after spending a very pleasant day, returned home at 10.30 10. m•</p>
        <p>BIBLI CLASS TRIP.—On Saturday the men who attend the Bible Claas at Bt. Mary's Mission held tfieir first annual trip. The class was formed in February of this year and numbers over 70 members. The wives of the married men were also invited and, needless to say, turned up in force. The destination of the trip was decided by voting, and Llandudno was unanimously selected. The party, numbering about 90 members, entrained at Fidlers Ferry and proceeded direct to Llandudno, and arrived there at 10.45 a.m. The people then dispersed themselves in the town and visited the various places of interest. The party began the ret urn journey•at 7.45 p.m. in special carriages and arrived at 11.45 p.m. The arrangem *Tits were in the hands of the ler_der of the class, the Rev. J. R. Jones, and were carried out without the slightest mishap. A most enjoyable day was spent by all. It is inteaded to make the trip an annual one. •</p>
        <p>Ass Corrine LADIES' SCHOOL.—Miss Annie FaircloJgh Burtonwood. IMS passed College of Preceptors 3rd class also Trinity Conejo of music, junior honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>VI MM.</head>
        <p>VI MM.</p>
        <p>SPHYCH DAY AT VIE - GRAMMER SBNOOL.—Thursday was tbe occasion of the annual speech day at the Gram- mer school. The event has become one of thesed letter days of Lyrom, and most of the elite of society assembled NUMEROUS APPLICATIONS, at the invitation of the master, the Rev. J. Edwardes- Evan, and Mrs. Klwardes-Evans. The day, which A man named Thomas Owens has been convicted at Evans, was at times threatening, was fine, so that toe "at he Central Criminal Court of theft and bigamy: He had pursued a career of crime for many years, but it home " held after the routine business of the reports appeared that after having been in prison and also entertainments in the school was of a highly for burglary and .fraud he had still been able to obtain situations of social and delightful character. The singing and reci- trost. He deserted his wife and children at Conway, tatious of the hovs was a most decided improvement on former years, and afterwards went throtigh the ceremony of marriage with two other women, whom he robbed and deserted ; satisfactory to the goverm.rs and the parents of the and just before his arrest lie had obtained 150 replies to pupils, as well as 1, the ma,ters. At the •tilisequent an advertisement for a wife. He described himself as seven meeting the chair was occupied try the Rev. G. W. "a young man in a cood situation." For the next Guest, chairman of the Board of Governors. The Mas- ter's report which was read was of a satisfactory char- years. according to the sentonee passed by Mr. Com- acter, as was alivrthe report of the examinations Made missioner Kerr, Owens will 'be diligently employed in a by the Rey. James wilson, M.A., 1 e ,c.s. prison. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WARRINGTON SEWERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SEWERS.</p>
        <p>THE DRAINAGE OF PADGATE-LANE AREA. The following report of tho Borough Surveyor has been submitted to the Paving and Sewerage Committee of the Warrington Corporation, and the committee, will, at the meeting of the Town Coun- cil, on Tuesday evening recommend that it be adopted. The Borough Surveyor says :In handing you the following report upon the drainage of Padgate- lane district, which was added to the Borough under the powers obtained in the" Warrington Extension and. Water Act, 1890," I would wish to preface the same by a few particulars of the general sewers of Warrington, which has a very important bearing upon the drainage of the new district. I find Sir Robert Rawlinson in his report on the drain- age of Warrington in the year 1843, which report was adopted by the Council, and the works subsequently carried out, states: " The area cf the town which will have to be drained by the main sewers is, town area 174 acres.; suburban area 171 acres ; total 345 acres. This is exclusive ef Latchford. The natural drainage area is tolerably square and compact." The size of the maid artery sewer running from Bank Quay, along Mersey- et., to Academy-st, was carried out 4 feet by 3 feet,and continued from Academy-st., along Mersey-street and Church-street,to the commencement of Manchester-lane, at a size of three feet six inches by two feet six inches. Since this sewer was laid the area draining into it has been increased from 345 acres to 795 acres. The area has become fully developed and populous, and works have been erected which contribute large volumes of water,which at the time the main artery sewer was laid down would be scarcely conceivable. Allies of streets have also been laid out and paved, and dwelling houses erected, all tending to cause the rainfall to be more rapidly transferred to the sewers. The population of the whole of the Municipal Borough of Warrington in 1850, was 23,000, and now it has become about 54,000. In 1869, a sewer was laid 2 feet 5 inches diameter, with a gradient ot 1 in 1,000, from the intersection of Hall-street and Mersey-street, continuing along Elles- mere-steeet to Hovvley-laoe, and extended by an 18-in. pipe sewer, at a gradient of 1 in 1,230, to the S.E. corner of St. Elphin's Schools. The district (Padgate-lane area) now proposed to be sewered contains an area of 150 acres. At present the district is practically without any system of drainage. The natural surface of the land falls eastward from the Cemetery to Padgate Brook, and the mean altitude is 26 feet above ordnance datum. The nearest existing sewers to which an outlet for the sewage could be gained are as follows :—No. I.—Manchester-lane at the junction of Padgate-lane opposite the Cemetery gates. No. 2. Marsh House-lane, opposite the, Barracks. No. 3. Inlet to sewer at the south-east corner of the Training College. In laying out the lines and gradients of the new sewers I have kept in mind the ultimate covering of the area and population. together with the requirements for proper velocities and the rainfall the Corportion are legally bound to take in the sewers, and find it is physically impossible to get the sewage into any of the beforenamed outlets from the proposed drainage area without resorting to mechanical lifting. Owing to the main artery sewer in Church and Mersey-streets having to contend with the rainfall and sewage from so large an area, I consider it unadvisable to increase its drainage area, and would therefore recommend the following Scheme- -Ist. Commencing near Bruche Bridge with sewer at a depth of 3 feet from surface, or 23.43 inches above Ordnance datum, and continuing westward along Man- chester-road to the lane east side of Cemetery, then in a southerly direction along the said lane to the south eastern corner of the Cemetery, then along the Cemetery wall and Howley Brook in a westerly direction to the junction of brooks, near the end of the sewer which runs from Mersey-st., along Hall-st., Ellesmere-at., and Far- rell-st. The new sewer at its outlet will be 8 feet deep below surface of ground, or 13.30 inches above Ordnance datum. The sewer from Bruche Bridge to the junction of lane east side of Cemetery in Manchester-road will be 12- in. diameter, and from that point to outlet 18-in diameter, all laid at a gradient of 1 in 400. . .</p>
        <p>2nd. A 1-2 -inch sewer with a gradient of 1 in 400, commencing near Padgate Lane Bridge at a depth of 3 feet from surface, or 25-89 inches above Ordnance datum, running along Padgate Lane for a distance of 370 yards, then crossing in a southerly direction to Manchester-road on tho line of a proposed new street, and kining with the sewer No 1, before-named, at the junction of lane east side of Cemetery. 3rd. A 12-inch pipe sewer commencing at the Cheshire Lines Railway in Gorsey Lane, at a depth of 3 feet from surface. or 28.28 inches above Ordnance datum, with a gradient of 1 in 400, cordinuing in a south-easterly direction to the junction of Manchester- road and Padg,ate Lane, near the Cemetery gates, then along Manchester-road in an easterly direction to the janction of No. 1 and 2 sewers at the lane east side uf Cemetery. At the commencement of each of the before-named sewers, I propose to construct a flush tank of 1,000 gallons capacity, to enable the sewer, if required, to be automatically 'flushed. The depth of all the before- named sewers is arranged to take in any further exten- sion of sewers from any portion of the drainage area. At the outfall I propose to put down an ejector butt the sewage into the existing sewer as before-named, work- ing the same from the air main connected with the compressor which work the Latchford sewerage system. This will enable the sewage to be disposed of without the construction of a sewage well and the constant at- tendance of men, which would be the case if a gas or other engine and pumps were erected for the purpose of lifting the sewage. The existing outfall sewer joins the main artery sewer at the junction of Hall-street and Mersey-street. to relieve the main artery sewer as much as possible, I propose to in- tercept the outfall sewer in Napier.street, and join it with the sewer lately executed for the drainage of How- ley Brook, having its outlet int-) the river, close to the Castle Rubber Company's works. - .</p>
        <p>- , I have carefully gone into the cost of the works, and consider that a sum of two thousand one hundred pounds will he required to complete the same, which sum does not include the purchase of any land or any payment for trespass rent.</p>
        <p>ASSIZE ACTION BY RUNCORN QUARRY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>P ROPRIETO RS.</head>
        <p>P ROPRIETO RS.</p>
        <p>- --- - At Chester Assizes on Wednesday Brundrit and Co., quarry proprietors, of Runcorn, Liverpool, and Penmaenmawr, brought an action against Sterling and Swan, contractors, of Liverpool, and formerly of Manchester, to recover .1:312 8. 2d. for paving setts said and delivered ; while Sterling and Swan brought forward a counter-claim fer about £1,150 damages for breach of contract for non-delivery of etts within the time specified, and for setts not being of the size ordered, and for keeping men and horses, &amp;c., while waiting for setts. Mr. Abel Thomas, M.P., and Mr. E. H. Lloyd, instructed by Mr. F. A. Lake, were for the plaintiffs, and Mr. F. Marshall, Q.C., and Mr. J. Eldon Bankes for defendants.—ln the beginning of 1891 the borough of Birkenhead decided to pave with granite stone Orange-road and Argyle-street. They advertised for tenders, and it resulted in the fact that the defend- ants tendered for the work, and obtained the contract. There were two contracts ; the first was entered into in March, 1891, and the second in July, 1891. Before send- ing in their render the defendants saw Mr. Littler, and came to terms as to the price Messrs. Brundrit and Co. would charge them for grey granite setts. On the day following the interview Mr. Littler put the prices in writing, and sent them to Sterling and Swan, the price agreed to being—for setts 3i lay 6 by 3±, 21s. 6d. per ton, less 6d. per ton, or 21s. per ton. On the 4th April the defendants wrote accepting this price, stating that the quantity r‘quired would be about 3,000 tons, and adding that they should require the first delivery in one month. By the 18th November the plain- tiffs had delivered 3,100 tons. The claim of the plain- tiffs now was for the balance of the account still due to !them for these setts, namely, £312 odd. On the other hand, the defendants' case was that the contract from the Birkenhead Corporation was bound to be completed in four mvinths, and that the plaintiffs Undertook to de- liver the setts within that period. The setts were much delayed from time to time, with the result that the con- tract, instead of being Completed in October, was not finished until early the following year, causing the de- fendants considerable loss. Moreover, another element which was stated to have caused defendants inconveni- ence and loss was the fact that the setts were not in all cases of the exact size bargained for.—For the plaintiffs it was shown that there was no reference in the corres- pondence to the alleged undertaking of Mr. Littler that the setts should be delivered within four months, and it was held that there was no unreasonable delay. More- over, it was elicited that defendants had not to pay one penny in the shape of penalty to the Corporatisn.—The jury, before the case had proceeded far, showed a strong leaning to the plaintiffs' viciw, abut eventually said they had heard enough of the case, finding for the plaintiffs on the claim and counter-claim.—His Lordship took the satire view, said the case was an undefended case, and gave judgment tor plaintiffs on the claim and counter- claim. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>CLERGY AND THE LIQUOR</head>
        <p>THE CLERGY AND THE LIQUOR</p>
        <p>TRAD E.</p>
        <p>In the Grmernment Rebarn relating to tied houses there is given the number of on-licenses by the Church of England through the Ecclesiastical Commissioners and by individual clergymen. No fewer than 88 of these licenses Are held by .the Commissioners, 22 of the licenses being centred in Finsbury, London. The " Con- stitutional Year Book" explains that the Ecclesiastical and Church Commission is administrated by a body Consisting f Archbishops, Bishops, three Deans and 20 , eminent Laymen ; and that the Commissioners apply the proceeds of the aitates in promoting the work and efficiency of the Church, and increasing its endowments, £lO,OOO per annum revenue from licensed housea in Lon- don alone, was stated by the Right Hon. W. H. Smith, M.P., in the House of Commons, as being enjoyed by Ecclesiastical Commissioners in England and Wales. It is perhaps even harder to realise the fact that 34 clergy- Men hold 100 on-licenses between them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>SALE OF WORK AT TETE RECTORY. A sale of work was on Thursday commenced in the Rectory grounds in aid (A the Warrington Parish Church funds. The ladies' committee hav- ing charge of the arrangements were Mrs. Willis, Miss Fairclough, Mrs. Alfred Woods, Mrs. W. Gill, Mrs. N. B. Percival, Mrs. Walton, Mrs. Dewhurst, Mrs. Partington, Miss Litton, Mrs. Arthur Browne, Mrs. Price, and Miss White. Assisting these ladies at the stalls were Mrs, W. Woods, the Misses Buck- ton, the Misses Appleton, the Misses Percival, the Misses Moores, Miss Boyle, Mrs. Wilcocks, the MiSses White, Miss Bartley, and Miss Fairclough. The sporting committee consisted of the Rev. T. W. Cook (chairman),ltev. A. A. Markham, Messrs. H. Litton, B. Fairclough, J. Ridley, VV. Hannah, and S. Wright.</p>
        <p>The Opening, ceremony on Thursday was performed by Lady Qreenall, and among thoSe present were the Rector (Rev. F. W. Willis) and Mrs. Willis, Rev. T. Gib* son Smith, Rev. E. C. E. and Mrs. Carleton, Rev. C. Z. Jackson, Rev. H. V. and Mrs Pigot, Rev. T. W. Cooke, Rev. J. J. and Mrs. Langliam, Revs. A. A Markham, P. Percival, and F. C. Timinins, the Misses Greenall, Mrs. Beamont, Captain J. D. Buckton, and Messrs. R. Davies, John White, A. Wo6ds, Craik, Walton, Win. Gill, and Taylor. The RECTOR in introducing Lady Greenall wished to say a few words as to the object of the sale of work. They had a debt of £350 for necessary expenses of the church. When the present people's warden took office, there was a debt of £lOO. During the last three years the debt had grown to over £3OO. This had not been money thrown away or foolishly expended. There had been some very necessary work to he done. He might just mention that they had rebuilt the churchyard wall, widened the paths, planted shrubs, and sown grass, and coming to the church itself they had remewed the large doors, and they had improved the church so that it was quite pleasant to a large congregation. They had thoroughly improved the organ, the choir, and stall, and put in a very valuable and necessary safe. All these things had been most necessary. They were long stand- ing arrears, and they did not want to delay it any longer so that the church should be as it should be. They bad to thank their past and present church- wardens and all their officers very much for the interest they had taken and for the work they had done. He had only now to introduce to them if lie had any right to introduce Lady Greenall, who was, with Sir Gilbert Greenall, such a great benefactor to the Church in Warrington. They had kindly helped the church for many years past, and he dared say hi' z audience had seen in the papers what Sir Gilbert had been doing at St. James' Church. He had very great pleasure in calling upon Lady Greenall.</p>
        <p>Lady GREENALL, who was received with applause said it gave her great pleasure in declaring the bazaar open. She hoped they would got over £3OO so that they might have a sum in reserve for other matters. She quite agreed in not allowing the church to be out of order. They always liked their own houses in order, and there- fore they ought to have the house dedicated to God the same. She now declared the sale of work open. Mr. ALFRED Woos (people's warden) proposed a vote of thanks to Lady Greenall. He was sorry that they had not the presence of Sir Gilbert. He hoped that there would be a good sale as there was £BOO of material on the grounds.</p>
        <p>„ The vote was put and carried with loud cheers after which the purchasing commenced. The sale of work is held in a tent behind the rectory building. In the centre is a flower stall, on which is a large quantity of various kinds of flowers. The rest of the articles are placed round the sides of the tent, and there is a provision department, a department for drapery, and fancy goods and various other kinds of articles. A little distance away on the church side of the rector's house, is a stall for refreshments, in front of which is a table on which are placed the various articles which are to be drawn for. On the opposite side of the ground is a lawn tennis ground on which tennis matches were held, the finals of which are to be playPd off to-day, the judge being the Rev. C. E. Jackson. Tugs-of-war were also held on this ground the final also coming off to-day. Putting the shot was held on Thursday night, the winner being Mr. B.Fairclough. Councillor Arthur Browne acted as referee in the tugs-of-war and putting the shot.</p>
        <p>During the day selections of music were played by the Blue Coat school band, and in the evening St George's Mission handbell ringers performed several selections.</p>
        <p>The sale will be continued to-day (Saturday) when Mrs T H. Lyon will open the proceedings at 2.30 p.m. At the close Mrs. Willis will present a solid silver cup and champion tennis racket to the winncr of the tennis tournament, and gold and silvermerlals to the winners of the tugs-of-war and the putting of the shot. The gardens will be illuminated with over 400 fairy lamps at dusk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>LATEST NE WS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NE WS.</p>
        <p>A JUDGE DISAGREES WITH THE JURY.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes, yesterday, before Mr. Jus- tice Lawrance, Jabez Wright, a clegger, of Farnworth, was charged with wounding his wife with intent to do grievous bodily harm. The prisoner fractured his wife's skull with a heavy rolling pin, tie was found guilty of. unlawfully wounding, but his Lordship declined to give effect to that view of the case, remarking that it seemed to be the fashion in parts of Lancashire for man to at- tack their wives. A sentence of four years' penal servi- tude was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>COAL CRISIS. THOUSANDS OF MINERS IDLE. COMMENCEMENT OF THE sniniE AT S HELENS.</head>
        <p>THE COAL CRISIS. THOUSANDS OF MINERS IDLE. COMMENCEMENT OF THE sniniE AT S HELENS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon, 3,500 men employed in Messrs. Pilkington'slq. Helens and Ravenhead pits, Phcenix, Whitecross, Ashton's Green, Coffin's Green, Bold, Sut- ton Heath, and i..ea Green collieries, St. Helens, ceased work and brought up their tools against a reduction of 25 per cent. Mr. Thomas Glover stated yesterday morning that there appeared no hope of a settlement at present, and was of opinion that the struggle would be a long one and would last at least two months. At Messrs. Richard Evans arid Co.'s pits at St. Helens, Havdock, Ashton, Golborne, and Edge-green, about 4,000 men brought up their tools and were paid off. To-day additional notices to expire on the 4th August were posted at Messrs. .Nottall and Co.'s glass bottle works, St. Helens, where about 600 hands are onployed. The firm have, however, about three weeks' stock of fuel, and are hoping, to tide over the difficulty without stopping any of the works. The notices to the 1,500 hands at Messrs. Cannim,,,ton, Shaw, and Co.'s glassworks terminated to-day, and the works will now be liable to stop any day, but this is not expected to take place at present. The 3,000 employees , in St. Helens Nf the United Alkali Company, Limited, have also worked out their notices,and should the supply of salt and fuel run short the works will be closed. Altogether the present is a most critical time for St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>YORKSHIRE.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE.</p>
        <p>The pits in Leeds, Normanton, Castleford, and Altoft districts are now all idle, so that about 20,000 men will be unemployed. At most of the pits the ponies are being brought out, but in a great many places the men are leaving their tools. A number of large orders will be left uncompleted, as the demand during the week has been great. The prices are likely further to advance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>FLINTSHIRE COLLIERS AND VIE STRIKE.</head>
        <p>FLINTSHIRE COLLIERS AND VIE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening, a meeting of the men employed at the Flint coal and cannel pits was held to consider whether they should go out on strike. After a lengthy discussion it was carried by an overwhelming majority thst they should continue working. In fact, only thir- teen men voted for striking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>HOUSE OF COMMONS.-FRIDAY. TIE DISORDERLY SCENES ON THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. HuNTka : I beg to ask the Prime Minister whether Her Majesty's Government will consider the advisability —I might almost say the necessity—of appointing a committee of inquiry into the deplorable occurrences of last night ? Mr. CrLaDsroNE : This question has been put without my having had any opportunity of previous considera- tion. It is one of very great difficulty, and the only admission I will make to my hon. friend is this, that these occurrences in themselves constitute a very serious fact in the history of this House. (Cheers.) Beyond that I don't think I ought to proceed at the moment, but if on Monday or Tuesday the hon. member will repeat his question be shall have the best answer I can give.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>BLOCKADE OF ELUL</head>
        <p>THE BLOCKADE OF ELUL</p>
        <p>Sir E. GREY, answering Sir Charles rine, said the blockade of Siam by the French Fleet had not yet been formally nostified, and it was perhaps not too much to hope that it might be averted. Yesterday the Govern- ment had received a. telegram from Paris from the British Ambassador, intimating that M. Develle had said the blockade would begin on the 31st. Later they received a second telegram from her Majesty's , Minister at Bangkok that the blockade would begin on the 28th. A third telegram, received almost simul- taneously from Singapore, intimated that the blockade had been notified on the 27th, and would begin on the 28th, and would extend across the Gulf of Siam. Three days were to be allowed to neutral vessels to load and leave Bangkok. In the meantime Her Majesty's Government had telegraphed to Paris for further in- formation. The question as to the effect of the block- ade of Siamese ports on British trade wee being dis- cussed by the Foreign Office M conjuncticAl with the law officers of the Crown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-29_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>BRUTAL PEASANT.</head>
        <p>A BRUTAL PEASANT.</p>
