ts have already taken plrice. seek justify his action on the ground of the 111 space available for new graves in the churchl I am not prepared to say that the rector ig acting within his legal rights. I can only OP' my own strong opinion that such a discrimination ag, seems to exercise is in entire variance with the intern and spirit of the Burials Act, 1880. The answer to ' third paragraph is in the affirmative. As I stated previous occasion, I think that the vicar in this in, violated the law, nor can I look upon the brat' which he made to me in the fir,t instance as f'lc complete account of the facts. As to the last (peg,", where the law is not broken, it it obvious that o° can interfere with, however much lie may proceedings of of the clergy. Where the law is brlkt is open to anyone to prosecute.</p>
        <p>COLONEL SA.UNDERSON'S AFTER - DINNER ORATORY. The annual banquet of the National Union of vative and Constjtutjo,il ASSOCiiitiMIS was held Criterion,London, on Wednesday eveniug, wfwa Londonderry and Colonel Saunderson, MP.,v1811:11, guests of theevening. Lord Londonderry denounce ; Home Rule policy of the Govern men t, pa area rls,,s:r affected the civil servants of Ireland, au,l also spo'' deprecatory terms of the character of the frishalrn whom the government of thst country wa4likelyt". entrusted in the event of Mr. Gladstone's policy be. successful. Colonel Saunderson, irk discussimi il bahle attitude of the Radioal members on the Home Bill coming ox for the third reading,, said he served amongst these gentlemen a indiriresr. tenderel be squared, which was peculiar to their race. charged Mr. Gladstone with hein:4 a traitor, and that he was ready to support that charge on the House. When the House of Lords ci ine to burY ' Home Rule Bill, as hury it they would, wirhout resurrection, lie proposed that ths following PP' t should be inscribed on its tombstone Here 10, misbegotten offspring of a treacherous and critn' union between the Prime Minister of England and enemies of his country."</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Court of aancery was a sce,ne much amusement, on Tuesday, when William ' Parker; the inventor of a simple but popular to)' slating of two coloured wooden ba:ls attached t 0 other by a piece of elastic and known as •'rBr Twins," sought to restrain R. A. Lowe and Co, chester, from selling the toy, and from issuing a: tim copy of a circular descriptive of it. A pair 0, ;. twins were exhibited, and made to dance fort struction, and it may be added, amusement 0' court. Tke description for the sale of the articls —" Parker's Twins." They- were born in America:, are now being educated in Europe. They eat but live on wind. (Laughter.) If one of the want to dance the other has to even if it don't Our so off they go on a lively waltz. All married c°,, with children can be supplied with these twins a'r, cost of one penny. (More laughter.) They nevepo —require no nurse. They make friends with every No nursing bottles ;no safety pins. After inakt9g a visit the home seems lonesome without them. ter.) The hearing of the application was adjout</p>
        <p>When M. Zola wrote his repulsive story on peasant life, it was said that such things as he 6 could not be. But a case which has just been the Assizes in the Department of the Tarn shoO, in '• La Terre "we have unfortunately only too .`ll picture. An old man named Veyrios had ceded Insd to his son, stipulating that he should have fOO lodging. He was immediately placed in the worst:o of the house and starved by his brutal son. °clef; the younger Veyrios asked his father to come stable and help him to move a large empty tutio the old man stooped down, his son pushed him in tub and turned it over. Then he put heavy ston it, and locked the stable door. After three da, again went to look, and finding his unhappy fatlifi alive, be strangled him. The son has been genre to death, his only defence being that he was too e' sive.</p>
