        <p>TO ALL WHOM it may CONCERN.—Whereas an INJUNCTION was granted on the 21st day of June, 1893,in the High Court of Justice (Chancery Division) by Mr. Justice Romer. in the action of THE SINGER MANUFAC- TURING COMPANY v. SPENCE in the following terms, " To restrain the defendant, his agents, servants, and work- people, from further advertising or issuing to the public posters, the cards, the catalogue, the advertisements, and the green card, and generally using the name . Singer' in connection with sewing machines not of the plaintiff's manu- facture, in any way calculated to induce the belief that such machines are the manufacture of the plaintiffs." Notice is hereby given that ALL PERSONS henceforth WRONG- FULLY using the NAME •• SINGER" in any way contrary to the said judgment will be HELD LIABLE for so doing, together with those who is ve previously so offended. (Signed) GILBERT DYKE WANSBROUGH, 39, Foste.-lane, London, E.G., Solicitor for The Singer Manufacturing Co.</p>
